C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\xscaleinten\panel_20260912_104522_xpadjud\ollama_kimi.txt:23:- **D1 REFINE — adjudicated against the packet's own definitions.** REASONED throughout. Both popular criteria are wrong: grok's "body never tests rd" cites the clause-(e) rule for value-case tests, but clause (d) keys swaps on body-**read** fields, not body-**tested** ones; codex's "bounded" and your lean ("floor because one cheap row") grade by cost, which you rightly distrust. The criterion already sits in the stated rule: (d) admits operand swaps drawn from body-read fields **into predicates that dominate the edit**. `crm==0` and `crn==0` dominate the edited line; rd is body-read ⇒ `rd==0` is a literal (d)-swap ⇒ **floor, by construction, not by cheapness**. Corollary nobody drew: a fresh-constant predicate (`rd==8` if 8 is no dominating constant; `inten==S1^1`) has no swap source ⇒ the `idata==k` shape ⇒ disclosure, by the packet's stated rule. So your "either a row per undriven rd, or unbounded ⇒ disclosure" is a **false fork**: the enumeration is (constants in dominating predicates) × (body-read fields) — bounded — and for rd it is {0, plus whatever opcode/op1 constants dominate}; c=0 was the one uncovered cell and is now closed. (If swaps may also draw on the sibling crn-4 dispatch, `rd==4` is floor too — and it is *already covered*, since r4 ∈ the landed read set; either way no new row is needed.) **"Any inten bit":** the packet shows no inten/isteer bit-test anywhere in the function ⇒ no swap source ⇒ **disclosure**, opportunistically closed for the set-form by RD0_ALLONES. Definitive ruling CANNOT SETTLE from packet — settling fact: the predicate list (and constants) of `arm_coproc_i80321_6`. rd==15 is not a separate class; it is the same analysis, incidentally pinned by an existing row.
