C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\xscaleinten\panel_20260912_095235_xscalepass2\kimi.txt:38:  Also candidate: exit only when `isteer != 0`. INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER writes isteer<-0 first, so isteer == 0 there. Do any rows set isteer nonzero and then read inten? SIB_WRITE does crn-4 non-zero write → dies (expected, that's a retained kill). SIB_READ: crn-4 read survives — that's a read of isteer, not setting it. So after SIB_WRITE the session is dead. If no session sets isteer != 0 and then reads inten, a mutant `if (i80321_isteer != 0) {fatal;exit}` in the inten read arm survives. But wait — would that be clause (d)? It re-introduces exit under a different predicate. Yes. Is isteer != 0 a "floor cell"? The body doesn't read isteer at all — so it's clause-(e) disclosure? Hmm, the distinction the author asks: "Is each a floor cell (the body tests or reads the lvalue) or clause-(e) disclosure (the body never distinguishes it)?" — I think "floor cell" means a predicate on state the body actually reads (so a test could in principle pin it), vs clause-(e) where the body never distinguishes it.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\xscaleinten\panel_20260912_095235_xscalepass2\kimi.txt:72:  And `isteer != 0`: INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER sets isteer to 0 and never nonzero before an inten read. SIB_WRITE sets isteer nonzero but that's a crn-4 write that dies by design (session ends). Does any session write isteer != 0 and then successfully read inten? If not, `if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer != 0) {fatal;exit}` in the inten read arm survives. Need to check whether any row sets isteer nonzero then reads inten in the same session. SIB_WRITE: crn-4 non-zero write still dies — that's the retained kill (isteer write with nonzero → fatal+exit by the original code). So after SIB_WRITE the emulator is dead; no subsequent inten read. In D arm, isteer never written. In ANCHOR, isteer never written (I think). So `isteer != 0` predicate is constant 0 in every live session → survivor. Unless... hmm, wait: does the interrupt controller or anything else set isteer? On reset it's 0. The machine might not touch it. So yes, survivor — but is it clause (d)? It reintroduces the exit behavior under a predicate on state. Yes. It's a "disclosure" class mutant (body never reads isteer) — the author asks to classify each as floor cell vs clause-(e) disclosure.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\xscaleinten\panel_20260912_095235_xscalepass2\kimi.txt:105:     j. Predicate on crm/crn — constant within the arm (crm==0, crn==0 dominates), so any predicate referencing crm/crn is constant true or false there; false ones are pure dead-code mutants (e.g., `if (crn == 4) exit` inside the crn==0 arm) — that's an "equivalent mutant" trivially, not interesting; clause (d) predicates drawn from dominating set... The brief says enumerate DEPENDS-ON: (1) predicates dominating the edited lines up to function boundary; (2) operand swaps from fields the body reads. A dead predicate like crn==4 inside crn==0 arm is an equivalent mutant (unreachable) — arguably not a real survivor since it can never trigger for any input. The interesting survivors are predicates satisfiable by real inputs but not by the suite.
