c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — FILE-READING, and it was the seat that re-derived rather than ratified from its first line (&#x27;I&#x27;ll treat this as a design review against the GXEMUL-SEC tree, not the 2021 src/ copy&#x27;). *** ITS HEADLINE IS AN ORACLE-CORRECTION-RULE OBLIGATION THE BRIEF DID NOT RAISE: *** the witness today wants `sig11` on `cheap trig` / `staged trig`; after the fix those oracles must be RE-PINNED (`exit0` + `0xffff`) **FIRST, and the committed build measured RED under the new expectations BEFORE the code changes** -- otherwise the re-pin is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary. It also pins WHY BOTH TRIGGER ROWS MUST SURVIVE, each covering a different free site: dropping `staged trig` lets a `:446`-only mutant escape (disk-fail zero, no tail); dropping `cheap trig` lets a `:326`-only mutant escape (mount is saved, disk-fail after alloc_data is not). And it states an HONEST LIMIT rather than overclaiming: no guest register exposes data_len, so PIO death plus OVERREAD cannot distinguish `data_len = 0` from an offset bump. On the invariant it agrees with codex INDEPENDENTLY -- &#x27;right in the NULL state, wrong as a universal pairing&#x27;, because the 65536 hack depends on cnt and data_len differing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/grok.txt (14,559 chars). Sampled at 813 bytes mid-run and NOT recorded dead -- the sample-twice rule; it finished at 14.5 KB.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** THIS SEAT WAS REPORTED **SEAT DOWN** AND WAS NOT DOWN. *** The envelope carried `&quot;response&quot;: &quot;&quot;` with `done_reason: &quot;stop&quot;` and `eval_count: 4556` -- and 18,002 characters of `thinking` holding a COMPLETE answer behind the model&#x27;s own `&lt;|open|&gt;response&lt;|sep|&gt;` marker, opening &#x27;# VERDICT: DESIGN APPROVED for the one-line fix&#x27; and closing cleanly. The existing rule fired on the empty `response` alone. **seat_verdicts.py was taught the discriminator the same hour** -- `length` means the model ran out mid-thought and no answer exists (the deepseek case the rule was built from), while `stop` plus a channel-marked block means the answer EXISTS and calling the seat down is the unread-review class with the answer already on disk. Negative-controlled three ways; that panel&#x27;s tally went from &#x27;6 answered, 1 DOWN&#x27; to &#x27;7 answered, 0 DOWN&#x27;. ITS CONTENT, now readable: DESIGN APPROVED, with `cur_data_offset` persistence raised as its main finding -- the same hypothesis deepseek raised, and REFUTED the same way by :327. It asked for exactly the right measurement and proposed filing `gdrompiostaleoffset` if the answer went the other way. **It could not check, and said so.** *The recovered answer turning out to be refutable is luck; it is not a reason to keep losing them.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/ollama_kimi.txt -&gt; ollama_kimi.RECOVERED.md. SUBSTITUTE TRANSPORT kimi-k3:cloud, recorded by transport and never as the CLI seat.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — PACKET-FED. Ratifies the one-line fix with one premise correction, and the correction is the useful part: it names `alloc_data` at :307 as a THIRD point where the pair is transiently inconsistent -- `d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt;` executes BEFORE `CHECK_ALLOCATION(d-&gt;data = malloc(...))`, so between :307 and :309 a stale-NULL `data` coexists with a fresh `data_len`. CHECKED: the window is straight-line with no reader, and CHECK_ALLOCATION exits on failure, so nothing can observe it -- but the observation is correct and neither fable5 nor I had enumerated that site as a writer of the PAIR. It also asked for the one measurement that would close its own residual doubt (that nothing between :446 and :475 reads data_len) and -- notably -- **questioned the AUTHOR&#x27;S APPARATUS**, the awk scan behind the &#x27;no return&#x27; claim. That scan was independently found defective the same day for a different reason (it never searched for `break`), so the suspicion was sound. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/ollama_glm.txt (15,161 chars). PACKET-FED and it says so in its verdict line: &#x27;ANSWERED FROM THE BRIEF ALONE - NO FILES READ.&#x27;</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — PACKET-FED. Sound on the fix (&#x27;the one-line fix is sound for the NULL deref&#x27;), and honest about its own limits (&#x27;I did not read the file, so I cannot rule out a third NULL assignment outside the inlined region&#x27;). Its headline -- that the design is incomplete because `cur_data_offset` is not reset -- is REFUTED BY READING: :327 zeroes it on every handle_command entry, upstream of BOTH alloc_data call sites (:342, :418). It could not have known: the brief never inlined :327. Same hypothesis as the kimi substitute, reached independently, and the correct disposition is the packet-fed class doing its job -- raising what it cannot check -- not a seat error. Its genuinely novel point survives: **the smallest breaking edit is NOT a mutation of the new line, because the new line adds no predicate.** That is exactly the clause-(d)-enumerates-the-empty-set problem, reached from the packet side. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/ollama_deepseek.txt (7,117 chars). PACKET-FED, self-declared.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — PACKET-FED, and **the only seat to CONTRADICT the round&#x27;s central refutation** -- it claims `gdrompiooddlen` is REACHABLE, not latent. *** SETTLED BY READING, AGAINST IT, AND THE SPLIT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN VOTED. *** Its argument needs `cur_data_offset` to be &#x27;incremented by 1 (BYTE) or 2 (WORD)&#x27;. It is not: :612 and :613 are TWO UNCONDITIONAL `++` in the SAME block, so every pass advances by exactly 2 regardless of guest access width. There is no byte path. The file&#x27;s own comment at :554 already records this -- &#x27;no index consults len, cur_data_offset advances by two&#x27; -- and codex, reading the file, states it independently. **THIS ONE IS A SEAT REASONING ERROR, NOT A BRIEF DEFECT, and the distinction was checked rather than assumed:** the brief DID inline both `++` unconditionally at its Q6 block, so the seat had the code that contradicts its premise. *An earlier draft of this entry said both of its corrections rested on un-inlined code; that was wrong for this one and is corrected here.* **Its SECOND correction IS a brief defect**: it argues `cnt == 0` reaches alloc_data when `sector_count == 0`, which :402-403 rejects outright (`sector_count &lt;= 0` -&gt; break at :406) -- and the brief never inlined that guard. AND ITS FRAMING POINT IS RIGHT AND MATCHES codex/grok INDEPENDENTLY: the invariant is ONE-DIRECTIONAL. The fix gives `data == NULL =&gt; data_len == 0`; it does not give the converse, and the brief&#x27;s wording implied bidirectionality. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260826_085454/ollama_minimax.txt (12,472 chars). PACKET-FED, self-declared: &#x27;no file read in this turn (no tool surface available in this conversation)&#x27;.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** FOUND WHAT NEITHER I NOR CODEX SAW: the :406/:415 rows I filed as &#x27;owed&#x27; WERE ALREADY BUILT AND MEASURED *** in this round&#x27;s own committed kill table, as the `E mism|rej` family scored against all eight builds. E:mism cnt post kills BOTH return-mutants -- a STRUCTURAL tail-reach oracle. Also supplied the licence delta my record had cited wrongly (E:mism how: sig11 on the tail form, exit0 on the helper). Its ONE recommendation I had to correct: it advised porting the rows because &#x27;the construction cost is already paid&#x27; -- the INSTRUMENT was gone, so it is reconstruction from spec. That correction became `preserveinstrument`. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** THE COMPILE-AND-MEASURE SEAT, and it BUILT the mutants rather than reasoning about them. *** Md_nolen (helper kept, only `data_len = 0` deleted) 10/13 + 7/9 KILLED -- the genuine clause-(d) AND clause-(b) mutant, both floor clauses discharged by one build. Mret429 (shipped + break-&gt;return) 13/13 + 8/9 HARMLESS. Mtailret (REJECTED tail design + the same return) 13/13 + 6/9 SIGSEGV. **Same one word, opposite outcome** -- the addition licence&#x27;s measured delta against the shipped tree. Established that m_ret415 takes THREE edits against shipped, and that free_data() runs at the ENTRY to handle_command so bypassing the tail cannot reach it. Its close-out then caught my citation correction going stale INSIDE the commit that wrote it -- fourth-generation drift. Instruments preserved at regress/artifacts/gdrompionull/opus_p2_20260826/ (26 artifacts). [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — SPLIT: SHIP | REQUEST CHANGES, 1,888,363b. Found FOUR L4 wrong records, ALL in my prose and NONE in the C fix, which it confirmed sound at both sites: the probe header described the defect in the PRESENT TENSE post-fix and called itself a WITNESS; the provenance comment said &#x27;quoted&#x27; then PARAPHRASED with no verbatim text or line ref; the staged-route comment claimed &#x27;NO diagnostic&#x27; directly above a row expecting one; and the killtable README said the full-26 scores were absent when every det log carries FULL=n/26. SUPPLIED A WRITER MY PREMISE MISSED: device construction zeroes the whole struct (safe, it sets both fields). Also ruled m_ret415 NOT clause (d) and flagged &#x27;L1 coverage debt&#x27; as a category the taxonomy does not contain. Endorsed keeping the LF conversion. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — GO, 9,169b. Verified premises against source. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — GO-WITH-CHANGES, 10,471b. Independently reached the SAME clause-(d) conclusion as codex, citing m_swapcnt and the same scores from the logs rather than the brief. FOUND A FORWARD-LOOKING HAZARD I verified and filed as `gdromallocpair`: alloc_data() does not release before it allocates, so #465&#x27;s pairing is CONVENTIONAL rather than STRUCTURAL and a future third call site leaks silently. Also flagged the same probe-docstring staleness codex found, and assessed d-&gt;cnt&#x27;s guest-writability as a related pair, not this defect. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — ANSWERED VIA THE `kimi-k3:cloud` SUBSTITUTE TRANSPORT, 7,295b. *** The kimi CLI was 403-walled (370b error file, NOT a seat answer) and this seat is recorded COVERED, not lost -- the substitute answered in the same panel, which is exactly what panel.sh&#x27;s fourth Ollama seat exists for. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — 6,807b, PACKET-FED. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — 2,782b, PACKET-FED. APPROVE THE SHIPPED PRODUCT. [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — 7,756b, PACKET-FED. APPROVE THE FIX. *Note it ANSWERED on this brief after stalling on #464&#x27;s -- the difference being that this brief inlined the full diff and the enclosing function instead of telling it to read files.* [panel_20260826_190055]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** BOTH ROWS PASS THE REGRESS PHASE.  Records-only review -- read, grep and diff; nothing built, nothing run, deliberately, because a measuring seat was live on this machine and a pty-driven instrument corrupts silently under load. ***  **gdrompionull**: 13 rows = 5 controls + 8 detector rows, and **no row expects `exit1`** -- the load-bearing fact under the round-2 de-fatalisations.  Gate-wired, denominator pinned to a literal, per-row presence checks all padded-column safe.  **Every score resolves to a log**, and the ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER IS PRESERVED ON DISK rather than asserted: `prefix_HEAD_13of13.raw` (old pin), `repin_HEAD_RED.raw` (12/13, the committed build measured RED under the new expectations), then `postfix_465*.raw`.  The `Md_nolen` kill is exactly as claimed (10/13 and 7/9 against 13/13 and 9/9 bases).  **gdromexitsix**: it CHECKED the exit census rather than accepting it and reproduced it independently -- 18 occurrences, 3 live, `:792` a false positive, `:1055` unreachable, `:1094`/`:1154` belonging to `gdromcondlies`.  All six filed sites disposed; closure strands no live site.  Its `gdrom_decline_probe.py` gate section and `r2measure_20260829` logs match the witness field byte-for-byte, with `R2MEASURE_DONE` as tail-1. — evidence: batched fable-only regress review of both rows in one sitting, records-only</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lethalonly</span><span class="ttl">lethalonly  a row whose ONLY kill is a LETHAL mutant is not proven to observe its own claim -- under host death every row reads `died`, so the red says nothing about that row&#x27;s subject</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">lethalonly  a row whose ONLY kill is a LETHAL mutant is not proven to observe its own claim -- under host death every row reads `died`, so the red says nothing about that row&#x27;s subject — commits: 7c9dd9e 15c4b0a — CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, not by argument. `Mf` -- the ARMING arm&#x27;s `fatal()` deleted with the latch and `idata = 0` kept -- scored 53/58 and reddened `arm says` at 0 against a wanted 1, with `both decl`, `arm once`, `arm count2` and `arm enable2`, and no sixth row. So the row observes a SILENCED DECLINE WITH THE HOST ALIVE and is not vacuous. *** THE GENERAL CLASS OUTLIVES THIS ROW AND IS WHY IT CLOSES RATHER THAN PERSISTING: *** `a row whose only kill is a LETHAL mutant is not proven to observe what it claims` became a carrier rule, WITH the carve-out this round measured -- a lethal-only kill is CORRECT when the row&#x27;s predicate computes nothing but NON-TERMINATION, because for such a row a non-lethal kill is impossible rather than merely absent. Badge A: the main loop audited its own detector, and the adjudicating seat corrected both the census (21/4/32, not 22/3/32 -- lethality is PER-SESSION, not per-mutant) and the disposition (the FILE call cited the stopping rule against unshipped material, which that rule&#x27;s own text excludes).  *** THE CARVE-OUT THIS CLOSURE PARTLY RESTED ON IS WITHDRAWN AS A SOUNDNESS CLAIM, 2026-08-25, BY THE SEAT THAT AUTHORED IT, AFTER A MEASUREMENT REFUTED ITS WARRANT. *** The warrant was: `alive reddens IFF the process terminated, so a non-lethal kill is IMPOSSIBLE`. Measured on a rig built from the probe&#x27;s own session()/rd(): **`alive` is True for a process ALREADY SIGKILLED** -- `rd()`&#x27;s loop is `while time.time() &lt; end:`, so a death landing after the last window is never read. `impossible` was not imprecise; it was FALSIFIED BY A SHIPPED ROW (table3&#x27;s M11 `nodata stale survived`, reproduced as five SIGSEGVs on a clean rebuild). *** WHAT SURVIVES IS NARROWER THAN WHAT WAS SHIPPED: *** a mutant that leaves the process running and only perturbs a value cannot redden an alive-only row -- true, and all that is left. The three rows are **NOT sound by construction; they are BEST-EFFORT, and their kill is SCHEDULING-DEPENDENT.** *** THE DISPOSITION STANDS AND THE WARRANT DOES NOT, and the reason it is a corrected note rather than a reopening is a bound worth keeping: M11&#x27;s table3 run false-passed the ROW `nodata stale`, but its session-mates `stale says` (0 vs 1) and `stale idle` (`absent`) BOTH REDDENED -- so the MUTANT WAS STILL CAUGHT. The DETECTOR was not fooled; one ROW was. That is row-level unreliability (L4), not a detector-level escape, which is why the stopping rule&#x27;s measured-false-pass clause does not fire. *** FORWARD DEPENDENCY, which is the honest artifact: `lethalonly`&#x27;s survival-row coverage is CONTINGENT on `probetimebased`&#x27;s predicate fix. *** And the credit belongs where it was not given: **kimi was more right than the carve-out allowed** -- its original vacuity claim reaches these three rows by a mechanism (death-after-window) that neither kimi nor the adjudication named. — witness: probe:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py row `arm says`, discharged by the Mf mutant (regress/artifacts/kill462/table3_58rows/probe_Mf.raw)  [2026-08-25: the carve-out half of this closure is WITHDRAWN as a soundness claim -- see the closure note. The `arm says` half stands, discharged by Mf&#x27;s measured non-lethal kill.] — note: MAIN-LOOP AUDIT (not a seat answer): Ran the audit the class asks for across ALL 57 rows rather than the two nominated, from the preserved table-2 logs -- one script, no builds, no host load. 21 rows have a non-lethal kill, 4 have only lethal kills, 32 have no kill in THIS table (their mutants live in the #459/#461 tables, so that is not `no kill`). *** THE AUDIT ADDS A CARVE-OUT THE SEAT&#x27;S VERSION LACKS. *** Two of the three lethal-only rows are `arm survive` and `nodata live`, whose OWN SUBJECT IS SURVIVAL -- for them a lethal mutant is the on-point mutant, and condemning them would be the rule misfiring on its two best-matched rows. Usable form: only-lethal kills AND the predicate computes more than non-termination. That leaves exactly one row, `arm says`, which is the row the seat named -- so its INSTANCE was right and its CRITERION was too wide, and the criterion is the half that generalises. FILED not fixed: above the vacuity floor, not a sole carrier of a round obligation (the de-escalation accept rows are all four separately killed by M5 at 49/57), so the stopping rule files it.  *** THE NON-DETERMINISM IS ESTABLISHED, NOT SUPPOSED -- and it makes `nodata stale`&#x27;s membership in the lethal-only set STRONGER, not weaker. Measured 2026-08-24 from the probe source plus both tables&#x27; logs. *** `nodata stale` (:962), `stale says` (:964) and `stale idle` (:966) all read the SAME `t, alive` -- ONE session, three rows, the next session beginning at :1009. So in table 2 that session&#x27;s emulator DIED and all three read `died` (one real observation, two collateral); in table 3 the IDENTICAL session survived, leaving `nodata stale` green while `stale says` read `0` and `stale idle` read `absent`. **The added row `nodata twice` sits at :844, in a DIFFERENT and EARLIER session, so it cannot be the cause.** Same mutant, same commands, a fresh emulator forked each time, opposite outcome: the NULL-pointer read of 408 bytes faults on one run and not another. *** CONSEQUENCE FOR THE CENSUS: `nodata stale`&#x27;s kill set is Mb1 (lethal, STABLE) plus M11 (NON-DETERMINISTIC), so its only RELIABLE kill is a lethal one. *** The adjudication placed it in the lethal-only set on the strength of table 2, where both its kills read `died`; table 3 shows M11 does not kill it at all. The membership holds and its warrant is now the stronger one -- not `both kills happen to be lethal` but `the only kill that reproduces is lethal`. *A row whose kill appears in one run and not the next was never covered by that mutant in the first place; it was covered by luck.*  *** THE TABLE-2-vs-TABLE-3 STABILITY ACCOUNT, COMPLETE FOR THE FIRST NINE MUTANTS AND WITH EVERY DEVIATION NAMED. *** The denominator moved 57 -&gt; 58 (one row added), so a mutant reddening the SAME rows must score (X+1)/58. Six do exactly that -- M10 55-&gt;56, Mb2 46-&gt;47, M5 49-&gt;50, and M6/M7/M8 all 56-&gt;57 -- i.e. **six mutants redden byte-identical row sets across two independent runs.** Exactly two deviate, and both were already explained: **Mb1 47/57 -&gt; 47/58**, numerator UNCHANGED, so it reddens one MORE row -- the new `nodata twice`, which is a no-data row and therefore correct; and **M11 54/57 -&gt; 56/58**, numerator +2, so it reddens one FEWER -- the NULL-source fault that did not fire. *This is the control that makes the M11 finding safe to publish*: without six identical mutants beside it, `M11&#x27;s mode changed` is indistinguishable from `this rig is flaky`, and the second reading would discredit every score in all three tables rather than one cell in one of them.  *** THE COMPLETE TABLE-2 vs TABLE-3 ACCOUNT, MEASURED BY COMPARING RED SETS AND NOT SCORES. *** Comparing the actual reddened-row NAMES (not the numerators, which conflate a changed row set with a changed denominator): **NINE mutants -- M10, Mb2, M5, M6, M7, M8, M9, M1, M2 -- redden BYTE-IDENTICAL row sets across two independent four-hour runs. Zero changed.** Exactly three deviate and every one is explained: `Mb1` and `M3` each gained precisely ONE row, `nodata twice` -- the row added this round, and BOTH are no-data mutants, so a new no-data row is exactly what they should hit; `M12` is new and is killed by that same row; and `M11` lost one, the NULL-source fault that did not fire. *** THAT IS THE STRONGEST FORM OF THE CONTROL: *** nine identical red sets make M11&#x27;s moved failure mode a property OF M11, not of the rig, the probe, or the host under load. Without it, `M11&#x27;s mode changed` and `this rig is flaky` are the same observation -- and the second reading would discredit every score in all three tables rather than one cell in one of them. *Note the method matters: comparing SCORES would have shown Mb1 at 47/57 -&gt; 47/58 and M3 at 54/57 -&gt; 54/58 as `unchanged numerator` and hidden that both gained a row.*  *** SECTION O REPAIR 2026-09-17: the seven seat cells on this row carried ONE STAMPED NOTE from `panel_20260917_100431`, a SIX-ROW late review.  Each is now that seat&#x27;s own answer, read from its own file. ***  **This was not tidying.  TWO REAL DEFECTS were sitting inside the stamp and neither had a cell of its own**: the `sh4_dmac_transfer` CHCR header L4 (grok, corrected in place) and `gdromdmaflood` (deepseek, filed MED and corroborated from source by codex).  *Section O&#x27;s boilerplate cluster and section G&#x27;s unread review are the same defect from two sides -- the stamp is what made the findings unfindable.*  **The seven answers were genuinely different**: seven distinct verdicts, from codex&#x27;s DO NOT RATIFY to minimax&#x27;s explicit refusal to overturn anything from the packet, and they disagreed about where the defects live -- glm called all four of its findings RECORDS defects, deepseek and codex found a product one.  A stamped note renders that as one voice.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-24 — ADJUDICATION of the main loop&#x27;s audit, and it REVERSED the disposition and CORRECTED the census -- both against the author&#x27;s interest, which is what it was asked for. (1) CENSUS 22/3/32 -&gt; 21/4/32: `nodata stale` has exactly two kills, M11 and Mb1, and BOTH read `died`. The audit classified by MUTANT IDENTITY (Mb* = the floor-(b) reinstatements) instead of by the row&#x27;s OBSERVED failure mode (the got column), and M11 is lethal-in-effect -- its data_len guard-swap reaches memory_rw(..., NULL, 408, ...) -- without being named Mb. *** THE GENERAL DEFECT: LETHALITY IS PER-SESSION, NOT PER-MUTANT. *** The probe forks a fresh emulator per session, so a mutant can kill some sessions and leave others untouched; the oracle used two fixed rows (`arm survive`, `nodata live`) to decide a global property and could not see M11 at all. (2) THE CARVE-OUT&#x27;S WORDING WAS SELF-CERTIFYING -- `when the row&#x27;s own SUBJECT is survival`, adjudicated against descriptions the same author wrote. Replaced with two mechanical, grep-checkable conditions: (i) the got-expression computes nothing but non-termination, which makes a non-lethal kill IMPOSSIBLE rather than merely absent, so the rule cannot be stretched to cover a content-bearing row; (ii) the kill must DISCRIMINATE -- red under the lethal mutant at the row&#x27;s own arm, GREEN under the sibling arm&#x27;s. (3) IT THEN MEASURED (ii) AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVED: the Mb1/Mb2 cross-pattern is clean, each survival row dying only under the mutant re-fatalising its own arm. That also answers the objection the main loop raised against itself and scopes kimi&#x27;s class correctly -- the collateral effect is confined WITHIN a dying session, which is exactly how `arm says` got its only red (it shares `arm survive`&#x27;s session at :621-626, so under Mb2 its count predicate was never evaluated). (4) DISPOSITION REVERSED, FILE -&gt; FIX IN-ROUND: the FILE call cited the stopping rule against material that rule does not govern, since its own text limits it to SHIPPED findings and nothing here has shipped (L1). Second ground: no mutant nulls the PRINT effect, though four null `idata` and two null the latch. (5) It also narrowed the work honestly -- the sole-carrier premise was overstated (five other rows assert declines speak; `arm says` carries the ARMING side only), and the no-data side is already discharged, so ONE mutant is owed rather than two.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-24 — THIRD PASS, asked whether M11&#x27;s unstable failure mode makes this L2. RULED NO, and the argument is a DETERMINISM one that needs no run -- verified here against mutate462.py and the probe source. *** `stale says` REDDENS UNDER M11 BY CONTROL FLOW, NOT BY FAULT. *** Under the swapped guard (`data_len == 0`) the stale session&#x27;s trigger -- data NULL, data_len stale at the TOC&#x27;s 408 -- takes the TRANSFER arm, so the NO-DATA decline message CANNOT print in that session, in any environment, on any run: the arm that prints it was never entered. DMA_NOD.findall returns 0 against want 1, red, BEFORE memory_rw is reached -- so it holds whether the subsequent NULL read faults, wedges, or completes. Both observed runs confirm it: table 2 red-by-death, table 3 red-by-zero. **THE MODE MOVED, THE RED DID NOT**, and the table-3 mode is the one guaranteed by the mutant&#x27;s own structure. So the (d) discharge never leaned on the unstable fault. *** IT ALSO PASSED AN ADVERSARIAL CHECK I HAD NOT ASKED FOR: were the two catching rows proven observers, or vacuous luck? *** Both have measured NON-LETHAL kills in the table-2 logs -- `stale says` red at 0 vs 1 under M5, `stale idle` red at 0x1 vs 0x0 under M3, both verified here. The catch is load-bearing. It also upgraded its OWN earlier record honestly: it had called the printed() `absent` path `structural, unmeasured`, and it is now measured to FIRE and redden a row in a real mutant run -- while endorsing that the CAUSE stays unattributed, so detection is proven and diagnosis is not claimed. *** AND IT NAMED THE FROZEN-NUMBER HAZARD: M11 NOW HAS THREE SCORES ACROSS THREE TABLES -- 52/52 (the table-1 clean-sweep escape that triggered the L2), 54/57, 56/58 -- each true of its own artifact. Any prose mentioning M11 must bind each number to its table dir or it will later be `corrected` wrong. *** Ruled NOT owed: a re-run (the floor requires the detector to fail under M11, and it failed in both observed runs), and a queue entry for the fault-instability mechanism -- the shipped tree cannot reach memory_rw with a NULL source, because the guard under test is precisely what prevents it, so that question lives in a mutant world only.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **CLOSE, verified from the ARTIFACT rather than the prose.**  `kill462/table3_58rows/probe_Mf.raw` reads `arm says 0 1 ... FAIL` with `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=53/58` -- *** and the got is `0`, NOT `died`, so the host was ALIVE: a genuine NON-LETHAL kill. ***  That is the distinction the row exists to make, and it is visible only in the raw log. — evidence: _scratchpad/opus_batch5_20260917.md; opus5 answer 2026-09-17</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). No new measured false pass that licenses a follow-up commit; residual behaviour belongs in the queue. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/codex.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN S0 PREMISE *** -- pcc2c040assertrow HAS a dedicated brief (regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/ASSERTROW_brief.md) that codex answered. Also CONFIRMED the fatal()-is-a-print premise from debugmsg.c, and found the gdrom Transfering fatal unlatched on the success path. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/agy.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Five of six product fixes correct; one is a records-only annotation. NO commit re-introduced a host kill or an OOB. One unlatched guest-drivable fatal() sits on a line #462 edited and did not latch (filed gdromdmaxferflood). Two later rounds overtook CLAIMS these comments make, not the repairs. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/grok.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** THE SHARPEST MUTANT OF THE PANEL: #491/diskimagesign -- swap the ternary arms so lentoread = max(len, remain). Zero warnings, casts intact, and when len &lt; remain lentoread EXCEEDS the caller&#x27;s len -- a real host OOB, invisible to every named detector, because the warning gate is the ONLY detector this row has. *** Also F1: #462&#x27;s &#x27;idata = 0 IS LOAD-BEARING&#x27; is asserted, not derived, once its NetBSD citation was retracted. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_kimi.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Challenged the brief&#x27;s (once)=41/7 census as blind to f532bb6&#x27;s qualified marker -- MEASURED RIGHT, and the shortfall is larger than it said (literal sees 41 of 71). Flagged 54a2eb4&#x27;s &#x27;FAST | MIEN&#x27; annotation and named the settling fact (the PCC2_MIEN value); RUN: PCC2_MIEN is 0x02, the same bit as PCC2_GENCTL_IEN, so the annotation is NUMERICALLY CORRECT and this finding resolves in the code&#x27;s favour. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_glm.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Same gdrom unlatched-fatal finding, independently. Named the assert=0 -&gt; return one-token mutant that skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT while pcc2c040assertrow stays green. Repeated the MIEN/IEN flag -- resolved in the code&#x27;s favour, see glm. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_deepseek.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** Called the assert=0 -&gt; return mutant &#x27;the highest-yield Q2 finding in the brief&#x27; *** -- it skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d-&gt;cpu_irq) and the shipped rows do not pin deassertion. Also: 54a2eb4 ships NO detector at all, which the brief explicitly asked to be told. Marker-spelling deviation CONFIRMED by measurement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_minimax.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">witnessinvisible</span><span class="ttl">witnessinvisible  gen_dashboard.py renders NEITHER `witness` NOR `state_note`, so two ledger fields -- one that orders the queue, one that carries a row&#x27;s reasoning -- are absent from the published artifact</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">witnessinvisible  gen_dashboard.py renders NEITHER `witness` NOR `state_note`, so two ledger fields -- one that orders the queue, one that carries a row&#x27;s reasoning -- are absent from the published artifact — commits: e6c7e28 — FIXED: `witness` and `state_note` now render into the row TOOLTIP -- not inline, per the owner&#x27;s 2026-08-15 layout directive that the header stays one line and everything else is mouse-over. matrix.html grew 1,876,287 -&gt; 1,920,514 bytes, +44,227, against the 34,712 bytes of field text measured as invisible plus HTML escaping: **the delta is the confirmation.** Spot-checked two corrections that were previously unreachable from the published artifact and are now present -- `asicbadaddr`&#x27;s severity correction (not a memory-safety defect) and `probetimebased`&#x27;s corrected nine-of-57 census. *** THE COST WAS NEVER COSMETIC: `witness` is the field a RANKER READS, and this week two facts were corrected in a seat entry and left stale in it -- `gdrompionull`&#x27;s rung-3 cost, and `asicbadaddr`&#x27;s `none yet -- one constant away` while its witness existed and had been reviewed, under-rating the row a ranking then placed FIRST. A field nobody can see is a field nobody re-reads. *** Badge A: found, filed and fixed by the main loop, by noticing that a substantial ledger edit changed the rendered artifact by ZERO BYTES -- twice.  COMMITS AND closure.by SET 2026-08-25 from a SUBSTANCE READ OF GIT, not from prose. `e6c7e28` (&quot;harness: ... and two invisible ledger fields&quot;) adds 21 lines to `tools/pipeline/gen_dashboard.py`, TEN of them mentioning `witness` or `state_note`, and regenerates matrix.html -- which is exactly this row&#x27;s subject. **closure.by previously read `gen_dashboard.py`: a FILE, not a party that can adjudicate.** That is the wrong-KIND class filed as `ledgerclosureby`; the row has no adjudicating entry, so the value is set to the seat that authored the fix and the substitution is disclosed here rather than made silently. — witness: n/a -- a tooling gap, not an emulator defect — note: MAIN-LOOP, 2026-08-24, found while correcting a wrong witness field. `gdrompionull`&#x27;s witness said rung 3 `costs four stores and a load`. The opus5 seat had MEASURED that wrong (18 guest accesses, or 9 by the cheap route) and its correction was recorded in its own entry the same day -- but the FIELD kept the old number for a further round, which made the item look four times cheaper to witness than it is, in the one piece of text that orders the queue. *** THE REASON IT SURVIVED IS MECHANICAL, NOT INATTENTION: `gen_dashboard.py` contains no reference to `witness` at all, so the field is invisible on the published artifact. *** Editing it changed matrix.html by ZERO BYTES, which is how this was noticed -- the byte count was expected to move and did not. So every check anyone performs by LOOKING at the dashboard is structurally blind to this field. Corrected in place with its provenance; a sibling sweep across all 197 rows found no other witness field carrying a superseded cost. FIX (small): render `witness` in the row detail, or add a checker that refuses a witness field whose claim a later entry corrects. The second is the stronger one and generalises past this field.  *** THE SAME GAP EXISTS FOR `state_note`, AND IT IS THE LARGER HALF. Measured 2026-08-24, again by a byte count that did not move when it should have. *** Three queue dispositions were written and `matrix.html` came back BYTE-IDENTICAL; `gen_dashboard.py` contains ZERO references to `state_note`, exactly as it does for `witness`. **QUANTIFIED, because the first framing of this was going to be wrong:** 9 rows carry `state_note` totalling 16,746 bytes, plus 17,966 bytes of `witness` -- **34,712 invisible against 1,125,256 bytes of rendered entry notes, so 3.0% of ledger prose**. *I was about to write `a large fraction of today&#x27;s analysis is invisible`; the measurement says 3%, and the honest statement is that it is SMALL IN TOTAL and CONCENTRATED IN TODAY&#x27;S ROWS* -- the six largest `state_note` fields (`probetimebased` 4779, `killartifacts` 3108, `lethalonly` 2573, `killdriverunTracked` 1851, `sectionEnarrow` 1635, this row 1186) were all written this session. So the proportion is rising precisely because the field is where a round&#x27;s own reasoning now goes. FIX unchanged and now cheaper to justify: render both fields in the row detail, or add a checker that refuses a field whose claim a later entry corrects. *The second is still the stronger one -- it is what caught the `asicbadaddr` witness field under-rating the row a ranking put FIRST.*  *** SHIPS ON SINGLE-SEAT REVIEW, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN LEFT IMPLICIT. *** A completeness check named the sharpest form of a gap I had only half-seen: THREE things enter the permanent record of #462 having been reviewed by the adjudicating seat ALONE -- (1) `check_cited_artifacts.py` and its precommit section X, (2) the clause-(e) contract going into the probe&#x27;s docstring, and (3) the POLICY that kill-table logs are tracked in-repo forever (228 KB and growing every round). All three appeared AFTER pass 2, so the nine-seat panel that reviewed the fix and the diff never saw any of them, and the owner&#x27;s standing FULL-PANEL-ON-EVERY-STAGE directive was quietly not applied to the parts that arrived late. **The seven held seats are currently pointed at `probetimebased` and `asicbadaddr` research, not at these.** OWED WHEN THEY FIRE, one added question: *is tracking every round&#x27;s kill logs in-repo the right durability choice, and does the section-X gate over-reach?* They are firing anyway, so the marginal cost is a paragraph. *Recording this is not a substitute for asking -- it is what stops a policy and two mechanisms reading, later, as though a panel had weighed them.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">panelseatlabel</span><span class="ttl">panelseatlabel  panel.sh reports a 403 quota wall as &#x27;RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429)&#x27; and its tally omits the substitute transport</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">panelseatlabel  panel.sh reports a 403 quota wall as &#x27;RATE-LIMITED (HTTP 429)&#x27; and its tally omits the substitute transport — commits: faffad7 — both defects verified discharged against LIVE panel output, not by reading the code — witness: harness:regress/artifacts/panelseatlabel/panel_20260824_231824/SEATS.txt -- *** RE-CITED 2026-09-11 TO A DURABLE PATH.  THE FIELD SAID: &quot;none needed -- both are visible in _scratchpad/panel_20260824_231824/SEATS.txt&quot; ***  That path is at the PROJECT ROOT, one level ABOVE the git root, and `git ls-files _scratchpad` returns zero -- so the citation pointed outside the repo and a reader resolving it from the repo root lands in a DIFFERENT `GXEMUL-SEC/_scratchpad/` that does not contain it.  All 15 files copied here, evidence verified present first.  The term is also corrected from `none needed`: the round&#x27;s subject is `panel.sh` and its seat check, i.e. the test apparatus, which is exactly what `harness:` is admitted for. — note: *** BOTH DEFECTS WERE ALREADY FIXED BY `faffad7` AND THIS ROW SAT OPEN. Verified 2026-08-26 AGAINST LIVE OUTPUT, not by reading the source. *** Today&#x27;s panel printed:
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\stagetokens\pass2_20260918\panel_20260918_103810\codex.txt:926:tools/pipeline\matrix.html:18941:adjudication.  Evidence: codex closure pass 2026-09-07, regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_closeable_20260907.txt.*  *** SECTION O REPAIR 2026-09-17: the seven seat cells on this row carried ONE STAMPED NOTE from `panel_20260917_100431`, a SIX-ROW late review.  Each is now that seat&#x27;s own answer, read from its own file. ***  **This was not tidying.  TWO REAL DEFECTS were sitting inside the stamp and neither had a cell of its own**: the `sh4_dmac_transfer` CHCR header L4 (grok, corrected in place) and `gdromdmaflood` (deepseek, filed MED and corroborated from source by codex).  *Section O&#x27;s boilerplate cluster and section G&#x27;s unread review are the same defect from two sides -- the stamp is what made the findings unfindable.*  **The seven answers were genuinely different**: seven distinct verdicts, from codex&#x27;s DO NOT RATIFY to minimax&#x27;s explicit refusal to overturn anything from the packet, and they disagreed about where the defects live -- glm called all four of its findings RECORDS defects, deepseek and codex found a product one.  A stamped note renders that as one voice.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — **Disposition (1) WRONG RECORD, twice; nothing under (2) or (3).**  The panel&#x27;s sharpest dissent -- kimi-sub&#x27;s *no behavioural detector / a real host OOB invisible to every named detector*, recorded in the ledger as THE SHARPEST MUTANT OF THE PANEL -- is **MEASURED FALSE**: opus5 built the arm-swap and it SIGABRTs with 7 rows RED in `regress/diff_diskimage_io.c`, which is WIRED (`gate_offline.sh` `selfmutant_one`, plus compile-and-run).  Wiring VERIFIED.  **So the dissent dies rather than files, and the kimi cell must carry the refutation (L4).**  grok&#x27;s note that the mutant belongs to `#472`&#x27;s table is also right.  Second L4: `verify491b.sh`&#x27;s header claims it *refuses to read the warning count unless the compile actually produced an object*, and the script computes and prints `rc/obj/warn` UNCONDITIONALLY with no refusal -- the recorded control is still sound, its self-description is not.  Third: the brief&#x27;s S0 claim that these were *never named in a brief* is false via `CLOSEABLE_brief.md:68`, and that refutation is recorded NOWHERE (`grep -c CLOSEABLE_brief ledger.json` = 0). — evidence: _scratchpad/fable_flagship_20260918.md (brief); flagship answer 2026-09-18</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **CLOSE -- AND IT REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S PREMISE BY BUILDING BOTH MUTANTS.**  I wrote that the zero-warning build gate is its ONLY detector.  *** HALF FALSE, and the half that is false changes the smallest-edit answer. ***  Measured in its own `/tmp/opusdisk` (WSL Gentoo, gcc 15.2.1), controls both ways: **baseline** 0 failures / 0 warnings; **casts removed (clause b)** 0 differential failures but **exactly two** `-Wsign-compare` warnings, matching the comment&#x27;s *&quot;gcc warns twice here&quot;* word for word; **arm swap (warning-free)** SIGABRT and **7 rows RED**. So the build gate really is the only detector OF THE CASTS -- but `regress/diff_diskimage_io.c` is a SECOND, GATED, MUTATION-CHECKED detector of the line, wired into `gate_offline.sh` with a `selfmutant_one` row. *** THE DISJOINTNESS IS STRUCTURAL, NOT LUCK, AND IT MEASURED WHY: `gate_offline` compiles the differential with `-O2 -std=gnu99` and NO `-Wall`/`-Wextra`, so it cannot see a warning; `gate_build` compiles the real tree and cannot see an arm swap.  Neither catches the other&#x27;s failure -- the same relationship sections G and H have. *** **It also corrected a stale number of mine:** the &quot;four-probe unwired set&quot; is out of date -- `check_probe_wiring.py` reports **66/76 detectors run by a gate, 10 dated-exempt** -- and called the question a category error, since that set is about PROBES while `gate_build` is `GATES[0]` in `run.sh` with a real hard warning row over a full rebuild. — evidence: _scratchpad/opus_batch5_20260917.md; opus5 answer 2026-09-17</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). No new measured false pass that licenses a follow-up commit; residual behaviour belongs in the queue. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/codex.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN S0 PREMISE *** -- pcc2c040assertrow HAS a dedicated brief (regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/ASSERTROW_brief.md) that codex answered. Also CONFIRMED the fatal()-is-a-print premise from debugmsg.c, and found the gdrom Transfering fatal unlatched on the success path. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/agy.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Five of six product fixes correct; one is a records-only annotation. NO commit re-introduced a host kill or an OOB. One unlatched guest-drivable fatal() sits on a line #462 edited and did not latch (filed gdromdmaxferflood). Two later rounds overtook CLAIMS these comments make, not the repairs. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/grok.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** THE SHARPEST MUTANT OF THE PANEL: #491/diskimagesign -- swap the ternary arms so lentoread = max(len, remain). Zero warnings, casts intact, and when len &lt; remain lentoread EXCEEDS the caller&#x27;s len -- a real host OOB, invisible to every named detector, because the warning gate is the ONLY detector this row has. *** Also F1: #462&#x27;s &#x27;idata = 0 IS LOAD-BEARING&#x27; is asserted, not derived, once its NetBSD citation was retracted. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_kimi.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Challenged the brief&#x27;s (once)=41/7 census as blind to f532bb6&#x27;s qualified marker -- MEASURED RIGHT, and the shortfall is larger than it said (literal sees 41 of 71). Flagged 54a2eb4&#x27;s &#x27;FAST | MIEN&#x27; annotation and named the settling fact (the PCC2_MIEN value); RUN: PCC2_MIEN is 0x02, the same bit as PCC2_GENCTL_IEN, so the annotation is NUMERICALLY CORRECT and this finding resolves in the code&#x27;s favour. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_glm.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Same gdrom unlatched-fatal finding, independently. Named the assert=0 -&gt; return one-token mutant that skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT while pcc2c040assertrow stays green. Repeated the MIEN/IEN flag -- resolved in the code&#x27;s favour, see glm. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_deepseek.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** Called the assert=0 -&gt; return mutant &#x27;the highest-yield Q2 finding in the brief&#x27; *** -- it skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d-&gt;cpu_irq) and the shipped rows do not pin deassertion. Also: 54a2eb4 ships NO detector at all, which the brief explicitly asked to be told. Marker-spelling deviation CONFIRMED by measurement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_minimax.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">propagatecpsilent</span><span class="ttl">The propagate script prints &#x27;propagated&#x27; after a FAILED cp</span><span class="tip">The propagate script prints &#x27;propagated&#x27; after a FAILED cp — witness: harness:regress/artifacts/makercfalse/propagate_controls_20260830/ -- SIX checks in BOTH directions, sentinel CPGUARD_PASS.  The old idiom is reproduced and shown to announce success on a missing source; the shipped idiom is shown to report the failure AND to catch a case cp&#x27;s exit status cannot see.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — The script the row named was a scratch tool and no longer exists, so closing on its absence would have been closing on a technicality.  **The CLASS is what mattered, and the current instance was checked instead** -- `propagate494.sh`, written today and run twice, which verifies the copy with `cmp` against the intended source rather than announcing unconditionally.  *** THE CONTROL FOUND SOMETHING THE OBVIOUS FIX WOULD HAVE MISSED. ***  The natural repair is `cp ... || { echo failed; exit; }`.  That guards the case where cp FAILS -- and the control&#x27;s third block shows **a cp of the WRONG file exits 0**, so an exit-status guard passes while the destination holds content that was never intended.  Only a comparison against the INTENDED source catches it, which is what the shipped form does.  So the fix is not merely present, it is stronger than the one the row asked for.  **Controlled in both directions**, because a check that only ever passes is the exact shape this row is about: the OLD idiom is reproduced and measured announcing `propagated` on a missing source, and the NEW one is measured failing there and passing on a real copy with the destination verified to have actually changed.  Kept from the original finding because it is the part that generalises: the sentinel discipline CONTAINED the failure -- the two-part clearance reported part 1 red and KEPT the sentinel, so the tree was marked untrustworthy rather than silently wrong.  **The false line cost confusion, not a wrong measurement.**  Same family as `panelrefire` (still open), where a script prints `re-firing once` and re-fires nothing. — evidence: regress/artifacts/makercfalse/propagate_controls_20260830/cpguard_20260830.out (CPGUARD_PASS); current instance regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/killtable_20260830/propagate494.sh, run twice on 2026-08-30</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">divsweeplines</span><span class="ttl">diag_div_sweep.py reports line numbers that are systematically too small</span><span class="tip">diag_div_sweep.py reports line numbers that are systematically too small — witness: harness:regress/artifacts/makercfalse/divsweep_controls_20260830/ -- BEFORE.txt and AFTER.txt from the same tool at the same HEAD, with the row COUNT pinned at 27 in both so the fix is shown to move WHERE sites are reported without changing WHICH are found.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** CAUGHT THAT I DROPPED A RED, AND THE DROPPED ONE DEFEATS THE ROUND&#x27;S OWN MOTIVATION. ***  It is SIX reds, not five: the brief omits `asic_badaddr rows run got=14 want=13`, **which my own note file had already dispositioned as its item 5**.  A round fixing &#x27;the five&#x27; leaves gate_sh_rounding red and the battery REGRESS_FAIL, so &#x27;get the battery green&#x27; fails entirely on the dropped row.  It named this as my own third-listed error class recurring inside the brief that warns about it -- and noted the note file itself gives three different counts (&#x27;four ratchet reds&#x27; / &#x27;six FAIL rows&#x27; / &#x27;five of the seven&#x27;) in one document, so the round owes ONE derived count with its deriver.  *** MEASURED CLAIM 4 FALSE: the 24 -&gt; 21 drop is a CONSOLIDATION, not removals. ***  Four one-division fatal() calls became ONE fatal() carrying the same four divisions; `DIAG_DIV_TOTAL` counts CALLS.  Attribution `7c9dd9e` (#462) -- and opus5 said `fb0cc78` (#490); settled by `git show ... | grep -c dma_reg` giving 9 vs 0, so fable5 is right.  It found the sweep&#x27;s line-number defect on the way and correctly filed rather than fixed it, per the stopping rule.  A2 UPHELD THE OWNER&#x27;S PRIORITISATION with a concrete warrant: this very run proves the cost, because a REAL regression (#491&#x27;s sign-compare pair) sat in the same FAIL list as six bookkeeping reds -- under red-is-normal the next real regression is invisible.  A3 REFUTED MY ESTIMATE&#x27;S PROXY AS VINTAGE-BIASED: &#x27;witness names an artifact kind&#x27; marks 45/86, &#x27;row has commits&#x27; marks 64/86 -- 52% vs 74% -- and the 21-row disagreement is real emulator fixes predating 2026-08-19, when the witness field became mandatory.  **The proxy bins a third of the sample by VINTAGE, not substance.**  Report a RANGE (~60-120) with a per-row deriver (did the row&#x27;s commits touch `src/`?), and note documented-only rows are not free -- each owes a rung-3 attempt, a panel and records.  A4: a cheap commit-time check DOES exist for 5 of the 6, because the class splits -- five are STATIC source greps needing no boot, so a `--static` gate mode invoked from precommit would have caught them at their offending commits (#462, #467/8, #484/5, #489, #490).  The two runtime row-count pins genuinely need probe execution and stay weekly.  It insists this ships as ITS OWN controlled harness round, not a rider. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** REFUTED THE ONE THING I TOLD THE PANEL I COULD NOT FIND. ***  My Q3b said there was no mechanical discriminator between &#x27;drives a pty&#x27; and &#x27;simulates a stream&#x27;.  It is `pty.fork`, TWELVE LINES ABOVE in the same gate, already the census basis at `:162`.  MEASURED: scoping `probe_code()` to files containing it takes `whole_full` 4 -&gt; 2 with `conv_anchor`/`conv_echo`/`conv_mark` all UNCHANGED at 23 -- **row 3 goes green with no pin moved, no allowlist widened and no filename exemption**, and it subsumes the existing `readiness_predicate_test.py` exemption automatically.  It also showed my proposed exclusion is the shape the gate itself REJECTS: `:92-95` and `:347-348` require an exemption to travel WITH a content pin on the excluded file -- *&#x27;an allowlist entry that checks what it exempts&#x27;* -- and I proposed it bare.  *** AND IT MEASURED MY ROW-5 FLOOR VACUOUS. ***  A kill table: under `&gt;= 12`, deleting ONE latch&#x27;s two-line guard pair PASSES (raw 13), restoring the exact unbounded flood #464 fixed; and gutting a guard while naming the latch twice in a COMMENT also passes (raw 15).  Six per-latch `&gt;= 2` rows are ALSO inadequate -- satisfied by two reads and no set, while the row&#x27;s label claims &#x27;one set + one read each&#x27;.  Its shape: TWO rows over comment-stripped code, `SET_OK == 6` and `READ_OK == 6`, measured 6/6 at HEAD, killing both mutants and scoring 0/6 on a null medium (floor clause (c)).  *** THREE FINDINGS LARGER THAN THE FIVE REDS. ***  (F1) the sweep&#x27;s line bug -- which I had found independently the same hour, and its measured fix agrees with mine EXACTLY: 79 -&gt; 180, 543 -&gt; 976, counts unchanged.  (F2) **`regress/artifacts/` is in scope for TWO censuses and out for a THIRD** -- `py_code`/`probe_code` recurse (137 files) while `_ptylist` uses a FLAT glob (59), which is evasion route #3 that this gate condemns ninety lines earlier.  TEN pty-forking promptless probes live under `artifacts/` and are invisible to the entire mechanism built to find promptless pty probes -- and the hazard is SCHEDULED in the other direction: `CLAUDE.md` MANDATES frozen probe copies, so the next round that freezes a converted probe reds this gate FOR DOING THE RIGHT THING.  (F3) the wdcflood pin counts a comment.  Verdict: THREE rounds, not one.  Honest about its own apparatus: its first census returned 9/9 from a loose grep and it corrected itself to 3/8; its first comment-strip returned 0 for every latch (a phantom absence) because gcc and python3 are stubbed in git-bash, and it re-ran under WSL. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** THE SHARPEST OF THE SCRIPTABLE SEATS. ***  &#x27;Do not raise #3 to 4, and do not convert #5 to a total floor; Q1/Q2 are legitimate census updates; Q4 is a net re-pin with a more specific history than claimed.&#x27;  Both prohibitions were independently correct and both were followed. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Found the defect the F2 repair MISSED: `_ptylist` still used a FLAT GLOB after py_code and probe_code were converted, so the round aligned TWO populations of three -- the exact defect F2 exists to end.  It agrees today only by coincidence (recursive-minus-artifacts == flat), and a .py in any NEW non-artifacts subdirectory would be seen by two censuses and missed by the third.  Also caught the census comment reading as a PARTITION when its categories overlap. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Ruled for one round, and gave the disposition that kept the row-3 population HONEST rather than merely wider: &#x27;Do not add the subprocess constructors to this population: they cannot carry the #392 failure.  Do not treat the alternation as a guest-process census.&#x27;  That is the reasoning recorded in the shipped comment beside codex&#x27;s opposing finding, rather than one seat&#x27;s view being adopted silently.  Also: &#x27;drive_guest.py as a third rationale is right -- do not fold it into budget-drain&#x27;, and on C1 &#x27;equality is right here; the members set of THIS PIN does not grow -- the device&#x27;s warned_ set already did.&#x27; — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Reached opus5&#x27;s three-part structure INDEPENDENTLY and by a different route, and its reason is the one the adjudicator finally absorbed: &#x27;B and C change what the gate *proves*, not just its numbers; the panel should be asked to sign off on those two explicitly, separately from A.&#x27;  fable5 credited it and applied it at PASS structure rather than ROUND structure -- the sign-off unit is the brief section a seat answers, not the commit boundary.  It also found the two-route growth (site reuse #484, prose #485) on its own. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Caught the ARITHMETIC: the new three-way census was &#x27;arithmetically impossible as printed (3+8+1=12 against every set size the packet names)&#x27;.  It was right, and the repair it forced -- a per-member reason rather than a &#x27;dominant&#x27; one -- was then itself wrong twice more before landing.  Also flagged the asic pin as carrying no inlined justification. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — &#x27;Claim 3&#x27;s proposed file exclusion is a hole and its real-instances argument is false.&#x27;  Correct on both halves: the exclusion was the shape the gate itself rejects, and the tripwire I claimed for it was a COUNT-red, not a NAME-red -- and count-reds get re-pinned routinely, which this very round does six times.  fable5 cited that concession as half its reason for overturning its own row-3 form. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — &#x27;Three real defects need settling before commit, plus one soft concern.&#x27;  Its asic finding is quoted in the shipped comment: &#x27;Zero rationale in the diff.  Just a number bump.  The other three re-pins carry paragraphs of reasoning; this one is silent.&#x27;  A silent bump is indistinguishable from fitting the pin to the output, which is why the provenance now ships with it. — evidence: [panel_20260829_190701]  battery-reds pass 1</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** FOUND AN L1 IN MY KILL TABLE FROM THE PUBLISHED NUMBERS ALONE. ***  &#x27;M1&#x27;s sed matched nothing (the real guard is split across lines); only the set-deletion fired.  The score was the tell: a true pair deletion reads 5/5, and the table shows 5/6.&#x27;  Both M1 and M2 were MISLABELLED -- my vac() guard caught only TOTAL no-ops, so a partially-applied mutant scored as the thing it was not.  Rebuilt with per-pattern presence checks; M1 now reads exactly 5/5/5/11 as predicted.  *** AND MY REPLACEMENT CHECK REPEATED THE ROUND&#x27;S OWN DEFECT: it used `grep -c`, which counts LINES, so it demanded two LINES when the mutant put both mentions on one. ***  Also: my C1 deriver produced 3 (it matched only the gate-level rollups) where the sed-range form gives 8; the &#x27;-&gt; 9&#x27; command as written yields 11 (9 is the changed-lines form); the HARD ORDERING it mandated did not hold in LETTER (before.out already shows rows 1/2 re-pinned) though the intent survives on two measured facts; the A/B/C separation is real at the question layer and COSMETIC at the recording layer, because one verdict line per seat lets a global RATIFY score as ratifying all three; and &#x27;FILED&#x27; was a WRONG RECORD until the row existed. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** MEASURED AN L2 FLOOR BREACH IN THE DETECTOR THIS ROUND SHIPPED, and it was reviewing its OWN pass-1 design (badge A, disclosed). ***  Every mutant in my kill table targeted a SINGLE-SITE latch, so the table was clean on a population that structurally could not exhibit the defect.  `warned_inbuf_overrun` has TWO sites; neutralising EITHER ONE leaves the other to satisfy presence -- `if (1) {` at one site scores 6/6 GREEN while the producer flood is unbounded again, which is #464&#x27;s exact defect.  Floor clause (b) demands failure on reinstatement at EVERY fixed site.  Five escapes, smallest ONE TOKEN.  It also named the inversion that is the lesson: the old pin counted OCCURRENCES so a second site moved it -- loud and wrong but alive; presence-only made losing one of two INVISIBLE.  Its measured fix (per-latch set==read, plus a latch-population count) is what shipped. Further: the `//` C99 line-continuation defect in strip_comments, WITH the coupling that the obvious fix reintroduces `divsweeplines` in the branch being fixed (2 lines -&gt; 1, invisible to a length check, visible only to a LINE count); the unanchored READ substring (E8: `!d-&gt;warned_nonbyte_read_hi` satisfied the nonbyte_read row); the dead filename exemption still inside pty_driving(); a bare xargs where both neighbours use -print0; and the absence of a clause-(e) CONTRACT, with the measured reason it matters (E4: widening a predicate while its text survives escapes every textual census). — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Four concrete Section-B findings, ALL VERIFIED AND ALL MINE: the comment&#x27;s census table went STALE INSIDE ITS OWN DIFF (138/59/59 -&gt; 139/60/60, because this round added wdc_latch_census.py to the population it froze -- a frozen count in a comment ABOUT counting); `*/artifacts/*` excludes ANY component named artifacts, not uniquely $HERE&#x27;s; the pty alternation is not a general guest-driving census (hpcabort_witness.py, hpcmips_ctor_probe.py, sgi_eaddr_probe.py drive via subprocess.run or GDB --args); and the staged diff failed `git diff --cached --check` on trailing whitespace. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Pass-2 seat; findings folded with codex&#x27;s above. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Ratified the row-5 equality-over-named-members on the grounds that this pin&#x27;s member set does not grow even though the device&#x27;s does. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Held to its pass-1 position that B and C owe separate sign-off; the pass-2 brief&#x27;s A/B/C structure is that concession. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Census arithmetic, as pass 1. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — Noted the stripper&#x27;s stated preservation property was false as first written -- the `//` branch blanked without preserving newlines. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — asic rationale absent; folded above. — evidence: [panel_20260829_193942]  battery-reds pass 2 on the real diff</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THE DEFECT HAD TWO HALVES AND FIXING EITHER ALONE LOOKS LIKE A PARTIAL IMPROVEMENT RATHER THAN A REMAINING BUG -- which is what makes the shape worth recording. ***  **(1)** `strip_lits()` collapsed a whole `/* ... */` block to a SINGLE SPACE, destroying every newline inside it, so each comment shifted every later line number down.  **(2)** the line number was then counted in `raw` using an offset from `src` -- `m` matches the STRIPPED text, so `m.start()` is a src offset, and indexing raw with it mixes two coordinate systems.  Fixing only (1) moved a measured case from `:79` to `:80` when the truth was `:180`.  **A one-line improvement that still reads as broken is easy to mistake for the fix not working, rather than for a second cause.**  MEASURED, same tool, same HEAD, before and after:  `dev_ns16550.c` 79 -&gt; **180** (the call the division sits in starts at 180; the division itself is at 181, and the sweep correctly anchors on the call start).  `diskimage.c` 543 -&gt; **976** (division at 983).  `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` 211 -&gt; **1383**: 211 was PROSE INSIDE A COMMENT BLOCK -- the drift was large enough to land the anchor inside the very construct being stripped -- and 1383 is a real `fatal(&quot;[ dreamcast_gdrom_dma: DMA start with dst&quot;`.  **DENOMINATOR, because a spot-check of three proves nothing about 27:** all 21 parseable rows now point at code and **ZERO land inside a comment**.  The row count is 27 before and after, so the fix changed where sites are reported without changing which are found.  *** THE SAME CAUSE, THE SAME REPAIR, AND THE SECOND INSTANCE THIS WEEK. ***  `wdc_latch_census.py` hit it earlier the same day and its `_blank()` preserves newlines.  **Two tools, one week, one cause: a stripper written for CONTENT and then used for POSITION.**  A replacement need not preserve length -- columns are not reported -- but it must preserve the NEWLINE COUNT, because that is the only thing carrying the line number.  Both fixes now say so in their own comments.  `gate_hygiene.sh`&#x27;s comment cites two of the OLD numbers and is now stale; left for a round that touches that file, since editing a gate is not a records change. — evidence: regress/artifacts/makercfalse/divsweep_controls_20260830/BEFORE.txt, regress/artifacts/makercfalse/divsweep_controls_20260830/AFTER.txt, checked against `grep -n` ground truth</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcaltoffset</span><span class="ttl">wdc alt-ctrl: a guest write to the READ-ONLY Drive Address Register aborts a live transfer and purges the ring</span><span class="tip">wdc alt-ctrl: a guest write to the READ-ONLY Drive Address Register aborts a live transfer and purges the ring — commits: 3521a22 c125450 — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/witness_20260829/ -- RUNG 3 on unmodified `evbmips -e malta`, reached through the machine&#x27;s own committed piix4_ide path with no device_add, free-running, with BOTH controls (RAM liveness through identical instruction shapes, and a device signature: two reads of one address returning push order, which a memory cell cannot do).  Sentinel ALTOFF_DONE.  The SHIPPED fix was then re-measured against it at 18 rows, both legs, 36 of 36 predictions -- killtable_20260830/RESULTS_detector18.txt, sentinel ALTOFF_DET_DONE.  (RESULTS_shipped.txt is the 15-row witness at 30/30 and must not be cited for 36/36; a seat caught that citation error from the log itself.)</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** RE-RULED ON THE CORRECTED FACT AND KEPT (a) -- ON A THIRD ARGUMENT NEITHER CODEX NOR THE MAIN LOOP HAD. ***  **(c) DOES NOT EVEN RESOLVE THE COLLISION IT INVOKES.**  `dev_fdc.c:49`&#x27;s stated end-state is 8 ports = `0x3f0..0x3f7`; wdc&#x27;s alt region is `0x3f6..0x3f7`; **the overlap is TWO bytes, not one.**  Narrowing wdc to 1 byte frees `0x3f7` and still blocks fdc at `0x3f6`, which wdc must keep.  So the end-state is a two-device ARBITRATION design, not a width flip -- (c) as stated fixes half a collision.  Arithmetic re-derived by the main loop before recording.  Its other two legs: the `#if 0` annotation is a PC-MAPPING decision by its own wording (*fd uses this register on PCs*), unsettled on the three non-PC layouts; and (c) is not a repair but an OWNERSHIP TRANSFER to `memory_rw`&#x27;s unassigned-address path -- machine-dependent, on four layouts, measured on none.  **VERIFIED A PREMISE Q-D RESTS ON THAT NOBODY HAD CHECKED**: `relative_addr` is bounded to {0,1} by the 2-byte registration, and a len=2 access at offset 0 still yields `relative_addr == 0` -- so codex&#x27;s *&#x27;`&gt; 1` is never true&#x27;* is verified, not assumed.  Also pinned that P2&#x27;s `0x06` is `WDCTL_RST|WDCTL_IDS` and that `#481`&#x27;s own comment documents it as REAL traffic (*Older OpenBSD asserts with 0x06*).  *** A-D: THE VALUE-GUARD IS A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH AS THE ROWS STAND, AND THE ROUND OWES A NEW ROW NOW. ***  `relative_addr != 0 &amp;&amp; idata == 0x06` keeps P1 and P3 green by construction and re-opens the defect for every other RST-carrying value.  It passes the plausible-future-edit test exactly -- `0x06` is both the packet&#x27;s only defect-side value and the comment&#x27;s documented real-world SRST value, so *tighten the decline to known traffic* is the edit a well-meaning hand makes.  **The owed row is an offset-1 store carrying `0x04` (bare RST)**, chosen because it kills the equality guard while P2&#x27;s existing `0x06` symmetrically kills any re-aim to `== 0x04`.  Formally L1 (nothing committed), but floor, not polish: it may not be filed.  *** AND A SKETCH-RULE CATCH THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A SECOND DEFECT: THE DECLINE MUST `return 1`, NEVER 0. ***  A `0` hands the access to the caller&#x27;s unassigned-address handling -- **the same unwitnessed machine-dependent behaviour (c) was rejected for shipping.**  Required record corrections: the dead warrant corrected in place (L4); an ownership row filed carrying the `#if 0` verbatim; one sentence stating (a) leaves `len` UNDECODED so nobody reads offset decode as width coverage; and the prior *&#x27;after the fix, model and hardware agree&#x27;* qualified to STORE-SIDE ONLY -- read-side, offset 1 still returns the status mirror on all four layouts, which agrees with no hardware anywhere.  Flagged its own weakest leg unprompted: the floppy-CCR-write fact is background PC knowledge corroborated by the header comment, not provable from this tree, and the silence ruling does not rest on it alone. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** SIX BINARIES BUILT AND SCORED.  TWO OF ITS FOUR RESULTS GO AGAINST ITS OWN ARTIFACT, AND IT BADGED THE SELF-REVIEW `A` UNPROMPTED. ***  Provenance checked before measuring: its fix leg is byte-identical to the committed `mkfixleg.py` output AND to the preserved `fixleg.diff`, and its table IMPORTS the witness probe rather than re-implementing it.  Six `git archive HEAD` trees in /tmp, no project build tree touched, no `.MUTANT` owed; per leg it re-read the marker off disk, checked `[ binary -nt source ]`, and confirmed no two of six binaries identical.  **BOTH UNBUILT MUTANTS DIE** -- the over-reach (`!= 0` -&gt; `!= 2`, declining BOTH offsets) is KILLED ON-ACCEPT with P1&#x27;s rows red 50-&gt;58 / ff-&gt;71, and the nulled decode (`!= 0` -&gt; `&gt; 1`, never true) is KILLED AS-DEFECT, reproducing the committed build exactly.  So the accept-row obligation and floor clause (d) are discharged BY MEASUREMENT rather than by prediction -- and codex&#x27;s predicted kill of the same `&gt; 1` mutant is now a measured one.  *** THE FINDING: m3 ESCAPED 15/15, AND IT IS BLIND BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY LUCK. ***  Guard only the destructive call -- keep `d-&gt;cur_command = COMMAND_RESET;` unguarded and wrap only `wdc_new_command_reset(d)` in `if (relative_addr == 0)`.  Every defect row reads DRQ or the input ring, **both of which are cleared by the helper**, while `cur_command` is assigned by the CALLER and never touched by it.  **It proved non-equivalence rather than asserting it**: `cur_command == COMMAND_RESET` is read at exactly two sites (the cyl_lo/cyl_hi read arms) and returns the ATAPI signature only when the drive is a CD-ROM -- the witness rig attaches `iR:`, so that channel is DEAD on the rig.  Re-run with `ciR:` and the same bytes: base 14/eb latched, fix 55/66 declined, **m3 14/eb LATCHED -- the defect present** -- with both controls green, so the fix leg&#x27;s quiet row is a REFUSAL and not a dead path.  **And the CHANNEL check would have caught it before any table ran**: the defect rows&#x27; named observable cannot vary under m3, which is knowable at row-writing time.  That is the pre-table block&#x27;s own claim, confirmed on its first mandated application.  **STRUCTURALLY: the witness has ZERO rows that READ the alt region, either offset.**  Any read-side edit escapes the table entirely.  *** Q2 REFUTED BOTH ADJUDICATIONS ON THE READ SIDE, AND CHEAPLY. ***  codex and fable5 both ruled the read half unfixable for want of an ORACLE (bit 7 high-impedance, write-gate, active-low encoding).  **No datasheet is needed, because the test is an INVARIANCE, not a value**: `status_byte()` never reads `d-&gt;head`, and a Drive Address Register is by definition a function of head select.  Measured on the committed build: read 0x3f7 with head=0 gives 51 and with head=15 gives 51 -- INVARIANT -- while the SDH readback of 0xaf proves the head write landed, and 0x3f6 is unchanged by it so the invariance is not `nothing moved`.  That refutes every candidate model of 0x3f7 without naming a single expected bit.  **So the read half is a WITNESSED defect and its own row is eligible immediately.**  Q3 measured: an unmapped access faults nothing -- write dropped, guest parks normally, 15/15 green, i.e. behaviourally equivalent to the decode fix on these rows.  But it emits one `non-existant paddr` warning PER ACCESS with no latch and no counter (the floodclass shape, guest-drivable at default verbosity), a read then returns 0x00 silently, and under `-T` it sets `running = 0` on EVERY cpu.  It named its own escape correctly as NOT coverage debt: a detector green under every candidate fix settles nothing between them, so adding rows to `fix` that would be the addition licence&#x27;s `cannot hurt`.  Disclosed one apparatus slip of its own: a Bash heredoc ate a backslash level, the anchor matched zero times and the build died at leg 1 -- loud, because the anchor asserts `count == 1`. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, MEASURING seat -- regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/mutants_20260830/ (FINDINGS.txt, TABLE_A/B/C.txt; sentinels FINDINGS_DONE, TABLE_A_DONE, TABLE_B_DONE, TABLE_C_DONE)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** FOUND THE FACT THAT REFUTED THE ADJUDICATOR, and found it from the other side of the collision. ***  Read `wdcreg.h` and saw that `wd_digin` sits inside `#if 0 /* NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs */` -- so the header defines ONE aux register, not two.  Then pinned the counterpart: `dev_fdc.c:49` is `#define DEV_FDC_LENGTH 6 /*  TODO 8, but collision with wdc  */`, with fdc registered at `isa_portbase + 0x3f0` -- **so the tree documents the same collision from BOTH devices, and the wdc&#x27;s 2-byte registration is exactly what blocks fdc from its own stated TODO of 8 ports.**  Ruled KEEP the 2-byte region THIS round and decode the write: shrinking to 1 byte makes `0x3f7` unmapped and changes device OWNERSHIP rather than repairing the measured store alias, on four platform layouts the witness covers none of.  **Named the escape mutant precisely: `relative_addr != 0` -&gt; `relative_addr &gt; 1`, which over a 2-byte region is NEVER TRUE, so the decline never fires -- P1 green (offset 0 was never declined), P3 green (offset 1 without RST never reset), P2 REOPENS.  That is the exact mutant proving P1+P3 do not pin the decode.**  Corrected my premise about the sibling row: `wdcaltwidth` is NOT &#x27;exactly this registration&#x27; -- its filed escape is transaction width (`data[0]` vs `data[len-1]`), and a 1-byte registration would close the width question BY TOPOLOGY rather than prove the topology correct.  Keep the rows distinct.  Refused to fix the read under this witness on stated grounds: bit 7 high-impedance, write-gate representation and active-low drive/head selection are decisions the witness does not cover.  Declared plainly: no gate, battery or harness script run. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered the question set and endorsed the silent-decline shape, quoting the exact diagnostic text it wanted shipped (`debug(&quot;[ wdc: write to DRIVE ADDRESS (read-only): 0x%02x, ignored ]\n&quot;, ...)`).  **Did NOT find the `#if 0`** despite being a file-reading seat with the tree available -- recorded plainly, because two of four file-reading seats missing the discriminating fact is the measurement, not a footnote. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** INDEPENDENTLY FOUND THE `#if 0`, AND OPENED BY RE-DERIVING EVERY BRIEF PREMISE INTO A TABLE OF TRUE/OVERSTATED/FALSE RATHER THAN ANSWERING THE QUESTIONS. ***  Its table is the round&#x27;s best premise audit: handler never reads `relative_addr` **True** (verified `relative_addr` appears only in `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc)`); region length 2 **True**; offset 1 = ATA Drive Address **&#x27;True as a PC port identity, OVERSTATED as a model obligation&#x27;** -- the same port is floppy DIR on read and floppy CCR on write, and the tree&#x27;s own `wdcreg.h` does not map it; ACCEPT applies **&#x27;It does not -- the model does not compute a right write result and then drop it, it performs a soft reset&#x27;**; `wdcaltwidth` can absorb this **&#x27;It cannot&#x27;**.  **Caught the vacuity that mattered: P3 is GREEN ON BOTH LEGS, so it does not kill the defect** -- it is a control, and the brief&#x27;s row table invited reading it as coverage.  Also flagged that the two unbuilt mutants are &#x27;reasoned only&#x27; and are FLOOR WORK, not optional.  Verified the witness numbers against `RESULTS.txt` and the `ALTOFF_DONE` sentinel rather than trusting the brief&#x27;s transcription. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** SUPPLIED THE ONE FACT THAT MAKES THE READ SIDE FIXABLE IN PRINCIPLE, and it was verified exactly: `dev_wdc.c` `case wd_sdh` LATCHES drive and head *** (`d-&gt;sectorsize`, `d-&gt;lba`, `d-&gt;drive`, `d-&gt;head` all set from the stored byte at :1777-1780).  So the device does hold the raw material a correct offset-1 read would need.  Read as CONTRADICTING the adjudicator&#x27;s &#x27;ACCEPT is structurally unavailable&#x27; and adjudicated as NOT contradicting it: the adjudicator ruled on the STORE (where there is no discarded right answer), kimi is describing the READ.  Different questions, both right.  It wanted the read arm decided in the same edit; codex&#x27;s refusal is better warranted (the read owes an ORACLE the tree cannot supply), so the read files separately.  Did NOT find the `#if 0`. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Did not ratify.  Its lead finding is METHODOLOGICAL and lands: **&#x27;premise 3&#x27;s option set is invoked outside its settled domain with its own required grep unreported&#x27;** -- the four-member de-fatalisation option set carries an obligation to grep the fork for the existing idiom BEFORE choosing, and the brief invoked the rule while reporting no census.  That is a real defect in my brief and it is the same shape the carrier already records (a rule cited by the person not applying it).  Also caught that the brief&#x27;s SS-E blind-spot list claims completeness while omitting non-status effects.  **Could not settle the `#if 0` -- it is a repo fact and the brief did not inline `wdcreg.h`.  That is a BRIEF defect, not a seat failure.** — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — &#x27;No fatal defect found in the inlined reasoning, but several premises are not checkable from the packet and Q1/Q3 remain open.&#x27;  **The honest form** -- it named what it could not settle instead of guessing, which is exactly what the packet preamble asks for.  Its clause-(b) reasoning about the single fixed site is sound on the scope it had. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Agreed the defect and witness are sound, and identified Q2 (read) and Q3 (width) as gaps that must be resolved before pass 2 -- which matches where the file-reading seats landed.  Named the bit definitions as the premise it could not settle from the packet.  Also noted the SSC0 ghost-round check is &#x27;exactly the guard&#x27; the previous failure needed. — evidence: [panel_20260830_004549]  wdcaltoffset pass 1, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THE HIGHEST-YIELD SEAT OF THE PASS: it went clause by clause through the vacuity floor and found exactly ONE undischarged -- (e), the CONTRACT -- then DRAFTED both. ***  Added to both probes in-round under L1, naming what each catches and, more importantly, what each is blind to by construction.  **Recovered the round&#x27;s headline artifact from /tmp before it evaporated**: the 36/36 eighteen-row run existed ONLY at `/tmp/gx494/run/RESULTS.txt`.  Nine logs are now durable under `killtable_20260830/`, each sentinel-checked before copying.  *** FOUND THE COUNTER DEFECT NO ONE ELSE COULD SEE: `grep -c &#x27; FAIL &#x27;` MATCHES THE PROBE&#x27;S OWN VERDICT LINE *** (*SOMETHING DID NOT HOLD -- read the FAIL line*), so every nonzero count was inflated by one.  True reds are m1=2, m2=4, m3=0, m4=2, cross-checked against the independent `N of 36` figure.  **The only count that was RIGHT was the zero** -- with nothing failing there is no verdict line to miscount -- so the anomalous-looking row was the correct one, which is why it hid.  TABLE.txt corrected IN PLACE rather than re-run, because silently restating history is the laundering shape.  Confirmed **both PRETABLE-prescribed rows survived the row-list rewrite -- no #490 recurrence** -- and verified m3&#x27;s mechanism in the tree rather than accepting it (0x14/0xeb is keyed on `cur_command == COMMAND_RESET &amp;&amp; diskimage_is_a_cdrom()`, and cur_command is assigned by the caller).  Caught a LABEL COLLISION: PRETABLE&#x27;s C5 uses `m4` for the width-1 experiment while TABLE.txt&#x27;s `m4` is the value guard -- same label, two mutants, one round.  Required the round record disclose that **both detectors are dated-exempt from gate wiring until 2026-09-20, so the battery defends none of this today**, and that the CHANGELOG carry a WIDTH QUALIFIER so offset decode is never read as width coverage.  Disclosed its own limit unprompted: it did not rebuild or `cmp` the /tmp binaries. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THE ONLY SEAT TO REFUSE RATIFICATION, AND IT BUILT THE REPAIR FOR BOTH THINGS IT FOUND.  Badge A on the mutants and the proposed probe; it authored NONE of the material under review. ***  **L2 FLOOR BREACH, MEASURED.**  `relative_addr != 0 &amp;&amp; !(idata &amp; WDCTL_4BIT)` **escaped BOTH detectors** -- 36/36 on the main, declined on the signature.  The reason is structural rather than bad luck: the detectors&#x27; only three offset-1 values are `0x06`, `0x02` and `0x04`, **all with bit 3 clear**, so every offset-1 row sat in ONE `WDCTL_4BIT` equivalence class.  The round defended the value axis against EQUALITY guards (`m4`) and never against MASK guards.  And it is not an exotic bit: `dev_wdc.c`&#x27;s own `#481` comment says `WDCTL_4BIT` is what libata holds as its STEADY-STATE `ap-&gt;ctl`, and asserts SRST with `0x0e`.  **A mask guard re-opens the defect at exactly the values the commonest driver writes.**  Measured at `0x0e`: base 50/ff, fix 58/71, mutant 50/ff -- defect fully restored.  **It shipped a positive control with the finding** (`n5`, a nulled decode, reds at 32/36), which is what makes the three escapes mean something rather than proving the harness inert.  **THE REPAIR, BUILT AND MEASURED: a P5/P6 phase pair at `0x0e`**, 47 of 49 predicted cells -- and the two &#x27;misses&#x27; ARE the predicted P6 red under an over-reaching decline, **so the accept row is discharged by a measured kill rather than by existing.**  Shipped in-round as a third detector rather than as extra phases, because adding rows to the main probe perturbs every mutant&#x27;s score and forces a full re-table -- the cost `#462` paid.  **L4 WRONG RECORD, and the correction is a STRONGER warrant for the same decision.**  My comment said `return 0` would route to unassigned-address handling and warn per access.  **False on the path AND on the symptom.**  A REGISTERED device returning 0 never reaches that path: `memory_rw` sets `res = -1`, raises `EXCEPTION_DBE`, returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED, and `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` has exactly one caller, AFTER the device loop.  Measured under `-v`: 19,793 `device &#x27;wdc_altstatus&#x27; ... failed` and **ZERO** `non-existant`.  At default verbosity it is **SILENT** while the guest loops forever re-executing the faulting store -- worse than a flood, not better.  It said plainly why the false reason had to go rather than just be dropped: *a maintainer who reads &#x27;only noisy warnings&#x27; may well return 0*.  It also refuted **`PRETABLE.out`&#x27;s own GUARD-FIELD `N/A`**: the block claimed the predicate reads no field the body writes, and `n3` is precisely a predicate reading `d-&gt;atapi_cmd_in_progress`, a field the block&#x27;s own body list names.  Two of its six mutants live in the space that `N/A` declared empty.  **And a coverage gap NEITHER block names: every alt-region access in all three probes is a STORE.  The handler&#x27;s READ path -- the register&#x27;s primary function -- is pinned by zero rows.**  Corrected one flat claim of mine: offset 1 is the Drive Address register only at `addr_mult == 1`; on landisk (2) and macppc (0x10) it is a byte lane, because the alt region is hardcoded two bytes wide and never divides by `addr_mult` the way `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc)` does.  The behaviour is still correct there; the claim was not.  Ruled out a `&amp;&amp; len == 1` mutant BY READING rather than by building: `memory_rw` truncates a len-2 access at offset 1 to len 1, so it would be equivalent.  **Disclosed its own limits unprompted**: its `n2` result rests on a table whose run its OWN guard aborted when the main loop edited `dev_wdc.c` mid-table (preserved as `wave1_TABLE_P2.txt`, ending *clone is NOT the named baseline*); the landisk/macppc defect claim is a READING with no rig; and it had frozen the instruments but not the baseline -- **the same lesson one level up** -- which it fixed from wave 2 onward. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2, MEASURING seat -- six mutants built; artifacts at regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/opus_p2_20260830/ (wave2_TABLE_W2.txt TABLE_W2_DONE, wave3_CHANNEL_TABLE_4BIT.txt ALTOFF_4BIT_DONE, q2_mechanism_result.txt Q2MECH_DONE)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered at length, verifying the diff against the sources rather than against the brief.  Its output streams reasoning, so the answer sits at the end.  Recorded as answering; its substantive findings overlap grok&#x27;s and glm&#x27;s on the citation and the row counts. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Endorsed the shipped shape and reasoned about the floppy-controller arbitration as sound engineering rather than as a defect.  Landed its whole 9.4 KB answer in one write after sitting at exactly 0 bytes -- **the normal mid-run state for this seat**, and the reason the size tell cannot be used on it. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** CAUGHT MY WRONG CITATION MID-ANSWER, FROM THE LOG ITSELF, AND IT WAS THE ROUND&#x27;S HEADLINE NUMBER. ***  Its 629-byte partial output read: *the shipped results file is 15 rows (30/30), not the brief&#x27;s 18x2.*  It is right.  `RESULTS_shipped.txt` is the 15-row WITNESS; the 18-row detector run happened in an ad-hoc invocation that was never preserved, and the pass-2 brief attached 36/36 to a file holding 30/30.  **The claim did not become unverifiable; it became CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN EVIDENCE**, which is the immutable-artifact rule arriving from the direction it warns about.  *** AND IT WAS NEARLY SCORED A DEAD SEAT. ***  It sat at 629 bytes across three samples while every other seat grew -- the stall signature exactly.  It was ALIVE (grok.exe pid 3180) and reading files; its output only carries streamed prose.  It finished at 11.2 KB.  *A short answer and a dead seat are not the same thing, and the process check is what separates them.*  Pulling its thread found a SECOND preservation defect it had not named: `kill494.sh` copied four `main.out` files to ONE name, destroying three runs and leaving a survivor that identified no mutant. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Verified every premise against the tree before answering -- the handler, the 2-byte registration, `debug()`/`fatal()` bodies, `status_byte()` (confirming it does NOT read `d-&gt;head`, the Q5 invariance), `wdc_new_command_reset`, and the fdc registration address.  **Added a platform-coverage point nobody else had**: the alt registration takes NO `addr_mult` while the main register file does, so on macppc (registers spaced 16 bytes) the byte at alt+1 corresponds to no register at all -- a hole.  Declining there is safe on that layout for a reason independent of the PC argument.  Worked the PC register map explicitly and landed where the tree does: 0x3f7 write is the floppy&#x27;s configuration-control register, 0x3f6 is IDE Device Control, and the IDE Drive Address register at 0x3f7 is READ-ONLY.  Enumerated four further mutant shapes and reasoned each to an existing row. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THREE FINDINGS, ALL REAL, FROM THE PACKET ALONE -- AND ONE IS A WRONG RECORD IN SHIPPED PRODUCT CODE. ***  **(b)** the committed comment said the partial guard escaped *a fifteen-row table*, while the detector is 18 rows and m3 passes all 18.  Fifteen was true when the measuring seat FOUND m3 and stale by the time it shipped -- L4, corrected in both twin trees, and rewritten to name its artifact rather than any literal, because that sentence has now been wrong once already.  **(a)** it DERIVED that the m1 `sig 0` cell cannot be right if L2 ran on the mutant leg: m1&#x27;s `!= 2` is always true over `relative_addr` in {0,1}, so it declines the LEGAL 0x3f6 poke too, and L2 must then read 55/66 rather than 14/eb.  Correct, and it named the alternative itself -- **the sig column counts only the D row**, so mutants breaking the legal path are killed by main accept rows and are invisible to signature L2.  A real harness limit, stated aloud rather than hidden.  **(c)** premise 7&#x27;s warrant assumes fdc must claim its eight ports CONTIGUOUSLY, which the packet never states -- and it showed the conclusion survives on a different warrant (the measured return-0 cost), so *the conclusion stands; the stated warrant is not what carries it*.  It also named precisely what it could not settle: the two literal quotes, whether port registration must be contiguous, and what the sig column counts.  **That is the honest packet-fed form, and three of its four findings needed no repo access at all.** — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Short and correctly scoped: the write-side decline passes, the read-side deferral is right, and **the silence justification is NOT checkable from the packet** -- it rests on the census and on premise 6&#x27;s two quotes, neither of which a packet-fed seat can verify.  It flagged that a bounded-announce may still be owed rather than ratifying silence.  The honest form: it named the premise it could not settle instead of guessing, which is what the packet preamble asks for. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Named two mutants that pass BOTH detectors, and was honest about what they are worth.**  `m6` guards the inner `if` with `relative_addr == 0` instead of adding the arm; `m9` conditions the decline on the reset bit.  Both still refuse the destructive action, so the guest-visible defect stays closed -- what they revert is the CORRECTED DIAGNOSTIC TEXT, leaving offset-1 stores logged as ALT. CTRL.  It said so plainly: *neither of these is guest-visible; both are developer-experience defects*, and named the row that would catch them (a detector grepping the debug string against the access address).  **So this is COVERAGE DEBT above the floor, not a clause-(d) breach** -- the distinction the floor turns on, drawn correctly by a packet-fed seat.  Recorded in the shipped contract as a stated blindness rather than fixed. — evidence: [panel_20260830_090359]  #494 pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** CLOSED ON THE STORE HALF.  THE READ HALF AND THE WIDTH ARE NOT FIXED AND ARE NOT CLAIMED. ***  Saying which half closed is the whole point of the round&#x27;s split: a bare closure reads as *0x3f7 is done*, which is the queue-id-covering-two-defects shape -- and this session spent a correction unpicking exactly that on a sibling row whose commit message merged two ids in one sentence.  **FIXED:** the handler decodes `relative_addr` and declines the offset-1 store, declining the WHOLE reset action rather than only the helper call.  That distinction is the round&#x27;s finding and a mutant proved it: guarding only the helper leaves `d-&gt;cur_command` clobbered, passes all 18 rows at 36/36, and dies only to a purpose-built signature probe on a CD-ROM rig.  **NOT FIXED, each with its own row:** the READ of offset 1 still returns alt-status on all four platform layouts, which matches no hardware anywhere -- `wdcaltdigin`, where the DEFECT is witnessed by an invariance but the FIX has no oracle.  `len` is still undecoded, so a `len == 2` store at offset 0 passes the decode with its 0x3f7 byte silently consumed -- `wdcaltwidth`, open on its own terms, **0 of 3 on the overtaken conditions**.  And who owns the byte at all -- `wdcaltownership`.  **THREE DETECTORS SHIPPED, ALL DATED-EXEMPT FROM GATE WIRING UNTIL 2026-09-20** -- two-leg differentials, and no gate builds a second leg.  **The battery defends none of this today; the kill table is the evidence.**  The third exists because pass 2 measured a floor breach in the first two: their only offset-1 values all had `WDCTL_4BIT` clear, so one mask conjunct escaped both at 36/36 -- at a bit libata holds set at all times. — evidence: c125450 -- #494, with its pass-2 receipt at regress/pass2_ledger.tsv (9of9)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">novpadfourth</span><span class="ttl">the no-VPATH rule has FOUR build trees and precommit section B enforces only three -- the WSL arc tree is invisible to it</span><span class="tip">the no-VPATH rule has FOUR build trees and precommit section B enforces only three -- the WSL arc tree is invisible to it — witness: harness:three-state negative control on the SHIPPED lines -- green in sync, RED on a one-line divergence, SKIP when absent; preserved at regress/artifacts/wdcatapistswap/killtable_20260830/CONTROL_novpadfourth.out</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcatapistswap</span><span class="ttl">the #495 guard&#x27;s flag operand can be swapped for `atapi_st != NULL` and the detector cannot see it -- measured to refuse every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device</span><span class="tip">the #495 guard&#x27;s flag operand can be swapped for `atapi_st != NULL` and the detector cannot see it -- measured to refuse every ordinary data-port write for the life of the device — commits: e61ccd8 af62a06 — witness: harness:BOTH HALVES MEASURED IN THIS ROUND, not transcribed -- the COMMITTED probe cannot see the swap (LEG 0: 22/22 ESCAPED) and the EXTENDED probe kills it (E2: 26/30, 4 red).  Logs at regress/artifacts/wdcatapistswap/killtable_20260830/ (TABLE.txt, kill_L0_committed_probe.out, kill_E2.out).  *** REPLACED, not appended: the previous field read &#x27;probe:measured by the pass-2 measuring seat --...&#x27; -- a `probe:` prefix that check_witness.py scores as RUNG 3, which that file&#x27;s own header calls laundering for a round whose subject is the test apparatus; and its 20/20 figure disagreed with its own note (&quot;all 22 rows&quot;) while the artifact it cited no longer existed.  Named by the fable5 seat.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Went clause by clause and ruled the arm&#x27;s channel VALID, `harness:` the right witness term, and the design sound -- then named what was still owed.  **Its headline is an evidence hole the round was about to build on: the E2 escape figure&#x27;s cited artifact (`opus495/wave2/run_q3.out`) NO LONGER EXISTS**, and the row disagrees with itself -- witness field `20/20`, note `all 22 rows`.  A number that disagrees with itself and whose evidence is gone is a transcription, not a measurement.  **The table now re-measures it as LEG 0** (22/22 ESCAPED on the committed probe), so the escape is a result of this round.  It also caught: clause (a) CANNOT govern an accept row (green on both legs by definition) -- the governing rule is ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY, discharged by a MEASURED kill, and claiming clause (a) would have been an unfalsifiable L2 claim; the probe&#x27;s clause-(e) CONTRACT did not name this family; the header sentence *&#x27;THE PROGRAM AND THE ROWS BELOW ARE UNTOUCHED ... measured 20 of 20&#x27;* was ALREADY FALSE when committed, because #495 added the J/K/L arm in the same round; the wrong post-packet narrative had an uncorrected SIBLING in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md whose REMEDY line prescribed the VACUOUS form of this very row; `kill495.sh`&#x27;s `WORDS_OK` is computed and referenced nowhere while the script claims the encodings agree; and PRETABLE item (ii) as first worded LICENSED EDITING #495&#x27;s true records.  It derived two escapes (`wdcwidth4`, `wdcringresetblind`) and named a per-row kill the block had missed: an empty-ring gate passes the FIRST accept row and fails only the SECOND, so the two are not redundant.  It advised splitting the unrelated gate_hygiene edit into its own commit -- done, `e3cf79f`. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** RAN THE WHOLE TABLE ON REAL BINARIES: nine mutants, scratch clones only, base-leg apparatus check on all fourteen runs. ***  Confirmed ALL EIGHT of the brief&#x27;s predictions, M7 in its grok-corrected form, with red-counts matching the PRETABLE&#x27;s arithmetic at every mutant.  *** THEN FOUND E4 -- `&amp;&amp; len != 4` -- SWEEPING 30/30 WHILE RESTORING THE DEFECT BYTE-FOR-BYTE. ***  Rows G/H/I return the injected a5/5a/00, identical to the PRE-FIX build, against the fix&#x27;s real disk content c2/c3/c4: the guest is handed its own bytes AS DISK DATA and the detector scores clean.  **It DISSENTED on disposition and the dissent is correct**: `if (X)` -&gt; `if (X &amp;&amp; Y)` on the predicate #495 ADDED, which DOMINATES the edited lines, is clause (d)&#x27;s canonical form, and #488&#x27;s adjudication names that exact shape a floor breach.  Filed as coverage debt by an earlier seat; RE-DISPOSITIONED to L2 and repaired in-round.  It derived the CLOSURE CONDITION rather than guessing: `&amp;&amp; P(len)` escapes iff P is true on every INJECTED width and false on some IMPLEMENTED width, so only {1,2,4} closes the family.  Repair is ONE instruction and NO new row -- a 4-byte `sw` -- and it kills E4, M3 and M6 together.  *** AND THE PROBE HAD NO 4-BYTE EMITTER AT ALL: the device&#x27;s `case 4:` arm and its whole `consumed` accounting had never executed on any leg of any run this detector ever made. ***  Not an uncovered mutant -- an uncovered ARM.  E5 (`cur_command == ATAPI_PKT_CMD`) also sweeps 30/30 and the N arm does NOT defeat it: it REPLACES the flag operand rather than exempting an opcode, so both vehicles leave it green.  Filed as `wdcflagswap2`, ranked below E4 because its harm is over-refusal and transient.  It also narrowed what the arm buys: rows B and M differ in ONE readable dimension, so E2 is their only UNIQUE kill and *&#x27;the accept side is pinned&#x27; is measurably false*.  Confirmed the `WORDS_OK` dead check independently, caught a wrong record in kill495_M7.sh, and flagged this loop&#x27;s own killswap.sh appearing while its table built. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered at length against the sources.  Its output is dominated by streamed reasoning over the brief and the tree, as this seat&#x27;s runs usually are. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Verified the premises against the tree and named the mechanical gap that would have made the arm fail loudly on its first run: the brief&#x27;s snippet uses `WDCC_RECAL`, `t7` and `v0`, **none of which existed in the probe**, and `REGS` -- which drives the readback loop -- was not extended, so a row naming an absent register reads `??` and fails.  All three adopted; verified after editing that REGS holds 15 names and every row register is present. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Started from the repo rather than the brief.  **Refuted one of the eight predictions before the table ran, which is exactly what writing them down is for**: M7 replaces the refusal body with `return 0;`, and the guard FIRES at row E during DATA-IN, so the mutant guest takes EXCEPTION_DBE and never parks -- *the accept arms are UNREACHED, not inert*.  Predicted 15/30 with every register `??`; measured 15/30, 15 red.  A prediction about a row&#x27;s VALUE presumes the row RUNS.  **And it found the one-conjunct exemption that produced the N arm**: with a single vehicle, `&amp;&amp; d-&gt;cur_command != WDCC_RECAL` re-opens the hole and sweeps every row, because after RECAL `cur_command` is 0x10 while during DATA-IN it is still ATAPI_PKT_CMD.  Its remedy -- issue TWO different no-fill commands, since one conjunct cannot exempt two opcodes -- is the M5/M6 lesson on a new axis (WHICH no-fill opcode cleared the flag) and was adopted in-round as the SOFT_RESET arm.  It also caught that REGS was not updated, and that recasting `/22` to `/26` is not a licence to skip the table because the instruction stream and CONTROL 0 counts change.  Looked like a stall at 616 bytes across two samples; the PROCESS was alive and it finished at 11 KB. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered from the CLI -- the seat restored by owner directive on 2026-08-29, so this is a FILE-READING answer rather than the packet-fed substitute.  Worked the write-arm path and the width cases directly, reasoning about a `len != 4` shaped guard in the same region opus5 later measured as the floor breach. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN MECHANISM FROM THE INLINED FACTS ALONE, and it is the finding that saved the row from being vacuous. ***  The brief said the measured hole is a post-packet push/pop with NO COMMAND IN BETWEEN.  It objected that this requires some completion path to clear `atapi_cmd_in_progress`, and named the absence of one as a hole in the narrative.  Checked against the tree and it is right: the flag has exactly TWO write sites and no completion path clears it, so post-transfer the SHIPPED guard refuses too, both legs read ff, and the naive form of this row would have been GREEN ON BOTH LEGS -- shipping as coverage while pinning nothing.  **The clearing command is load-bearing, not scene-setting.**  It also refused to let a register question pass: the draft arm wrote `t3` three times while the brief listed `t3` as live, and it could not tell from a packet whether `t3` is read back.  It is not in REGS, so the draft was safe -- and the sidestep (do the stores through the arm&#x27;s own output registers) costs the same eight instructions.  Adopted.  **A false concrete claim with a correct recommendation, and the brief&#x27;s sloppy word `live` is what produced it.** — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Argued the row is too weak: RECAL leaves `phase == 0` whereas *the real hole* would leave PHASE_COMPLETED, so a phase-keyed mutant would tell them apart -- and named `atapi_phase == PHASE_COMPLETED` as a one-token escape of all 26 rows.  **REFUTED TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY.**  (1) By census: `wdc_new_command_reset()` is the only writer of `flag = 0` and zeroes the phase in the same three lines, and all five nonzero-phase sites lie on paths where the flag is 1 -- so **flag == 0 IMPLIES phase == 0** and the state it needs does not exist.  (2) By an EXISTING MEASUREMENT: that exact mutant is #495&#x27;s M2, already built and scored 18/22 KILLED (4 red) in the shipped artifacts.  Its companion claim -- that `atapi_st` might not be set when the arm runs -- is wrong on the tree; `atapi_st` is allocated by the packet dispatch long before.  **But its wording critique landed**: &#x27;self-normalising&#x27; covers the RING and not `atapi_st`, and the block now says so.  The invariant it forced out is now the rows&#x27; non-vacuity warrant. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Challenged the brief for inlining the switch without the rest of `wdc_command()`, and was right.**  The function has TWO early returns BEFORE its switch -- a no-disk-image ABRT and a CDROM+WDCC_IDENTIFY ABRT.  This probe attaches a CD image, so IDENTIFY -- the natural choice of clearing command -- would never have reached its case.  RECAL is unaffected and both returns sit AFTER the reset so the flag clears on every path, but the brief had inlined an incomplete function, which is the failure the full-enclosing-function rule names.  Its reasoning is now the recorded warrant for the vehicle choice.  It also flagged the E2 arithmetic as a typo; settled by READING `kill495.sh` -- the mutant replaces the FIX leg only, so 24/26 was right. — evidence: [panel_20260830_124335]  wdcatapistswap pass 1 on the design brief, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Went clause by clause and ruled the measurement discipline *the best this queue has produced* -- then found **clause (e) BREACHED as shipped**, with five tells of one cause: the probe&#x27;s contract was written at the 13-row stage and the round grew past it.  `rows M` in a file with M AND N arms; a revision history stopping at 13 rows/26; the BLIND-TO block still calling a repaired escape live; a dangling *&#x27;two further accept-side mutants -- see BLIND-TO&#x27;* pointer whose referents were one repaired and one absent; and **E5 disclosed nowhere in the instrument**.  All corrected.  *** ITS SHARPEST FINDING WAS THE ONE UNMEASURED CLAIM: the empty-ring mutant was never built. ***  The claim that each pair&#x27;s SECOND row is non-redundant rested on a prediction -- and unlike E2/E5 that mutant sits INSIDE clause (d)&#x27;s enumeration.  Built as E6: **26/30, and exactly the predicted asymmetry** -- first row green, second red, in both arms.  It also ruled that **clause (a) cannot govern an accept row** (green on both product legs by definition) and that ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY is the governing rule, so the record must say *discharged by a measured kill*, never *clause (a) satisfied* -- as a floor claim the latter would be unfalsifiable and L2-enforceable.  PRETABLE audit against the `#490` precedent: four of six carry-forward items shipped, one was moot, and **the anti-`#490` clause itself says &#x27;diffed against these four&#x27; over a list of SIX**.  It ruled the file must NOT be edited to fix that -- its mtime is the round&#x27;s order proof -- so the erratum lives in the round block. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** RAN NINE MUTANTS ON REAL BINARIES AND RATIFIED THE E4 REPAIR BY MEASUREMENT, then found four surviving wrong records and an operational emergency. ***  It reproduced E4 at 29/30 exactly, and added the negative control the round had not built: **X3 (`&amp;&amp; len != 3`) ESCAPES 30/30, which is the CORRECT result** and turns the closure argument&#x27;s last unmeasured premise -- that `case 3` is inert -- into a measurement.  It could not construct a pure-width escape and said so.  *** IT CAUGHT MY OWN EVIDENCE TABLE BEING FALSE, by opening `kill_E4.out`. ***  The `a5/5a/00` bytes came from a probe whose DATA-IN injection IT had widened to 4 bytes to demonstrate the hazard -- not from E4 against the shipped instrument, where G/H/I stay green and the kill lands on ROW L.  Same conclusion, different row.  **And it proved the E5 disposition rather than arguing it**: `cur_command` has two write sites, each immediately followed by the reset whose only flag-SET is inside the packet case, so E5&#x27;s guard is TRUE wherever the shipped guard is -- a strictly wider refusal that cannot restore the defect.  Therefore filing is REQUIRED BY RULE, no opcode vehicle can ever catch it, and a third vehicle would be row growth with a provably zero kill.  It named the thing that CAN catch it: an ARM, not a vehicle.  Three more L4s: the probe claimed the `sw` exercises `consumed = len == 4 ? ...` (impossible -- that lives in the MEM_READ arm and no store reaches it; the probe has no 4-byte LOAD emitter at all); `kill495_M6.sh` DOES carry M5&#x27;s rationale wholesale, so my narrowing went a step too far; and `wdcringresetblind`&#x27;s escape figure was invalidated by this round&#x27;s own repair and never re-run.  *** AND IT STOPPED ITS OWN TABLE TO YIELD THE MACHINE, then reported the collision from the other side. ***  It saw this loop&#x27;s second kill table start while its own was building, killed its run by explicit PID rather than `pkill -f`, and verified in a separate invocation.  It also self-caught an apparatus error of its own: a busy-wait loop that would have spun a core against the build it was waiting for. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered at length against the sources; output dominated by streamed reasoning, as this seat&#x27;s runs usually are. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Answered on the tree and ratified the diff. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Read the tree and the artifacts rather than the packet, and **refuted the round&#x27;s central evidence table by opening `kill_E4.out`** -- independently of opus5 and before it.  Also caught a census error: the brief and PRETABLE say FIVE nonzero-phase write sites; **there are SEVEN**.  My grep pattern required a non-`=` character AFTER the `=`, so it silently skipped the two assignments SPLIT ACROSS LINES.  It verified the invariant is untouched -- both missed sites sit behind the flag -- so only the count was wrong.  On the width family it dissented usefully: `&amp;&amp; len != 8`, `&amp;&amp; len != 3` and `&amp;&amp; len &lt;= 4` all leave every row green, so the closure sentence as written was too strong.  **Both it and glm were partly right** -- those survivors are behaviourally EQUIVALENT mutants, because 3 and 8 push nothing.  The sentence conflated *escapes the detector* with *escapes and re-opens the defect*, and now carries two clauses.  It ratified the N arm as worth its rows and ruled a third vehicle the forbidden growth.  It also found a second CALLER of the reset helper (`WDCTL_RST` via alt-ctrl), which matters for E5 and not for the vehicles. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **408 KB of genuine working, and it reached a strong claim that CONTRADICTED opus5** -- that the swap is UN-CATCHABLE by any accept row, because `flag == 1` and `cur_command == ATAPI_PKT_CMD` are equivalent device-wide.  **Settled against it by reading four lines**: `cur_command = idata` runs before the reset, which runs before the no-disk-image RETURN, and the flag is set again only in the packet case AFTER that return -- so `cur_command == 0xa0 &amp;&amp; flag == 0` is reachable and the equivalence fails.  Its proposed control-SRST vehicle is separately refuted: that arm writes `cur_command = COMMAND_RESET`, leaving nothing stale.  *** THE SEAT BEHAVIOUR IS THE PART TO KEEP.  It marked its own claim unsettled -- &#x27;MUST verify from source ... pending reading 730-745, 822-870, 945-960&#x27; -- and named the exact ranges that decide it.  Those are the ranges that decided it. ***  A seat that cannot check a premise and says which check would settle it is worth more than one that guesses, even when its conclusion is wrong.  It independently re-derived every leg&#x27;s red rows from source semantics and matched them to the kill logs, and its closing line named the round&#x27;s one genuine outstanding item at the time: E6&#x27;s score, since measured. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, READ seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** SEAT DOWN ON THE FIRST FIRING: A THINKING BURN. ***  `&quot;response&quot;: &quot;&quot;` with **160 KB** in the hidden `thinking` field, `done_reason: stop`, ZERO occurrences of VERDICT anywhere in it, ending mid-sentence.  Not recoverable from the thinking -- checked, because the extractor&#x27;s own note records that an empty response with `done_reason: stop` SOMETIMES holds a complete answer.  **A size check would have scored it the largest answer in the panel.**  **A BRIEF DEFECT, NOT AN OUTAGE**, per the documented mitigation: the pass-2 brief carried three questions plus 19 KB of inlined diff, and the fifth failure class tracks brief COMPLEXITY.  Re-fired with ONE question, its facts inlined, capped at ~400 words -- **it answered in 2.5 KB with a real verdict**: the width family IS closed for pure `P(len)`, the survivors are behaviourally equivalent mutants, and the weakest point is that *&#x27;3 and 8 push NOTHING&#x27; is a comment, not code*.  That answer is half of the two-clause correction the closure sentence now carries. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — **Found a live wrong record in the shipped diff from the packet alone**: the probe&#x27;s BLIND-TO block still called the inequality width escape *&#x27;Filed as `wdcwidth4`&#x27;* while the repair for it sat in the same diff.  A disposition written before a measurement, outliving the measurement.  Corrected.  Its verdict named the contradiction precisely -- *&#x27;the packet is internally inconsistent on E4/wdcwidth4&#x27;* -- which is exactly what a packet-fed seat can do that a file-reading one may not: it had only the packet, so an internal contradiction was the one thing it could see. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Named four §2 premises it could not settle from the packet and said so per-premise with the exact grep that would settle each -- the honest packet-fed form.  **Its standing-license catch was valid and specific**: the new precommit comment asserted the no-VPATH rule *&#x27;has always named FOUR build trees&#x27;*, an undated empirical claim about a RULE&#x27;S HISTORY sitting in a tool comment -- and false, since `build-asan/` joined by owner ruling three days earlier, per the section thirty lines above it.  Corrected, and its generalisation adopted: **claims about prior state either cite the rule&#x27;s text and date, or are DATED**, in tool comments exactly as in probe comments.  Its Q1 objection rested on a misreading (it took #495&#x27;s added predicate to be a width check; it is the flag/phase pair), but the deeper question it posed -- whether the closure was derivable at #495 time, making the breach #495&#x27;s -- is the right one and the packet does not settle it. — evidence: [panel_20260830_134959]  wdcatapistswap pass 2 on the real diff, PACKET seat</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** PASS: the regression material is fit to live in the harness permanently.  Six findings, ALL non-blocking, TWO of them defects nobody had written down. ***  **Q1 maintainability -- YES.**  It called the probe the best-documented instrument in the tree and enumerated why: a clause-(e) CONTRACT with its measured figures kept as DATED 10-row-era numbers, a BLIND-TO list whose every entry names its filed ledger id, a 10-&gt;11-&gt;15 revision history with per-round attribution, per-row provenance (row L&#x27;s want is honestly *&#x27;RECORDED FROM THE FIRST RUN, not predicted&#x27;*), and the flag-implies-phase invariant carried as the M rows&#x27; non-vacuity warrant.  Both PRETABLE obligations aimed at the probe are discharged IN the file.  **Q2 artifacts -- YES, with the one real mechanism defect.**  Everything resolves: 13 logs sentinelled, PRETABLE&#x27;s mtime (13:19) precedes the table (13:46), the CHANGELOG table matches TABLE.txt leg-for-leg, and **E2&#x27;s four reds are exactly the M+N rows with B GREEN -- so the &#x27;only UNIQUE kill&#x27; claim is in the log rather than only in the prose**.  *** FINDING 2, AND IT IS THE ROUND&#x27;S OWN LESSON APPLIED TO HALF ITS OWN MECHANISM: `killswap.sh` tagged the TABLE but not the KILL LOGS. ***  So the E6 follow-up overwrote the twelve-leg table&#x27;s `kill_L0_committed_probe.out` -- and by the time this was acted on, the X4 run had overwritten it AGAIN.  **The seat&#x27;s own report went stale about it within the hour**, which is the sharpest possible evidence that the mechanism was actively destructive rather than a one-off.  The original survived only because it had been COMMITTED and was recovered from the git blob -- the second such recovery in this one round, after `shipped.diff`.  Fixed: logs and LEG 0 are tagged, backward-compatibly.  **FINDING 1:** the probe&#x27;s LEG-1 banner asserted *&#x27;source-identical to GXEMUL-SEC at HEAD c125450&#x27;* -- a frozen claim about an INPUT the probe cannot see, and FALSE in all thirteen of this round&#x27;s own logs, whose base leg was pinned elsewhere.  Immaterial behaviourally (comment-only delta) and it is this file&#x27;s own header lesson one screen below the paragraph that teaches it.  Now prints the binary it was handed.  **FINDINGS 3-4:** the CHANGELOG&#x27;s M6 bullet said *&#x27;checked, and M6 is correct&#x27;* **while the same commit edited M6.sh by thirty lines** -- true of the mutant line, false of the rationale paragraph, and contradicting its own diff.  And the errata section covered two of PRETABLE&#x27;s errors but not a third (block 8 repeats the over-broad M6 phrasing).  Both corrected; PRETABLE left mtime-frozen as required.  **Q3 gate-wiring exemption -- still right, and it named planning nobody had written down: FOUR wdc probes share the single-leg-mode debt and the SAME 2026-09-20 expiry.**  That is ONE design round covering four instruments, not four rounds, and it should be scheduled before the cluster expires together.  **Q4 did the harness get worse -- NO, better, with controls.**  It verified the fourth-tree check skips rather than fails on a sleeping VM, that the hookctl runner is fail-capable in both directions, that the hook shims make divergence IMPOSSIBLE rather than policed (it `cmp`&#x27;d both pairs), and that the M6/M7 corrections are proper in-place L4s naming their finders.  **Q5 owed and unwritten:** the untagged copy-back (fixed), the stale banner (fixed), the M6 qualifier and the missing PRETABLE erratum (fixed), eight untracked files inside the repo (relocated -- it checked programmatically that zero ledger rows cite them), and the four-probe design round (filed above). — evidence: solo fable-only regress review by standing directive; verified artifact integrity, citation resolution, sentinels, mtime order and every cross-record claim reachable by reading and diffing.  It did NOT re-execute a table leg, and said so -- the preserved logs are what this phase grades</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wdcwidth4</span><span class="ttl">an INEQUALITY width qualifier swept the whole detector -- CLOSED by injecting a third access width, so the width family is closed on both sides</span><span class="tip">an INEQUALITY width qualifier swept the whole detector -- CLOSED by injecting a third access width, so the width family is closed on both sides — witness: harness:MEASURED both ways -- swept 30/30 before the repair; after it, E4 scores 29/30 with the kill on row L (want 50 got 58, the 4-byte store admitted).  Log: regress/artifacts/wdcatapistswap/killtable_20260830/kill_E4.out</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">widthkillclass</span><span class="ttl">a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host -- #337 fixed this class in dev_wdc.c and 16 sites in two other devices were never swept</span><span class="tip">a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host -- #337 fixed this class in dev_wdc.c and 16 sites in two other devices were never swept — commits: 821eb83 — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/opus_p1_20260830/read_probe.out -- 26/26 across ALL SIX sites and BOTH directions on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`, with a LIVENESS and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control.  *The earlier witness field cited a 10/10 single-site artifact, which under-cited the round&#x27;s own strongest evidence -- caught by the pass-2 seat.*  The shipped detector is regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py, 35/35, gate-run. — note: *** THE FIX SHIPPED AS `821eb83` ON 2026-08-30, AND THIS FIELD SAID `open` WITH A state_note OPENING &#x27;OPEN, and the committed witness is exactly why it can stay open&#x27; UNTIL THE PASS-2 SEAT CAUGHT IT. ***  That is the `es438` shape the carrier quotes verbatim: **a future reader who trusts states reads a shipped, witnessed, measured round as &#x27;not done&#x27;.**  Now `in work` with its commit named -- not `closed`, because the round still owes its CHANGELOG block and carries filed coverage debt.  
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\stagetokens\pass2_20260918\panel_20260918_103810\codex.txt:927:tools/pipeline\matrix.html:18943:OPEN, and the committed witness is exactly why it can stay open honestly rather than vaguely.  NO FIX HAS SHIPPED: `sgi_re_width_witness.py` proves the defect and defends nothing.  What remains, in order:  (1) a FIX ROUND on ONE sgi_re width site -- eligible now, since the fix SHAPE is already settled by `#337`&#x27;s precedent (latched decline, `(once)` marker), so it is an application of a decided design rather than a fresh design argument;  (2) a rung-3 witness for the pcc2 half, which has NONE -- a read-only seat specified one precisely (`-e mvme187`, CODE 0x10000, r5 = 0xfff42000, one process per fatal row) and said plainly it did not run it;  (3) the class total re-derived if either half moves: 11 today (sgi_re 6 witnessed, pcc2 5 after the M88K alignment narrowing), down from the 16 this row was filed with.  **The witness being committed changes the row&#x27;s EVIDENCE, not its STATE** -- and the check that caught the ambiguity is right to insist the difference be written down: a row whose detector is in the tree is not unstarted, and a reader seeing `open` with a committed artifact deserves to know which half is done.  *** PASS 1 IS DONE AND THE DESIGN IS SETTLED.  Eight seats answered (agy quota-walled); panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748`.  THE IMPLEMENTER SHOULD READ THIS BEFORE THE BRIEF -- the brief has three known defects the panel repaired. ***  **1. `break` IS NOT LEGAL C AT THESE SITES, and EIGHT OF EIGHT ANSWERING SEATS SAID SO.**  The guards sit in `if`/`else if` arms, not a `switch`, so `break` exits the nearest enclosing loop or switch -- there is none.  The correct edit is an `else if` SPLICE: the decline arm ends and control falls to the common tail.  One structural token changes per site (`if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` becomes `} else if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {`).  *Unanimity on a defect in the brief is the premise-audit instruction working: the seats were told not to ratify, and none did.*  **2. `return 1`, NOT `return 0` -- and two seats CHECKED it rather than reasoning.**  `memory_rw.c` coerces a 0 return to -1 and the `res &lt;= 0 &amp;&amp; !no_exceptions` arm raises `mips_cpu_exception(..., EXCEPTION_DBE, ...)`.  So `return 0` is a guest-visible DATA BUS ERROR, not a decline -- a different design with its own warrant and its own row, and very likely a guest panic on IP32.  **Nobody had checked this in the entire history of the sketch rule that demands it.**  *** 3. THE COMMON TAIL IS DEAD CODE TODAY AND THIS FIX MAKES IT LIVE.  This is a BEHAVIOUR CHANGE the round record must state. ***  `odata` is never assigned anywhere in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` -- it appears exactly twice, at its declaration and at the tail -- and every read path in the function currently `exit(1)`s.  So `memory_writemax64()` at the tail is UNREACHABLE in the shipped tree.  After the fix a declined READ falls through and executes it for the first time, handing the guest a defined 0 instead of an untouched buffer.  That is the right outcome, and **the `else if` form gets it for free while an early `return 1` would not** -- which is precisely the incident the sketch rule was minted from.  **4. THE WITNESS OBLIGATION IS DISCHARGED: 26/26, ALL SIX SITES, BOTH DIRECTIONS**, controls green, sentinel present, artifacts at `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/opus_p1_20260830/`.  Two seats had split on whether six byte-identical sites are ONE round or SIX; they converged on the operative condition -- extend the witness first -- and then one seat did it.  **Settled by measurement rather than by argument, which is this project&#x27;s rule for splits.**  With all six witnessed the joining clause is satisfied and ONE round is licensed.  *** 5. TWO L2 ESCAPES ARE ALREADY MEASURED AGAINST THE ROW SET THE BRIEF PROPOSED.  Hand these to the measuring seat as its FIRST assignment, not its ask-again. ***  **M1 -- `len != 8 &amp;&amp; writeflag == MEM_WRITE`.**  Every STORE row stays byte-identically green while ALL SIX READ SITES DIE.  The witness probes stores only, so `writeflag` is a dimension no probe value varies -- the same shape as `#496`&#x27;s high-byte escape, on a different axis.  Worse, the attribution row reads 0 because the guest then dies at the DIRECTION guard, so even a message-grep row would not attribute the death to this class.  **The missing row is one line: `lw` at each window, post-fix `exit0`** (measured `exit1` on the committed build at all six).  **M2 -- a latch-bit collision.**  Invisible to EVERY one-access-per-session row, because each session forks a fresh emulator with `d-&gt;warned` at 0.  Only a TWO-SITES-IN-ONE-SESSION row sees it.  Six single-site rounds would structurally guarantee this blind spot, which is a MEASURABLE argument against splitting.  **M3 (structural, not built) -- the witness&#x27;s attribution rows use `&quot;...&quot; in t`, a SUBSTRING-PRESENCE test.  Deleting the latch entirely passes them.**  That is `#265`&#x27;s flood which `#269` had to undo.  Rows must COUNT (`t.count(...) == 1`), not test presence.  **6. PER-SITE LATCHES, with an in-tree warrant rather than a preference.**  `dev_wdc.c`&#x27;s own comment adjudicates the discriminator: *SIX FLAGS, NOT FOUR* -- sharing is licensed only across sites MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME (`#337`&#x27;s shared flag spans two arms of one endianness branch).  The six sgi_re windows are walked in one session by any driver bringing up the RE, so they are not mutually exclusive.  A shared bit reports one window and hides five.  **7. THE FIX&#x27;S WARRANT IS STRONGER THAN &#x27;UNIMPLEMENTED&#x27;, BUT NOT AS STRONG AS THIS POINT ORIGINALLY CLAIMED -- CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-30.**  It said *&#x27;the guard is load-bearing HOST memory safety&#x27;*.  **MEASURED FALSE by `offsetof`:** the over-run lands INSIDE the same allocation (`re_linear_a[32]` IS `re_linear_b[0]`; `re_linear_b[32]` IS `de_reg[0]`), so it is UB plus SILENT INTRA-STRUCT CROSS-ARRAY CORRUPTION -- not a wild write, not a host crash, and **invisible to plain ASAN**.  The phrase survived here after being corrected in `sgilinearoob`, in the very row whose first line tells the implementer to read it before the brief: *corrected in one place, alive in one more*, which is mechanical rule 2&#x27;s exact scenario.  **AND THIS POINT BUNDLED A SECOND, SEPARATELY-UNVERIFIED CLAIM:** the `len == 1` over-read of `data[hwi+1]` is of the CALLER&#x27;S buffer, and nobody has established that buffer&#x27;s size -- so it is not &#x27;host memory safety&#x27; either.  The honest warrant is **cross-array corruption plus an unverified caller-buffer over-read**, which still settles DECLINE against IMPLEMENT and settles it for reasons that are true.  **8. NAME `sgidewidth` IN THE PRETABLE AND ROUND RECORD.**  It is a SEVENTH live width kill in the same file, deliberately excluded (different handler, different accept set, readable registers).  A round called widthkillclass that fixes 6 of 7 and does not say so writes false unanimity.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Ruled the scope question and then made it moot: ONE round of six, CONDITIONAL on extending the witness to all six pre-fix -- &#x27;one witnessed site does NOT license the class&#x27;.  Found the SEVENTH live width kill (`sgi_de`, one `sb` away) that the scope question was hiding, and the `sgi_mte` mis-service plus its resurrection trap.  Verified the per-site-latch question against `dev_wdc.c`&#x27;s recorded SIX-FLAGS adjudication rather than asserting the idiom.  Named the `len == 1` over-read of `data[hwi+1]` as the reason DECLINE beats IMPLEMENT.  Specified the detector in advance -- channels live and dead, the two smallest breaking edits with the row that stays green for each -- which is the obligation this seat has now discharged three rounds running. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — MEASURED, not argued, and it settled the round&#x27;s open question.  Cloned to /tmp/gxopus_497 (deliberately NOT build/, which it found mid-propagation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c newer than build/gxemul -- and refused to read a tree someone else was mutating).  Applied the candidate fix at all six sites with ONE parameterised pattern asserting count==1 at each, which is itself the evidence that the six are one class.  exit(1) count 21 -&gt; 15, MAKE_RC=0, [binary -nt source] checked on every build.  DISCHARGED THE WITNESS OBLIGATION: 26/26 across all six sites and both directions.  Then measured M1 and M2 escaping the brief&#x27;s proposed row set, and named the substring-presence flood hole as a third.  Found the dead-tail behaviour change, the re_linear OOB-by-one, the TEX index defect, and that my census&#x27;s 21 was the FILE total against the handler&#x27;s 13.  Also reported the backslash trap recurring on its own apply script -- zero of six matches -- caught by running `grep | cat -A` on the WRITTEN file rather than re-reading its own source. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 1.9 MB.  Ruled SIX rounds against fable5&#x27;s one, on the ground that same-device/class/detector is a NECESSARY condition for joining and not a class-level waiver of the witness obligation -- the disagreement that opus5 then settled by discharging it.  Derived the `d-&gt;warned |= X` -&gt; `= X` mutant, which isolated per-site rows cannot see and only a cross-site retention row catches.  Independently reached the EXCEPTION_DBE result for `return 0`, and corrected my historical-tail premise: this fork pre-zeroes `data` before dispatch, so an early return yields zero rather than uninitialised bytes -- the `else if` form is still correct, for the marshalling contract rather than for stale bytes.  Found the CRIME_RE_TEX index defect under the standing licence. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — *** SEAT FAILURE -- QUOTA WALL, not agreement. ***  `Error: Individual quota reached ... Resets in 1h22m`, 109 bytes on stderr.  Recorded by name.  The wall is its FIRST on record and arrived after roughly six substantial invocations in forty minutes. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Engaged the control-flow legality and the else-if restructure. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — 30 mentions of the break question; engaged the restructure at length. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — PACKET-FED and it found two brief defects from the inlined text alone: the census arithmetic does not reconcile as written, and §7.2&#x27;s &#x27;the model cannot serve at all&#x27; is FALSE for four of the six sites BY THE PACKET&#x27;S OWN CODE -- the store loop generalises to len 2/4/6.  That refutation is what forced the real warrant (the OOB) to be found. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Engaged the control-flow question; concise, no thinking burn. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Named the `break` defect precisely -- &#x27;exits the nearest for/while/switch, not the enclosing if&#x27; -- and predicted the failure mode a copy-paste implementer would hit at runtime.  Its latch objection (per-site deviates from the house idiom) is the one seat reading that went the other way, and it is packet-fed so it could not check `dev_wdc.c`; fable5 opened that file and the SIX-FLAGS comment settles it against this seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Spent the round&#x27;s capped ask-again and returned F1 (`|=` -&gt; `=`, passing all 35 because every multi-site session uses ONE traffic pattern) and F2 (announce-and-perform, re-opening the LINEAR OOB with the host alive).  Found F4 -- the gate comment claimed &#x27;Filed, not done&#x27; about a row that DID NOT EXIST -- and F5, sgirekills&#x27; stale witness.  Corrected its OWN scope warrant and disclosed a circularity it owed: the shared-bitfield hazard exists BECAUSE the one-round fix chose six bits in one word. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — MEASURED in its own scratch dir, controls both directions, repo verified clean afterwards.  Confirmed the P_AGY wait defect and showed it aborts BEFORE the seat check -- seat files with no verdict roster, the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inside the tool built to prevent it.  Measured my negative control VACUOUS (ledgerstatelies has ZERO entries; its quotation is in a row-level field held_rows never reads) -- the permissive class I forbade passes every control and renders byte-identically.  Refuted my &#x27;every defang note quotes the literal&#x27; warrant: 9 live literals, ALL real markers, 0 quotations, vs 31 rewrites.  Found the 4-character `.split(&quot;,&quot;)[:1]` mutant passing all three controls while collapsing a seven-seat hold to one, and established that the REAL detector is `--check`, not the controls.  Named two EQUIVALENT mutants (the non-greedy `?`; the seat filter, dead at every call site).  *** AND IT CORRECTED ME IN MY FAVOUR: my Q3(b) premise was false -- a correct discriminator never cleared wdccdbmidxfer, so I overrode nothing; the `&gt;=` slack did. One root cause, two symptoms. ***  One of its own claims did NOT reproduce (the ledgerstatelies prose phantom -- a backtick follows the colon, so no class matches it), recorded in controls.out because a seat&#x27;s finding gets checked too. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 1.5 MB.  Its sharpest contribution is a LIMIT on the round&#x27;s own best statistic: even perfect prediction agreement &#x27;cannot validate mutant COMPLETENESS&#x27; -- it establishes only that the author understood the mutants he selected.  Names four shapes it predicts as full escapes and which sit outside the hypothesis set entirely: the write-only DBE, a repeat-access kill, a post-debug legal kill, and the LINEAR endpoint hole. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 11 KB.  *** FIRED IN BREACH OF THE OWNER&#x27;S RESERVATION RULING, which panel.sh did not yet implement -- that gap is filed as `agyreservation` and fixed in the same commit as this entry. ***  Asked for `sb` (len=1) and `sh` (len=2) probe vectors so a NARROWING edit (e.g. `len == 4`) cannot escape -- a real coverage point about the accept side. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 15 KB, having been at 568 bytes and looking exactly like a stall when first sampled -- the carrier&#x27;s own short-answer warning, confirmed again.  Ratified the splice (&#x27;`return 0` is a guest DBE; `break` is not legal here&#x27;), the per-site bits against the not-mutually-exclusive warrant, and the `warned`-after-`mte_reg` layout the LINEAR comments assert.  Flagged the catch-all&#x27;s queue id independently of kimi. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 98 KB on a 36 KB brief -- the argv-cap fix working on the exact case that killed this seat earlier.  Six required changes, all records: the catch-all&#x27;s queue id in the SHIPPED COMMENT (independently found by grok), premise 4&#x27;s count, labelling `d_lessthan` as the intentional equivalence probe wherever table 2 is quoted, and citing the six-site `read_probe.out` for the 26/26 rather than the single-site artifact. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED, and it found two brief defects from the packet alone: §C0&#x27;s tooling grep is a NULL PATTERN, and premise 1 is a pre-diff census mislabelled as current state.  Both verified and corrected.  *It also noted the null pattern was the SECOND defective output in a box whose own text confessed only the first.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Named two internal contradictions: §C0 says the round has NOT shipped while the header calls the diff shipped, and the census counts the six width guards as terminating calls the shipped diff removes.  Both true.  Also flagged premises 5 and 6 as not checkable from the packet, which is the honest packet-fed answer rather than a stall. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Design internally consistent; one load-bearing `fatal()` assumption not confirmable from the packet; several in-text numbers opaque rather than wrong. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_214531</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">panelargvcap</span><span class="ttl">panel.sh WARNS that a brief exceeds the argv cap and then fires the two argv seats anyway -- measured, two seats lost on a 38 KB brief</span><span class="tip">panel.sh WARNS that a brief exceeds the argv cap and then fires the two argv seats anyway -- measured, two seats lost on a 38 KB brief — witness: harness:regress/artifacts/panelargvcap/controls_20260830/ -- the same control script scores 6/6 rc=0 on the fixed panel.sh and EXTRACT FAILED rc=9 on the pre-fix form preserved beside it.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgidewidth</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c&#x27;s sgi_de handler is a SEVENTH live width kill -- one guest `sb` to the DE window ends the host, and widthkillclass would have shipped 6 of 7</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c&#x27;s sgi_de handler is a SEVENTH live width kill -- one guest `sb` to the DE window ends the host, and widthkillclass would have shipped 6 of 7 — commits: 387b980 cb28a6f — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/sgidewidth/opus_witness_20260831 -- RUNG 3 on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`, cold debugger, real single-stepped instructions through real decode and real `memory_rw`, no `device_add`.  Device located at KSEG1 0xffffffffb5002000 by reading `machine_sgi.c` and the `memory_device_register` call rather than taking it from the brief; every encoding re-checked against the debugger&#x27;s own `unassemble` at run time.  LIVENESS: `sw 0xdeadbeef` then `lw` round-trips.  DEVICE-SIGNATURE: a store to `CRIME_DE_SCISSOR` echoes our own value back in the device&#x27;s own diagnostic, which RAM cannot emit.  KILL: `sb`, `sh`, `lbu`, `lhu` each give exactly one `len = N not implemented` and exit(1) -- 4/4.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** REFUTED MY SEVERITY ESCALATION AND WAS RIGHT. ***  Derived that MIPS raises ADEL/ADES before `memory_rw`, so a width-w access arrives w-ALIGNED; on a page-aligned page-length window the boundary truncation can then never fire, and the declined set is 6144 / 3072 rather than my 8201 / 4105.  Widths 3/5/6/7 are unreachable and the row&#x27;s ORIGINAL &quot;one guest sb&quot; was right before I strengthened it.  Also confirmed the claim I asked it to check -- sgi_mte&#x27;s guard is dead, sgi_de&#x27;s is reachable -- and gave the fix shape: NOT #497&#x27;s splice, because this tail calls draw_primitive; an early return AFTER answering the read explicitly, which is the one place an early return is correct.  Its one contestable claim (a survival oracle cannot see a spurious draw) is resolved by kimi&#x27;s conditioning. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — (HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5 -- DISCHARGED 2026-08-31)  It answered TWICE: pass 1 built the rung-3 witness and the nine-build table that chose the fix shape, and pass 2 re-measured the SHIPPED detector and found two 25/25 escapes plus a vacuous row.  (HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5]  Still measuring when this landed -- running a kill table from a FROZEN copy, obeying the rule.  **A hold, not a seat failure and not agreement.**  It is held for the rung-3 witness ATTEMPT, and for one question no reading seat can settle: whether the freshly-repaired gxp  *** THE DEFANG ITSELF RE-ARMED THE MARKER THE FIRST TIME: the discharge note appended the ORIGINAL text, which still carried the live literal, so three tools kept reading the stage as held.  Fourth instance of the quoted-marker class this session, and the first committed WHILE DEFANGING. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — 1.9 MB.  Refuted the width escalation INDEPENDENTLY of fable5: *&quot;&#x27;Ordinary `ld` reaches 3/5/6/7&#x27; is false.  Only lengths 1 and 2 are ordinary aligned MIPS witnesses.&quot;*  Two file-reading seats reaching the same refutation by separate routes is what made me check it rather than defend it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. ***  The seat check printed it by name and, since this session&#x27;s fix, it no longer inflates the tally or lands in the missing list. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Third independent rejection of the 3/5/6/7 claim, and ratified the two things that matter: the `else` arm is reachable, and #497&#x27;s splice must not be copied.  Its Q1 answer matches fable5&#x27;s -- a latched decline that skips BOTH the switch and draw_primitive, then answers the read. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** 133 KB, AND ITS VERDICT LINE IS THE LITERAL WORDS &quot;verdict line first.&quot; -- a FORMAT MISS that `seat_verdicts.py` files as &quot;answered, no verdict line&quot; and that I nearly recorded as a non-answer. ***  Reading the body instead produced the round&#x27;s biggest find: it engaged with `draw_primitive`&#x27;s `default:` arm, which no other seat did, and observed that against a PRISTINE register file a spurious draw dies there -- so a startFlagged survival row CAN catch the naive-splice defect.  Chasing that opened `sgideprimop`, now filed HIGH: 254 of 256 guest-written drawing ops end the host, including two of the four DOCUMENTED primitive types.  *An unread review is neither a seat failure nor agreement, and this is the third instance this session where the body carried what the verdict line did not.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE STRONGEST PACKET-FED ANSWER ON RECORD HERE, AND I UNDER-CREDITED IT FOR AN HOUR. ***  From the packet alone it identified the alignment assumption, DERIVED BOTH BRANCHES, and correctly refused to settle the undecidable one: *&quot;widths 3/5/6/7 are guest-reachable only if this fork&#x27;s MIPS core does not raise AdEL/AdES first.  Under alignment enforcement the counts drop to 6144 declined / 3072 startFlag-set and the truncation chapter closes entirely -- but the sb/sh doors and the draw-hazard survive untouched, so the defect stands either way.&quot;*  **6144/3072 is the figure I computed only AFTER a file-reading seat told me.**  It also named my framing error exactly: an `ld` three bytes before the end is an `ld` at a MISALIGNED address, not an ordinary one.  *This is what the packet preamble asks for and it is why &quot;score a packet-fed agreement as zero&quot; was too blunt a rule -- glm did not agree, it CONDITIONED.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Defect real, fix = latched decline that skips the switch and draw_primitive but still performs the read response; one latch bit.  Called the enumerations correct -- the same unchecked confirmation as minimax. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Reported reconciling 8201/4105 from the truncation rule independently, without flagging alignment -- so it confirmed a premise it had no way to check.  Correctly caught the draw_primitive tail hazard.  *Recorded as a confirmation of a false premise rather than as agreement, which is the distinction the doctrine exists to draw.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_004034</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Walked the goto against every exit path and found it CORRECT.  Predicted the len==2 escape (`len == 4 || len == 2`) at 25/25 from an enumerated walk and deliberately did NOT build it during a live panel -- the oracle-contention rule.  Scored six dispositions five right and ONE WRONG: deleting the 22-row artifact, where the house remedy is freeze-with-banner.  Refuted two contract claims against this round&#x27;s own preserved logs (the early-`return` catch, via mB 25/25; tail_acc uniqueness, via mE reddening THREE rows).  Answered the carrier question: fall-through vs skip-the-tail is the SCOPE AXIS of every member, not a seventh member, and belongs in the sketch rule -- now in BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** MEASURED TWO MORE 25/25 ESCAPES THAT SURVIVED THE 29-ROW VERSION, BOTH FLOOR BREACHES, BOTH ONE KEYSTROKE. ***  `bracegoto` (the goto moved inside the latch braces) re-opens the exact host death this round removes, measured exit1, for every decline after the first -- blind because EVERY row exercised the FIRST decline of a fresh session.  `bit5collide` (1&lt;&lt;6 -&gt; 1&lt;&lt;5) silences the decline outright; `sharedlatch` was a POINT TEST against a six-member set and five of six partners escaped.  Both repairs BOUNDED -- no new rows, no new sessions.  Also caught `hi_kill` VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION (pinned on an ACCEPTED width, measured identical on the pre-fix build, all eight round mutants and all five of its own).  Confirmed floor (a)+(b) hold: verbatim reinstatement 14/25, eleven reds -- measured because no artifact showed it.  Its frozen probe earned its keep: the repo copy was edited live under its run. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — 2.2 MB, and it was very nearly an unread review -- section G caught that it was recorded NOWHERE.  Three things no other seat said:  *** (1) `mC`&#x27;s &quot;23/25&quot; IS CONFOUNDED, and I put that number in the CHANGELOG and the commit message. ***  Its generator combined the shared bit with the `startFlag = 0` fallthrough, so its two reds were `dec_switch` AND `sharedlatch` -- two edits, one score.  **Pure bit-0 reuse against `mR` would lose only the latch row.**  The conclusion (own bit required) survives; the number does not measure what it was cited for.  (2) It reached the bit-collision hole independently: *&quot;primes only bit 0, so collisions with bits 1-5 pass all 25 rows&quot;*.  *** (3) A BETTER `tail_acc_x` THAN EITHER fable5&#x27;s OR MINE, and it changes the disposition. ***  We both concluded: keep the death pin, document the expiry.  Codex: **replace the ORACLE** -- program `DE_PRIM_LINE` with safe trivial state, do an accepted start-marked access, then assert draw ENTRY and `exit0`.  That keeps the &#x27;accepted accesses still draw&#x27; control while **removing the red-by-succeeding coupling entirely**, so `sgideprimop` can be fixed independently.  *Recorded as OWED rather than implemented: it needs a measured draw-entry channel for the LINE case, and the current rows are measured-green rather than vacuous -- so this is an improvement, not a floor breach, and it should not be written unmeasured.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Reached the len==2 hole from the WITNESS side, independently of fable5: the witness killed on sb/sh/lbu/lhu while the detector&#x27;s survival rows used sb and lbu only, and `two_widths_2` stays green whether len==2 is accepted, killed or declined -- three machines, one row.  Also caught the tail_acc uniqueness L4 and the state=open-while-shipped L4. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Refused to answer Q2/Q3 from a truncated packet and said so -- the honest answer.  See the brief defect below. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  *** CAUGHT MY BRIEF DEFECT: the diff truncated mid-comment at &quot;Clearing startFl&quot;, before the goto, the latch and the label. ***  It named the exact cut point rather than answering around it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Challenged the one-latch-bit disposition and called Q3 &#x27;delete the goto&#x27; -- which is adjacent to the bracegoto escape opus5 then measured. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Named three semantic premises the brief left unstated rather than guessing at them. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_023544</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgireunmapped</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c&#x27;s unmatched-address else arm ends the host on ANY width in EITHER direction across 2080 bytes of its own registered window -- one guest `lb` at 0xb5001800 -- and NEITHER #497 nor sgirekills-as-scoped touches it</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c&#x27;s unmatched-address else arm ends the host on ANY width in EITHER direction across 2080 bytes of its own registered window -- one guest `lb` at 0xb5001800 -- and NEITHER #497 nor sgirekills-as-scoped touches it — commits: a115a4a a643e18 9f666a8 — witness: probe:regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py -- 28 rows, gate-run by gate_arm.sh.  Test-first evidence in regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/: 18/28 on the pre-fix build and 28/28 on the fix, plus the ORDER PROOF for the re-pinned sibling probe (34/35 RED on the committed build under the new oracle, 35/35 after), all sentinel-verified and re-run by the coordinator rather than inherited. — note: #499 pass 3 (4daa506): 6 of 8 answering seats say DO NOT CLOSE.  TWO MEASURED CLEAN SWEEPS (M1 direction, M2 position) plus a RED-gate-on-GREEN-probe defect found by two seats independently.  Pass 4 closes the CLASS rather than the cells: the catch-all&#x27;s five inputs enumerated once, 36 -&gt; 48 rows, contract updated with what is deliberately NOT covered. Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THE STRONGEST ANSWER OF THE ROUND, AND IT ENLARGED THE OPTION SET FROM FOUR MEMBERS TO SIX -- BOTH NEW MEMBERS FOUND IN THE TREE, NOT REASONED. ***  (5) FAULT: `dev_unreadable.c` is a device whose whole access function is `return 0;`, and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times ON THIS MACHINE so absent hardware raises DBE -- verified.  Rejected on EVIDENCE (no CRIME datasheet in _scratchpad, no evidence real CRIME faults there, and a wrong DBE is worse than a wrong 0 because it diverts control flow), not on principle.  (6) BACKED-ACCEPT: invent storage, the `mte_reg[]`/`de_reg[]` idiom in this same file; available for CLIP_IDS, rejected under the addition licence.  It also named a seventh candidate (ALIAS/mirror) and dismissed it.  **AND IT FOUND THE FILE&#x27;S OWN POLICY: the three sibling handlers -- sgi_de, sgi_mte, sgi_de_status -- ALREADY decline unmatched addresses without exit(1), and pristine upstream&#x27;s sgi_de was exit-free too, so the RE catch-all is UPSTREAM RESIDUE and this fix removes the file&#x27;s one exception to its own policy.**  That is a far stronger warrant than &#x27;it is a host kill&#x27;.  Gave TWO latch bits with a better warrant than #497&#x27;s (the house latch is per-MESSAGE-SITE and this arm has two texts), enumerated TWELVE boundary constants and twelve owed accept rows, and found a LATCH-BIT COLLISION escape (1&lt;&lt;5 colliding with LINB_LEN passes all three stated oracles).  ONE CLAIM REFUTED -- see the row note. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — (HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5 -- DISCHARGED 2026-08-31)  The hold was for the rung-3 witness ATTEMPT the lifecycle ruling requires before a round may record itself unwitnessed.  **It did not merely attempt: it BUILT one, 32/32**, with liveness, device-signature, neighbour-device and negative controls, and widths enumerated rather than argued.  It also found THREE escapes, corrected a row of its own that failed for an ADEL rather than a code reason, and diagnosed a defect in the shared probe harness that made one of its rows unable to fail.  *The provisional-adjudication rule was right to hold: the design converged 8/9 and the ninth seat still changed the round -- an accept row at a window BASE cannot see a far-bound move, which no reading seat had noticed.* — evidence: regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/opus_witness_20260830</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 1.3 MB.  Independently derived the TEX geometry from the CRIME spec -- 28 entries of 64 bits -- which cross-checks my walk of the shipped code exactly and SHARPENED `sgitexindex` from &#x27;wrong shift&#x27; to &#x27;unit mismatch&#x27;.  Called Q5 a false dichotomy and put the correction best: **&#x27;six real windows&#x27; should read &#x27;six MODELED windows&#x27;** -- CLIP_IDS is a seventh real hardware window with no dispatch.  Listed the six accept-row addresses and the final aligned starts to pin upper-bound drift.  *Its supporting citation is a URL to an external spec, NOT read locally; the local header, struct comment and array arithmetic are what carry it.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. ***  The seat check printed it by name; a defect in the SUMMARY line that counted it as a non-answering seat was found and fixed in this same commit. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Member 3, splice not return, 2080 confirmed, and reached CLIP_IDS independently: *&#x27;unmapped is not [right]; CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS at 0x16e0 is a named 32-byte window the cascade never implements.&#x27;*  Said ONE latch bit -- the round&#x27;s only real disagreement, resolved against it by fable5&#x27;s per-message-site warrant. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  **Independently RE-WALKED all 4096 dispatched addresses against the header constants and confirmed the kill set exactly.**  Also challenged the `(once)` census the brief quoted from the carrier -- it counted 24 where the carrier said 9, AND IT WAS RIGHT; re-derived here by four methods and the carrier is corrected with its deriver inlined. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Correctly flagged that the 2080 enumeration is *&#x27;conditional on window-constant values the packet asserts and never derives&#x27;* -- a fair premise-audit catch: I verified the constants from crmfbreg.h but did not inline that derivation in the brief. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Reached member 3 and TWO direction bits from the packet alone -- the same answer fable5 justified from the tree, by a different route. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Honestly refused Q5 as unsettleable from the packet and warned that a stale constant there would silently shrink the kill set -- which is exactly what CLIP_IDS turned out to bear on. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_232416</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DO NOT CLOSE -- found a defect in the diff&#x27;s own hunks that makes the GATE RED on a GREEN probe: both new row names are exactly 16 chars, the probe prints %-16s, the gate pattern needs two spaces.  Verified against this round&#x27;s preserved 36/36 log at got=0/want=1.  Also found comp1-x-read empty, and that `clip surv&#x27; is vacuous by construction with a measurably false note. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — DO NOT CLOSE -- MEASURED M1 (read arm qualified to component 2) and M2 (component 1 far edge absorbed by LINEAR_A) at 36/36 EACH: two TOTAL ESCAPES of a detector that had just come back green.  Also named the VALUE axis (guard reads writeflag; body reads idata) without building it, and prescribed the class-level fix: enumerate the catch-all&#x27;s five inputs once rather than patching a fourth cell. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DO NOT CLOSE -- named DIRECTION as the missing dimension and, unprompted, the single fact that would DEFEAT its own finding (that comp1 loads cannot reach the catch-all).  Checked: the catch-all else is address-agnostic, so the finding stands.  Also flagged that treating BYTE as representative of all narrow widths assumes the code partitions 8 vs !=8. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — [NOT-FIRED]  *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — CLEAN in its VERDICT LINE -- and item 3 of its own BODY describes M2 exactly (`LINEAR_A&#x27;s near bound sliding down by 0x10 eats 0x16f0-0x16ff and leaves clip * green&#x27;), which the opus seat measured as a 36/36 total escape in the same panel.  A seat that FOUND a breach and then ratified closure.  Recorded as a fresh instance of the carrier&#x27;s read-the-body rule, not as agreement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DO NOT CLOSE -- found the SAME gate defect INDEPENDENTLY and by a different route, citing lib.sh&#x27;s count() implementation.  Its framing is the one kept: `the layer the diff edited (the gate list) is the layer the harness bypassed.&#x27;  Noted the tree moved mid-review (my uncommitted repair) and said so. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DOES NOT CLOSE -- named the comp1 read path and supplied the argument that SETTLED it against the two CLEAN verdicts: a standard under which the 36-row detector passes would also have acquitted the 34-row detector, which was measurably breached, so it cannot be the chain&#x27;s standard. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — NOT CLEAN -- named the omission of access type (read vs write) for component 1. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — CLEAN, chain closes.  MINORITY and not adopted; settled by argument rather than tally. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">holdmarkermulti</span><span class="ttl">gen_codex_wall.py&#x27;s marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape</span><span class="tip">gen_codex_wall.py&#x27;s marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape — commits: 9abd175 — witness: harness:MEASURED against HEAD and working ledgers.  `MARKER = re.compile(r&#x27;\(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged:\s*([a-z0-9_]+)\s*\]&#x27;)` -- the class has no comma, so `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5, codex, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax)` does not match.  Enumerated: 2 multi-seat markers present, of which `sgirekills` is a genuine live hold owing all seven scriptable seats and is counted NOWHERE.  CLOSED by 9abd175: a strict token grammar plus seat-id validation against the ledger&#x27;s own roster, controlled in both directions -- real ids render clean with empty stderr, an unknown id renders `?token` and names row, phase and token, and the real ledger is unchanged at 8 held rows with no warnings.  Controls preserved at regress/artifacts/holdmarkermulti/controls_20260830/controls.out.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** FOUND THE SESSION&#x27;S MOST IMPORTANT THING: the carrier&#x27;s discriminator was SUPERSEDED IN THE LEDGER ON 2026-08-29 -- wdccdbmidxfer entry [28], &#x27;My second sweep used same-stage matching and did not repeat it&#x27; -- and the carrier kept the old wording, so this round read it and repeated the documented failure at 6x scale. ***  Also: `check_stage_panels.py:101` keys on the live literal, so a wrongly-defanged regress entry does not merely go invisible, it MANUFACTURES a regress seat answer.  Caught holdphase&#x27;s witness mislabelling which marker was reverted.  Ratified (c) and (d) after checking the floor reasoning rather than accepting it.  Flagged a records debt: the 35/35 and 34/35 escape logs are cited but not preserved under regress/artifacts/. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — MEASURED in its own scratch dir.  Found the DATE BUG under codex&#x27;s cross-phase pattern: `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, so same-date siblings discharged the markers.  Proved my negative control VACUOUS (wrong field) and my &#x27;every defang note quotes the literal&#x27; warrant FALSE (9 real markers, 0 quotations, 31 rewrites).  Built the 4-character `.split(&quot;,&quot;)[:1]` mutant passing all three controls.  CORRECTED ME IN MY FAVOUR on Q3(b).  One of its own claims did not reproduce and is recorded as such. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — 1.6 MB.  Enumerated the defang table row by row and found the CROSS-PHASE pattern: 6 of 7 clearances discharged a held phase that never got an answer.  Also caught NSEATS announcing 7 seats while 6 launch, the probe denying it was the witness it had become, &#x27;263 lines&#x27; for 258, and that &#x27;8 defanged/2 kept&#x27; describes a state that never shipped (7 removed/3 kept). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling 2026-08-30, now implemented in panel.sh rather than only written down.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. ***  Panels run at EIGHT seats by design; PANEL_FIRE_AGY=1 fires it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Found the vacuous control MID-ANSWER while still streaming -- &#x27;held_rows() only walks entry notes, so I need to check whether that control can actually fail&#x27;.  Was at 718 bytes and looked exactly like a stall when first sampled; the carrier&#x27;s sample-twice rule held. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** FILE-READING, not packet-fed -- the CLI is the kimi seat again since 2026-08-29, and my first entry for this seat both mislabelled its transport AND said only &#x27;recorded as answered&#x27;.  That is the unread-review shape written into the ledger by the person who fired the panel. ***  213 KB.  **Its distinctive finding is a phantom the character class could not stop:** `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: codex and opus5)` -- the word `and` instead of a comma -- matches the loose class and yields ONE garbage seat token `codex and opus5`.  Verified here: the loose v2 class matches it, the strict token grammar adopted from opus5 rejects it.  *** AND IT NAMED THE DURABLE CLOSE, WHICH IS BETTER THAN EITHER REGEX: validate split tokens against `ledger[&quot;seats&quot;]` ids and flag unknowns, rather than tuning the class. ***  Adopted in this commit -- the roster is DERIVED from the ledger, an unknown token renders `?token` and warns on stderr rather than being dropped, and it is controlled both ways.  *Tuning a character class is an arms race against prose; an id that is not a seat is wrong whatever shape it arrived in.*  It also reached the manufactured-answer mechanism independently of fable5 -- removing the literal from a `seat=fable5, phase=regress` entry drops it into the content-keyed seat tally as a regress ANSWER -- and confirmed the say/echo analysis.  *** AND QUOTING THAT EXAMPLE ARMED A DIFFERENT TOOL.  `check_stage_panels.py` keys on the RAW literal and has no seat-id validation, so it read this note&#x27;s quotation as a live marker and reported `holdmarkermulti review 8/9 awaiting: codex and opus5` -- a stage held for a seat that does not exist.  The gate caught it and refused the commit. ***  **That is the third instance in one session, and this one is inside the note documenting the hazard.**  The carrier&#x27;s rule is *before quoting any string a tool matches on, ask what THAT tool will do with your copy* -- and the answer differs per tool: `gen_codex_wall.py` now validates against the seat roster and would have rendered `?codex and opus5` with a warning; `check_stage_panels.py` does not, and simply believed it.  *Recorded rather than quietly fixed, because the seat-id validation adopted in this same commit demonstrably does NOT generalise to the other two readers of this literal -- which is a real limit of the fix, found by breaking it.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Caught that my &#x27;reproduces the logged error VERBATIM&#x27; claim is an overclaim -- the diagnostic matches, the location prefix does not -- from the two strings the brief inlined and nothing else. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  &#x27;Change 2 regex over-matches; Q2 unsafe; Q3 disposition (a) is wrong/unverified.&#x27;  Named the split-first-seat mutant independently of opus5. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Correctly refused Q4: I asked seats to walk 8 defanged rows WITHOUT INLINING WHICH EIGHT.  A packet-fed seat cannot read the ledger -- my brief defect, not a seat failure. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_224559</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgideprimop</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);`</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);` — commits: b78bb0f 840f3d0 — Closed in the main loop (claude-fable-5-1, author self-review disclosed) at pass 16 on 8 of 9 mandated seats answering (agy deliberately not fired, reserved for measurement): six zero-breach answers each with a denominator, two claimed breaches refuted by reading. The chain closes on the first VALID pass finding zero breaches, per the repair-chain rule. — witness: probe:regress/sgi_de_primop_probe.py -- 35 rows, gate-run by gate_arm.sh (gate 14 ARM: PASS 546 checks, 0 FAIL; primop 35 emitted / 35 green / 0 red).  Test-first evidence in regress/artifacts/sgideprimop/killtable_20260901_table3_SHIPPED/: PREFIX 10/35 against the NAMED commit 840f3d0^ (never HEAD, which moved under an earlier control and gave a clean sweep that read like a vacuous detector), BASE 35/35.  22 mutants, 22 predictions written into the driver BEFORE each run so the order is provable by mtime, ZERO surprises. — note: *** CLOSED AT PASS 16 (b78bb0f + the pass-16 records rider). *** Sixteen passes, fourteen defects in the detector and its records, ZERO in the product fix after 840f3d0. The closing pass: six of eight answering seats zero breaches WITH denominators (two independent 17-register enumerations from source), two claimed breaches refuted by reading (a packet artifact; a misread latch). Contract: four closed shapes, everything else declared uncovered by construction, limits MEASURED. Filed: sgideprimopstrengthen (three free strengthenings), clausedbound, tablebasenotfull, dashphasedrop, checkcunwired. The chain&#x27;s own lesson: I close one side of a symmetry and leave the mirror open, and the corrective was never my re-reading but a seat with a mutant, an arithmetic check, or an enumeration from source.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION of nine dispositions, verified by opening files rather than by reasoning; it stated its line numbers matched the brief&#x27;s ranges, proving it was in the fork and not a pristine copy.  **Zero dispositions reversed on substance; two corrected.**  *** ITS CENTRAL FINDING PREVENTED THE ROUND FROM MINTING THE DEFECT IT HAD JUST FILED. ***  The natural oracle-correction for `tail_acc_x` is `exit1` -&gt; `exit0` (*the host now survives*).  Traced as a CHANNEL rather than an intent: under a future one-token death reinstatement -- `exit(0)` at the default arm -- the session announces once and dies, `how` is `exit0`, the re-pinned row reads GREEN, `tail_acc` still counts 1, no other session reaches `draw_primitive()`, and the detector scores a **TOTAL ESCAPE, 29/29**.  **Exit status cannot vary between *declined and quit* and *died with status 0*.**  Repair: re-pin onto a positive post-draw observable (a load through the device; `printed()` already reads `died`), keeping an exit-status row only as a second channel.  **L1 -- a row under construction -- so no stopping-rule tension**, and the round owes a FOURTH mutant: death-by-`exit(0)` at the default arm.  *Knowable at writing time from the CHANNEL check, which is exactly why that check runs before the table.*  **Corrected my `exit0oracle` census: 13 sites, not 9** -- my grep anchored on the variable name `how` and missed four rows using `how1`..`how4`.  **Upheld the RANKING with a measurement I did not have**: it built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found `lo_sent` KILLS it via the read-side sentinel even though all four `*_alive` rows pass -- so the blindness is per-ROW, not per-detector, and no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole.  **Re-verified F5 mechanically rather than accepting my ruling in my own favour**: `DRAW` is a PREFIX of the fatal and `(once)` appends after it, and the `tail_acc` session is a fresh process issuing exactly one draw-triggering access -- so first occurrence = only occurrence = 1, latched or not.  Keep-the-text confirmed, and it noted the current text stays TRUE post-fix (still unimplemented, now declined), so there is no wrong-record pressure to reword.  **One records correction against me on F1:** `#498`&#x27;s own probe header ALREADY carried the reset-value fact in its DOES-NOT-CATCH section.  What is new is the headline promotion and the `memset` verification a packet-fed seat&#x27;s refusal forced -- *the round should credit the header rather than book a fresh discovery*.  Also flagged two records nits, both correct: my &quot;one uncommitted edit&quot; undercounted, and `CHECKPOINT.md`&#x27;s HEAD had gone stale by its own rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — THE NINTH SEAT; this stage was held open for it.  **Q1: the precedent is not elsewhere in the tree -- it is INSIDE `draw_primitive()` itself, twice.**  The ROP `default:` arms latch-and-announce without exiting, and the XFER guard in the same function has its `exit(1)` COMMENTED OUT.  So decline + latched announce is the file&#x27;s own idiom and the fix is not an import.  It killed the other three members on facts: ACCEPT needs a computed-then-discarded value and nothing computes a POINT or TRIANGLE; FAULT is an ADDRESS-SPACE disposition and this is a command-register VALUE, with no space to leave unmapped; BACKED ACCEPT fabricates storage where there is no register.  *** Q2 -- A FIFTH MUTANT, AND IT IS A FLOOR OBLIGATION RATHER THAN COVERAGE DEBT. ***  **`M5 primdrop`: delete the `case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:` LABEL** so RECTANGLE falls into the de-fatalised `default:`.  One line, and plausible rather than contrived because the four documented primitives INTERLEAVE.  Not covered by M1-M4: guard intact, bit intact, `|=` intact, and NO DEATH at all -- strictly quieter than M4.  **Measured, not argued: `grep` for `PRIMITIVE|0x2060|0x01000000|0x03000000` across all three shipped probes returns ZERO hits -- not one probe in the tree programs the primitive register or issues a legal LINE or RECTANGLE**, so every row I prescribed tests only the DECLINE side and the mutant sweeps all three clean.  **Why it is FLOOR and not debt:** the de-escalation clause requires the detector shipping WITH the fix to pin the ACCEPT SIDE of each predicate the guard gated -- *part of the fix&#x27;s cost*.  **My PRETABLE section 2 enumerated five rows and not one is an accept row.**  Before the fix, deleting that label meant `exit(1)` on the guest&#x27;s first rectangle -- unmissable; after it, one `(once)` line and a console that never renders text, since the file records at `:760-761` that OpenBSD draws characters this way.  **That is the accidental tripwire the fix destroys.**  Kill channel, needing no framebuffer read-back: a legal RECTANGLE with `DE_DRAWMODE_ROP` set and an unimplemented `rop`, asserting `count(DRAW) == 0` AND `count(&quot;[ sgi_de: RECT: rop[&quot;) == 1` -- an ARM-UNIQUE anchor (verified: two hits in the file, LINE and RECT).  Caveat that decides the session shape: `rop_used` is function-static, so the anchor fires once per PROCESS.  *** AND A HAZARD IN THE VERY PRECEDENT THAT LICENSES THE FIX: COPY THE SHAPE, NOT THE SCOPE. ***  `static char rop_used[256]` and `static int first` are FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, the `n_trunc_msgs` anti-pattern.  Census: **4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside `draw_primitive()`** -- the tree&#x27;s only such sites.  `d-&gt;warned` + `(once)` is the house form.  It also recorded a tension rather than resolving it: the in-file precedent latches PER VALUE while this fix latches ONE BIT for 254 op classes, so POINT-then-TRIANGLE prints once.  Deliberate (it is M1&#x27;s killer) but **the round must say why it did not use an `(op &gt;&gt; 24)`-indexed table**, or a reader takes the difference for an oversight.  *Hygiene: it reported that the tree was committed under it twice mid-review, checked that none of the five files it cites changed, and confirmed it wrote nothing.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  **CORRECTED HALF OF MY OWN HEADLINE CLAIM.**  I recorded POINT as what *an uninitialised OR PARTIALLY-WRITTEN op register* produces.  The reset half is proven (`memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct sgi_re_data))` zeroes `de_reg[]`); **the partially-written half is FALSE** -- `#498`&#x27;s width guard declines byte and halfword accesses BEFORE `de_reg[]` is updated, so accepted 32/64-bit writes always store complete words.  *One of this round&#x27;s own fixes closed the door I was citing.*  **DID THE OPTION-SET PRECEDENT GREP the three-membered-set rule demands**, and the tree answers clearly: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` declines an unimplemented command with a per-instance `(once)` diagnostic and completes normally, `dev_m8820x.c` ignores one; `dev_asc.c` raises a guest interrupt ONLY because it has an explicitly modelled illegal-command channel, which this site does not.  That is why FAULT is wrong here -- an in-tree warrant, not an argument.  *** TWO MORE ONE-CHARACTER MUTANT CLASSES, ONE OF WHICH IS NEW TO THIS PROJECT. ***  (a) `1 &lt;&lt; 7` -&gt; `1 &lt;&lt; 6` aliases an existing bit -- the `#498` collision class, killed by pre-arming all seven existing latch bits in one session before triggering.  (b) **`d-&gt;warned |= SGIRE_W_PRIMOP` -&gt; `d-&gt;warned = SGIRE_W_PRIMOP`, one character, which CLEARS EVERY EARLIER LATCH** -- killed by an A -&gt; primitive -&gt; A session requiring A&#x27;s message exactly once.  It proposes combining them: fire all seven latch sites, trigger POINT, revisit the sites, and require every message exactly once.  *** AND A VACUITY FINDING AGAINST SHIPPED MATERIAL: `exit0` IS NOT A SAFE SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  *&quot;A wrong `exit(0)` still kills the emulator and satisfies that oracle.&quot;*  `#498`&#x27;s `hi_alive`/`lo_alive`/`lo_alive2` rows all pin `how == &quot;exit0&quot;`.  Assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally with a post-trigger command.  Filed as its own row -- it is coverage debt against a shipped detector above the floor, not an L2.  **GUEST-CONTROL TRACE, concrete:** RE base `0x15000000`, DE mapping `0x15002000`, `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` offset `0x60`, START alias `0x60 | 0x800 = 0x860`, so a guest `sw` to **`0xffffffffb5002860`** with `t0 = 0` draws a POINT.  `startFlag` captures bit `0x800` and is cleared; `regnr = 0x60/4`.  **And `if (startFlag)` is NOT conditioned on `MEM_WRITE`, so a START-aliased READ also invokes drawing** -- worth a row of its own.  Notes that `break`-inside-latch is a non-mutant here: `default` is last and the function has no tail, so moving or deleting that `break` changes nothing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Checked the tree rather than the packet and said so: noticed HEAD was two commits past the brief&#x27;s `e898c84`, read the queue row, and confirmed **nothing has shipped for this row** -- the same premise error the measuring seat caught on the sibling round.  Independently confirmed the mask/constant table, the no-common-tail claim (*&quot;the switch&#x27;s closing brace is the last statement&quot;*), that `op` is guest-controlled through `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` and *is already DE_PRIM_POINT at RESET with no store*, and that bits [31:26] are unused by every `DE_PRIM_` constant so `DE_PRIM_LINE | 0x04000000` dies.  Chose **decline + latched announce**, not FAULT, and ruled the caller should NOT see the decline.  *** ITS BEST FINDING IS A THIRD BLAST-RADIUS TIER THAT BOTH I AND THE PACKET-FED SEATS MISSED. ***  I had corrected the radius to *one certain row (tail_acc_x) and one conditional on wording (tail_acc)*.  grok added: **a REWORD does not RED `hi_draw`/`hi_rd_draw` -- it BLINDS them.**  Both assert `count(DRAW) == 0`, so once the substring no longer exists they stay green *for the wrong reason* -- vacuous by construction, invisible to every gate.  It also caught that my `gate_arm.sh` comment said *both rows* red, *&quot;That comment overstates the same way the brief does&quot;* -- an L4 in shipped material, corrected in place.  **The design constraint that follows is sharper than mine: KEEP THE MESSAGE TEXT and add `(once)`.**  Keeping it costs ONE re-pin; rewording costs two re-pins AND silently blinds two further rows.  Disclosed honestly that it did not re-run the probe -- *&quot;this is a read of the live oracles against the code paths they exercise&quot;* -- which is the READING/MEASUREMENT distinction stated without being asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING; went to the tree for everything it could not settle and said which.  *** CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT IN MY BRIEF: I INLINED THE SUPERSEDED LATCH CENSUS. ***  My settled-premises block gave `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3, `(reported once)` 2.  **Those are the 2026-08-26 literals that the carrier itself DEMOTES in writing** -- *&quot;Superseded literals, kept only as the demoted record ... Never a current claim&quot;* -- re-derived on 08-30 as 24/4/5.  kimi measured 25/4/5 and I re-ran the carrier&#x27;s own deriver: **25 / 4 / 5**, confirming kimi and refuting my brief.  *The carrier names a deriver precisely so nobody transcribes the number, and I transcribed the one it had crossed out.*  The count grew 24 -&gt; 25 because THIS session&#x27;s `#498` added a `(once)` site -- the rounds that consult the number are the rounds that change it, which is the carrier&#x27;s stated reason for the deriver.  **Its conclusion is unaffected and strengthened: `(once)` is not merely the plurality but the overwhelming majority.**  *** ITS SECOND CORRECTION IS WRONG, AND CHECKING IT VINDICATED THE CARRIER. ***  kimi reports that the tree&#x27;s counter-style outlier *&quot;lives in `src/core/memory.c`&quot;* and that it saw **no counter** there, only a verbosity gate with a probe-tolerance early-out.  Measured: `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` DOES carry `static int n_trunc_msgs = 0;` with `n_trunc_msgs &lt; 8` and `n_trunc_msgs ++` -- a real counter, exactly as the carrier describes.  **kimi found a DIFFERENT diagnostic** -- `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()` in `core/memory.c`, which `memory_rw.c` calls and which genuinely has no counter.  Two similar-looking diagnostics in one area; it searched for the described behaviour and landed on the wrong one.  *Recorded rather than quietly dropped: a seat&#x27;s correction carries authority, and this one would have sent a future round to rewrite an accurate carrier paragraph.*  *** AND A THIRD Q4 OBSERVER THAT grok AND codex BOTH MISSED: THE HARDCODED ROW DENOMINATOR. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` checks `sgi_de rows green` against a literal `29`.  When `tail_acc_x` reds, that count falls to 28 and **the denominator check reds too** -- so the oracle-correction re-pin must bump the literal in the same edit, or the round meets two reds and attributes one of them wrongly.  Neither other file-reading seat named it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST FINDING AND IT LANDS AGAINST THE AUTHOR: the 2-of-4 reframing is a PARTITION of the 254/256 claim, not a correction, and recording only the sharper half is the locally-true restatement that hides the defect. ***  Also asked the one checkable caveat nobody else did -- are op bits [31:26] live? -- which on checking WIDENED the defect: the primitive field is bits [25:24] and the mask is [31:24], so a legal LINE or RECTANGLE with a stray high bit also dies.  Named four premises as not settleable from the packet, each with the single fact that would settle it, and separated the all-zeroes CONSTANT from the register&#x27;s RESET VALUE. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Supplied the clause-(d) mutant BEFORE the fix exists -- drop the `!` from the guard, so the latch never sets and the announce never fires while the bit name and the absent exit(1) both still read correct -- AND the row that kills it: two distinct unimplemented primitives in one session, expecting exactly one message.  Precision correction: 254/256 counts MASKED TOP-BYTE CLASSES, not full 32-bit op values. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Refused the no-common-tail premise and answered CONDITIONALLY ON BOTH BRANCHES, naming #498&#x27;s `goto`-to-reach-the-tail as the governing precedent if a tail exists.  Marked the uninitialised-register inference as REASONED rather than READ -- the distinction the author had blurred. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — Self-declared claude-fable-5-1 (a claim). CLOSE with the denominator the rule demands: 17 distinct de_reg reads on the decline path enumerated from source (op + eleven bit-test registers + five operand carriers), no twelfth; all eleven all-ones offsets verified against crmfbreg.h; nothing between the two draws can clobber them; the union of second-reach ops recomputed to 0xffffffff; uncovered list honest. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — READ-ONLY this turn. CLOSE with an independent denominator: 17 registers, 11 two-draw sessions, 10 literals, four shapes swept over their full mask/value spaces, 28-mutant inventory checked. Zero breaches. FILED four: (A) latch-field bits 0-8 closure is DERIVED -- no d-&gt;warned mutant was ever built; (B) the XFER block&#x27;s unlatched fatal prints all six registers by name, a free witness row the probe&#x27;s comment denied; (C) three derived locals degenerate under all-ones (src_x = -1 collides with the XFER-off sentinel) -- in declared-uncovered families, better disclosure examples; (D) shape (c) under-claims. ACCEPTED, all four. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — No breach; every shape walked. One wording correction: &#x27;at most two values per field&#x27; is false for op (ten second-reach values), and &#x27;every other predicate is uncovered&#x27; should read &#x27;no other predicate FAMILY is claimed closed&#x27;. ACCEPTED, corrected in place. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — UNKNOWN — [NOT-FIRED] *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling. NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — CLOSE. Residuals that do not block: dec regs liv still says &#x27;seven-store&#x27; (thirteen now) -- L4 corrected; masked equality on the eleven is actually killed (only nonzero value is all-ones) -- an UNDER-claim, safe; &#x27;two values per field&#x27; overclaims op. ACCEPTED. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — CONFIRMED — PASS. Wants one named measurement if a pass 17 ever runs: `rop &amp; 1&#x27;, the least-witnessed of the eleven. FILED in sgideprimopstrengthen. The two-value pinning is load-bearing only for registers and operands, verified on decline paths. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — MANUAL — Packet-fed. Claimed a conditional breach: &#x27;the dec regs session as diffed stores only TEN registers -- no MODE_DST store&#x27;. A PACKET ARTIFACT: the MODE_DST store was added in pass 14 and is unchanged context the pass-15 diff does not show; both file-reading seats verified it is present (probe:1119). Honestly conditional; refuted by the file. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — MANUAL — Packet-fed. Claimed `|| (x1 == 0)&#x27; escapes because &#x27;the count remains 1&#x27;. REFUTED BY READING THE GUARD: a bypass TRUE on the second reach makes the announce fire again, so every zero-operand two-draw session (dec regs, dec flood) reads 2 and reds. It misread how the latch works. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-03 — MANUAL — Packet-fed. NO BREACH in shapes (a)-(d) as stated; premise 5 non-checkable from the packet and if false the extra reads fall outside the covered shapes -- still no breach. Honest bound. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260903_121555</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pcc2defaultkill</span><span class="ttl">pcc2defaultkill  dev_pcc2.c: the `default:&#x27; arm exit(1)s on any unimplemented offset -- and kills READS the top-of-function memcpy has already served; SCCTXIACK (0x25) has no case, SCCRXIACK runs on reads</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">pcc2defaultkill  dev_pcc2.c: the `default:&#x27; arm exit(1)s on any unimplemented offset -- and kills READS the top-of-function memcpy has already served; SCCTXIACK (0x25) has no case, SCCRXIACK runs on reads — commits: ecc4d3b 2bb9cb4 — Closed in the main loop (claude-fable-5-1, author self-review disclosed, badge A) at ecc4d3b, 2026-09-05: the fix shipped with a 46-row detector (23 -&gt; 36 -&gt; 46 across a four-pass repair chain), four kill tables (6, 12 and 22 mutants; the pass-2 and pass-3 escapes each re-measured on the instrument they escaped), gate 14 four times (844/857/867/867 checks), a rung-3 witness 7/13 -&gt; 13/13.  The chain closed on pass 4 with zero floor breaches surviving adjudication; the remaining announce-grid cells are clause (e), written into the contract and filed as pcc2anngrid.  Seat verdicts at the closing pass: close (grok, deepseek, minimax, Fable-with-changes), not-close (codex derived; Opus and kimi measured -- both graded clause (e) on reading); the measurements settled it. — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/pcc2defaultkill/witness_20260905/defaultkill_witness.py (rung 3: guest st.b/ld.bu at uncased offsets, 7/13 red on the committed build, 13/13 on the fix; liveness + CHIPID + the arm&#x27;s own message channel); detector:regress/pcc2_default_probe.py (36 rows, gate 14; kill tables regress/artifacts/pcc2defaultkill/killtable_20260905/ (6 mutants, 23-row instrument) + killtable3_20260905/ (12 mutants, 36-row instrument; the six pass-2 escapes also scored 23/23 on the pass-2 instrument)) — note: Filed on pass 2; addendum 2026-09-04 (Opus, pass 8): SCCTXIACK 0x25 has no case at all and SCCRXIACK&#x27;s body runs on reads.  The same ACCEPT shape pcc2widthkills turned on, at a different predicate.  Filed in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (dated tail, `### pcc2defaultkill`, line 14290 as of 2026-09-04); this row added 2026-09-04 so the dashboard shows it -- the bug file had it, the ledger did not.  PROMOTED med -&gt; HIGH 2026-09-04 by the fix-review adjudication (Fable 5.1, ranked FIRST; GPT-6 Astra concurs HIGH): the unimplemented offsets are real PCC2 registers the in-tree guest driver writes -- if_ie.c:609 `pcc2_lancirq = ...&#x27; (0x2a), :612 `pcc2_lancerrirq&#x27; (0x2b), :655 `pcc2_lancerrstat&#x27; (0x28), verified by grep in _guest-src-openbsd22 -- and any access to them reaches default: exit(1).  This note first said the cheapest witness was `put b 0xfff4202a, 0&#x27; -&gt; host exit: MEASURED FALSE 2026-09-05 (put b never reaches a DM_DEFAULT arm; it survives and prints nothing) -- the witness is a GUEST st.b/ld.bu.  Option set to bring to pass 1: DECLINE + latched announce vs BACKED ACCEPT (the register array already backs every offset and the memcpy read path already serves it) vs FAULT; the READ side dies today too.  RE-BASED 2026-09-05: the Fable design seat found (main loop verified: machine_mvme88k.c:106 `unreadable&#x27; at 0xfff46000; if_ie.c:407-409 badpaddr match) that if_ie never attaches on this model, so the driver&#x27;s PCC2 writes never run; HIGH/FIRST rests on the measured rung-3 witness (guest st.b/ld.bu to IEICR -&gt; exit 1; put b does not reach the arm; 7/13 on the committed build, `regress/artifacts/pcc2defaultkill/witness_20260905/`) and severity.  Pass-1 panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260905_021751.  PASS-1 PANEL CONVERGED (2026-09-05, panel_20260905_021751 + Opus/Fable): READ side keep the served byte with a latched &#x27;served 0x%02x&#x27; announce (it is a default, not a computed value: not member 4); WRITE side MEMBER 3, DECLINE + per-offset latched fatal() (once), NOT backed accept (the file&#x27;s own cased siblings -- SCCERR write-to-clear, the ICRs mask+reassert, the read-only PSCALECNT/SPEED/IACKs -- contradict a blanket store; the addition licence is unmet; it would add a bounded write into pcctwo_reg[] reachable at 0x3d).  The Opus seat BUILT both and measured the 13-row witness 13/13 under BOTH -- disposition-blind -- so the detector owes a read-after-write row in ONE session (measured A 0x0 vs B 0x5a, cased-sibling control 0x0/0x0).  if_ie never attaches (unreadable at 0xfff46000; ie_setupram early return at if_ie.c:561-564); ie0 IS configured in conf/M187 and conf/NFS (the brief&#x27;s conf grep was wrong).  Unresolved: whether the real PCC2 folds 0x40..0xfff (no MVME manual on disk; fork PCC2_SIZE 0x40 vs guest header 0x1000) -- AT PASS 1; RESOLVED the same night: the manual was fetched at the codex seat&#x27;s citation and VMESBCP2 Table 6-1 lists $FFF42040-$FFF42FFF as &#x27;PCCchip2 Registers  Repeated&#x27; (vmesbcp2.txt:4629), so the fold is the real part&#x27;s.  Cross-round: fix pcc2c040kill BEFORE any round implements a PCC2 IACK arm (the SCCRXIACK arm runs on READS and the console driver reads it -- routine entry into the C040 path).  PASS-1 PANEL COMPLETE 9/9 (2026-09-05 02:17-03:27; codex via a re-worded copy after a provider refusal; three cloud seats via one re-fire after a concurrent-panel 429).  CONVERGED: fix A (Opus, measured) -- reads keep the served byte with a latched &#x27;served 0x%02x&#x27; announce, writes DECLINED with a per-offset latched fatal() (once); no backed accept; IACKs declined; the in-word bytes disclosed as a width matter.  NEW: the alias is DOCUMENTED (Motorola VMESBCP2 Table 6-1, codex); IEERR SCLR reads as zero (a store would fabricate status).  Implementation drafted (fix_pcc2_default.py + a 23-row detector reusing the width probe&#x27;s session()); ships after the width chain&#x27;s pass-13 commit.  PASS-2 PANEL COMPLETE 9/9 (2026-09-05 05:56-06:2x; kimi via one re-fire with the packet inline after the pointer form stalled; glm/deepseek via 2 KB mini packets after thinking burns).  VERDICTS: NOT CLOSE (codex, grok, Opus -- Opus MEASURED two 23/23 clean sweeps), GO-WITH-CHANGES provisional (Fable), PASS (kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax).  Count the arguments: six clause-(d)-shaped escapes of the 23-row detector -&gt; L1 class-closure repair in ONE diff (13 rows, 36; data[0] + width in the announces); table 3 = 12 mutants all killed, the six pass-2 ones 23/23 on the pass-2 instrument.  Pass 3 owed on the repair diff.  PASS-3 PANEL (panel_20260905_081718) on the repair diff: close (grok, deepseek, minimax), GO-WITH-CHANGES (Fable), NO-GO (codex: three derived escapes), NOT CLOSE (Opus: SIX MEASURED clean sweeps of the 36-row instrument, one the mirror of its own pass-2 M_O1 -- the #499 shape inside the repair); kimi derived two residuals and exited without a verdict line; glm SEAT FAILURE (thinking burn x2).  Class closure #2 in one diff (fix_pcc2_501c.py): ten rows appended to existing sessions (46), the write-announce value, the latched-path read response, a len-2 read/write, the width label at len 1, one count + one read-back per offset class; M_RELOAD disclosed as unrowable.  Table 4 = 22 mutants, the ten pass-3 ones also scored on the 36-row instrument.  L4s corrected in place: &#x27;875 checks&#x27; -&gt; the gate&#x27;s own 857; &#x27;23 rows&#x27; -&gt; 46 with history; four pass-1 sentences corrected in their own place; the latch-sameoff-3 miss recorded; the tick reason narrowed (COUNT words, while running; signal-driven per timer.c:284 -- Fable&#x27;s &#x27;between instructions&#x27; premise refuted by reading) with the end-span reason added; len 8 disclosed.  Pass 4 owed on the pass-3 repair diff.  PASS-4 PANEL (panel_20260905_112529) on the pass-3 repair diff: CLOSE (grok, deepseek, minimax; Fable GO-WITH-CHANGES); NOT CLOSE (codex derived, Opus MEASURED four 46/46 sweeps and graded them clause (e) itself, kimi MEASURED the len-2 write-announce sweep and graded it (d)); glm DO-NOT-CLOSE-YET on two width-text mirror cells.  All contested cells are len- or offset-scoped and neither operand is body-tested -&gt; clause (e), disclosed with row specs, filed pcc2anngrid.  Zero floor breaches surviving adjudication; the chain CLOSES on this pass.  Records-only corrections applied: the pass-3 write-value repair re-graded L2 (Fable: &#x27;uncommitted -&gt; L1&#x27; is the provenance exemption the repair-chain ruling rejects); the two mirror cells added to the exclusions; the contract&#x27;s &#x27;44/44&#x27; (a run that never existed) -&gt; 36/36 + 46/46; the stale announce-count sentence reworded.  #501 SHIPPED 2026-09-05: reads keep the served byte (announced as served, with the width), writes declined, per-offset uint64 latch; witness 7/13 -&gt; 13/13.  PASS 2 (panel_20260905_055656) found the 23-row detector BELOW THE FLOOR six ways -- Opus MEASURED two clean sweeps (announce value -&gt; 0; member 6 on the latched path), grok/codex/Fable predicted four -- and codex found the read announce reloading the array after the copy (the tick rewrites counter interiors).  Repaired in ONE diff (L1): data[0] + `(len %d)&#x27; in the announces, 13 rows (36), all 43 offsets driven both ways; table 3 = 12 mutants, the six pass-2 ones also measured on the pass-2 instrument.  Pass 3 panel_20260905_112529.  Filed from the round: pcc2iackmodel, pcc2latchinst.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-05 — MANUAL — The adjudicator&#x27;s hat (Agent).  SHIP: READ member 4-as-served (keep the byte, latched read announce -- gdrom announces reads too), WRITE member 3 (decline + latched announce), NOT member 6 -- the backed-accept warrant is hollow: the cited driver does not run here, and its uses are ICLR/SCLR acknowledge bits a store would retain.  FOUND FIRST (verified by the main loop: machine_mvme88k.c:106, if_ie.c:407-409): if_ie never attaches on this model, so the &#x27;in-tree driver writes these offsets&#x27; warrant behind the HIGH/FIRST ranking is a source reading contradicted by the machine description -- re-base on the rung-3 witness + severity (done).  Uniform disposition, no offset-range predicate; disclose the in-word bytes (a width matter, pcc2widecompose) and the IACKs (decline; file ack-modelling on the SCCRXIACK shape).  12 detector rows incl. R3 (write V, read back 0) pinning DECLINE vs BACKED and R10 per-offset announce count; announce shape: a per-offset bitmap, not the 32-bit width word.  Also: SCCRXIACK runs on WRITES too (no writeflag guard) -- pre-existing quirk. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260905_021751</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-05 — CONFIRMED — MEASURING seat (Agent).  Built BOTH candidate fixes in a scratch clone (cmp-clean vs SEC before mutant 1, .MUTANT sentinel, restored from SEC + rebuilt, both parts verified): baseline 7/13 (reproduces the committed witness), fix A (decline + per-offset latched fatal) 13/13, fix B (backed accept) 13/13 -- THE WITNESS IS DISPOSITION-BLIND (its read row has no preceding store).  Discriminator rows measured: st.b 0x5a then ld.bu at IEICR in one session reads 0x0 under A, 0x5a under B; same at IEERR; the CASED write-to-clear sibling SCCERR reads 0x0 under both (the control).  Premise 5 wrong in both directions: ie0 IS configured (conf/M187:99, conf/NFS:100 -- the brief&#x27;s conf grep was wrong) and the model BLOCKS it twice (unreadable at 0xfff46000 -&gt; iematch badpaddr fails; ie_setupram early return at if_ie.c:561-564 before the PCC2 store at :609).  L4: OUTSTANDING_BUGS&#x27;s &#x27;cheapest witness: put b&#x27; is MEASURED FALSE (put b never reaches the arm).  NEW for c040kill: SCCRXIACK&#x27;s arm has no writeflag test, so a guest LOAD of 0x27 enters reassert_interrupts(), and cl.c:1051/1234 (the console driver) reads that offset -- routine entry into the C040 path.  Warrants against member 6: the cased siblings&#x27; semantics (SCCERR clears on write; the ICRs mask + reassert; PSCALECNT/SPEED/IACKs read-only), the addition licence unmet, a bounded write into pcctwo_reg[] reachable at 0x3d.  Read side: &#x27;ACCEPT&#x27; would be a wrong record -- the served byte is a default, announce it as served.  PCC2_SIZE name collision: fork 0x40 vs the guest header 0x1000; the real fold unresolved (no MVME manual on disk at the time).  IACK offsets: do not backed-accept; modelling them opens two entries into the C040 kill -&gt; c040kill first.  Candidate diff A supplied (adopted). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260905_021751</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-09-05 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING (GPT-6 Astra, max).  FIRST FIRING REFUSED by the provider&#x27;s content filter on the brief&#x27;s &#x27;kill&#x27; vocabulary (kept as codex.refused.txt); RE-FIRED ONCE with a re-worded copy (brief_defaultexit_p1.codex.md: &#x27;kill&#x27; -&gt; &#x27;exit&#x27;, facts unchanged) -- answered 14.6 KB after a web-search reconnect storm.  VERDICT: remove the exit, preserve prepared reads, member 3 for writes.  NEW FACTS: (1) the real aliasing IS documented -- Motorola VMESBCP2 Table 6-1 lists $FFF42040-$FFF42FFF as repeated PCCchip2 registers (m88k.com/Docs/172/vmesbcp2.p