tes before and after the edit, indentation-tolerant -- unchanged**, both L4s land inside the module DOCSTRING, and the gate&#x27;s EQUALITY pin at 62 is untouched.  No kill table is perturbed. *Two further correlate slips in that same guard, recorded because they are this session&#x27;s pattern: my &#x27;row sites 54, was 56&#x27; alarm compared `^row\(&#x27; against an earlier `^\s*row\(&#x27; -- two REGEXES, not a lost row; and my &#x27;non-comment lines&#x27; count keyed on `#&#x27;, while the contract block is a docstring whose prose never starts with one.* — evidence: regress/gdrom_default_probe.py contract block; regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh pin unchanged at 62</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">highrankunmeasured</span><span class="ttl">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun</span><span class="tip">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun — witness: none -- documented-only.  ANSWERED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR on both sides: armmsr&#x27;s reachability by mode enumeration (7 handled, 25 unhandled, none producible by a correct in-tree kernel), and ip30&#x27;s by gate_asan_sweep.sh:593 running ip30 green.  The ruling is ip30abortkill first. — note: *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently. — evidence: gate_asan_sweep.sh:593; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py (built, unrun); ledger witness fields</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).** — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/REACH_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/cpu_arm.c:559,606; cpu_arm.h mode names</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probedeathoracle</span><span class="ttl">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day</span><span class="tip">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day — witness: none -- documented-only, and the CLOSURE IS THE RESULT: a census with its denominator (88 live launchers, 5 with WNOHANG, 1 sampling once, 0 using waitpid-once as the survive oracle) bounding a class rather than leaving it open. — note: *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/probedeathoracle_20260917/CENSUS_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">grokampkill</span><span class="ttl">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count</span><span class="tip">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count — witness: harness:mtime on the redirect target -- 3 of 5 jobs unwritten for 338-675 s while 2 were written within 80 s, plus a partial ending mid-sentence with no verdict line. — note: *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.* — evidence: _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_lunadefault.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_maplebound.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_cpusweep.STOPPED_partial.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">scorehyphenfp</span><span class="ttl">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe</span><span class="tip">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe — witness: harness:check_score_claims.py controlled in both directions -- 8 real score claims still match, 6 non-scores rejected; SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS at 30 pinned probes — note: *** FIXED 2026-09-17 BY OWNER DECISION, WITH A SIBLING AUDIT RATHER THAN A ONE-INSTANCE PATCH. ***  `check_score_claims.py`&#x27;s `SCORE` regex was `(?&lt;![\dx/])(\d+)\s*/\s*(\d+)(?![\d/%])` -- a lookbehind excluding a digit, `x` and `/` **but not a hyphen** -- so the ORDINAL PAIR `entries-4/8` was reported as a stale score claim of 4/8 against a pin of 188. *** THE REASON THIS WAS NOT LEFT ALONE: THE CHECKER&#x27;S OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A FALSE STATEMENT. ***  Its message reads *&quot;if that is a deliberate historical quote, write &#x27;8-row&#x27; on the same line&quot;* -- and on a line about ARRAY ENTRIES that silences the check by writing something untrue into a probe.  **A check whose repair corrupts the thing it guards** is the shape the carrier already records for the build-tree clearance recipe, arrived at from a new direction. **THE AUDIT, with a denominator, because a correlate-keyed matcher usually has more than one victim:** 710 N/M matches across 121 `regress/` files; the hyphen-aware form keeps **706** and drops exactly **FOUR**, every one an ordinal pair and none a score -- `entries-4/8` (`crime_width_probe.py`), `len-3/5` (`lca_isalane_probe.py` comment 96), `opcode-59/63` (`ppc_halt_probe.py` comment 238), `pass 2-3 / 6-7` (`sgi_de_primop_probe.py` comment 985).  *** THREE OF THE FOUR WERE LATENT: they fire only once their probe gains a gate pin, so the class had four members and one visible face. *** **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE LANDING:** eight real score claims still match (13/13, 26/26, 8/32, `52 / 52`, 16/16, `scored 27/29`, `base 29/29`, `RESULT=6/9`); six non-scores rejected (the four hyphen pairs plus the two pre-existing hex lists).  `SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS` at 30 pinned probes. *The fix is one character in a lookbehind.  What made it worth a round is that the audit converted an anecdote into a population -- and the population was four.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** FIXED 2026-09-17 BY OWNER DECISION, WITH A SIBLING AUDIT RATHER THAN A ONE-INSTANCE PATCH. ***  `check_score_claims.py`&#x27;s `SCORE` regex was `(?&lt;![\dx/])(\d+)\s*/\s*(\d+)(?![\d/%])` -- a lookbehind excluding a digit, `x` and `/` **but not a hyphen** -- so the ORDINAL PAIR `entries-4/8` was reported as a stale score claim of 4/8 against a pin of 188. *** THE REASON THIS WAS NOT LEFT ALONE: THE CHECKER&#x27;S OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A FALSE STATEMENT. ***  Its message reads *&quot;if that is a deliberate historical quote, write &#x27;8-row&#x27; on the same line&quot;* -- and on a line about ARRAY ENTRIES that silences the check by writing something untrue into a probe.  **A check whose repair corrupts the thing it guards** is the shape the carrier already records for the build-tree clearance recipe, arrived at from a new direction. **THE AUDIT, with a denominator, because a correlate-keyed matcher usually has more than one victim:** 710 N/M matches across 121 `regress/` files; the hyphen-aware form keeps **706** and drops exactly **FOUR**, every one an ordinal pair and none a score -- `entries-4/8` (`crime_width_probe.py`), `len-3/5` (`lca_isalane_probe.py` comment 96), `opcode-59/63` (`ppc_halt_probe.py` comment 238), `pass 2-3 / 6-7` (`sgi_de_primop_probe.py` comment 985).  *** THREE OF THE FOUR WERE LATENT: they fire only once their probe gains a gate pin, so the class had four members and one visible face. *** **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE LANDING:** eight real score claims still match (13/13, 26/26, 8/32, `52 / 52`, 16/16, `scored 27/29`, `base 29/29`, `RESULT=6/9`); six non-scores rejected (the four hyphen pairs plus the two pre-existing hex lists).  `SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS` at 30 pinned probes. *The fix is one character in a lookbehind.  What made it worth a round is that the audit converted an anecdote into a population -- and the population was four.* — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_score_claims.py; 710-match sibling audit over regress/*.py and *.sh</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">boilerplateprefix</span><span class="ttl">check_boilerplate.py keyed on note[:60], so it clustered on the opening sentence -- 20 of 64 clusters were false positives AND it hid ten genuine stamps by merging them into mixed buckets</span><span class="tip">check_boilerplate.py keyed on note[:60], so it clustered on the opening sentence -- 20 of 64 clusters were false positives AND it hid ten genuine stamps by merging them into mixed buckets — witness: harness:check_boilerplate.py controlled in both directions -- BOILERPLATE_PASS with 7 false positives cleared and 54 genuine stamps retained; an injected 4-seat identical note post-cutoff reds it and names the row; removal restores green — note: *** `check_boilerplate.py` COMPARED THE FIRST 60 CHARACTERS OF A SEAT NOTE, SO IT CLUSTERED ON THE OPENING SENTENCE RATHER THAN ON THE NOTE.  Fixed 2026-09-17 by owner decision, with the audit run first. ***  Cells that carried each seat&#x27;s OWN finding but shared an identifying preamble -- `LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431).` is 66 characters before the first seat writes a word -- were reported as boilerplate **while satisfying this check&#x27;s own stated rule**, which asks for *&quot;each seat its own sentence from its own file&quot;*.  They had their own sentence. **MEASURED OVER EVERY CLUSTER IN THE LEDGER, BOTH KEYS, SAME RUN:**  OLD `note[:60]` -- 64 clusters, 44 genuine, **20 NOTES DIFFER**; NEW full note -- 54 clusters, **54 genuine, 0 notes differ**. *** IT FAILED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THE SECOND HALF IS THE SURPRISE: GENUINE STAMPS ROSE 44 -&gt; 54. ***  A 60-character bucket holding a real stamp ALONGSIDE other notes scored as one mixed cluster, so **the prefix key was HIDING TEN REAL STAMPS BY MERGING THEM**.  Truncation cost recall as well as precision; it never bought either. **ALL SEVEN LIVE REDS WERE FALSE**, each with seven genuinely distinct notes -- so section O was blocking commits on nothing. **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** `BOILERPLATE_PASS` with the seven gone; an INJECTED identical paragraph across four seat cells post-cutoff turns it red and names the row; removing the injection restores green. *** AND A HARNESS BUG OF MINE IS RECORDED IN THE FILE, because it produced a wrong number first: `shared_notes` returns its key with NEWLINES REPLACED BY SPACES for display, so a verification that compares a recomputed key against the RETURNED one silently matches nothing for any note containing a newline -- and then reports the cluster as non-identical. ***  It made one cluster (`wdcnoirq`) look like a survivor when the fix had worked.  *Recompute the bucket; never compare against the display key.*  **Third time this session that a check of mine keyed on a correlate of the thing rather than the thing.**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `check_boilerplate.py` COMPARED THE FIRST 60 CHARACTERS OF A SEAT NOTE, SO IT CLUSTERED ON THE OPENING SENTENCE RATHER THAN ON THE NOTE.  Fixed 2026-09-17 by owner decision, with the audit run first. ***  Cells that carried each seat&#x27;s OWN finding but shared an identifying preamble -- `LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431).` is 66 characters before the first seat writes a word -- were reported as boilerplate **while satisfying this check&#x27;s own stated rule**, which asks for *&quot;each seat its own sentence from its own file&quot;*.  They had their own sentence. **MEASURED OVER EVERY CLUSTER IN THE LEDGER, BOTH KEYS, SAME RUN:**  OLD `note[:60]` -- 64 clusters, 44 genuine, **20 NOTES DIFFER**; NEW full note -- 54 clusters, **54 genuine, 0 notes differ**. *** IT FAILED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THE SECOND HALF IS THE SURPRISE: GENUINE STAMPS ROSE 44 -&gt; 54. ***  A 60-character bucket holding a real stamp ALONGSIDE other notes scored as one mixed cluster, so **the prefix key was HIDING TEN REAL STAMPS BY MERGING THEM**.  Truncation cost recall as well as precision; it never bought either. **ALL SEVEN LIVE REDS WERE FALSE**, each with seven genuinely distinct notes -- so section O was blocking commits on nothing. **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:** `BOILERPLATE_PASS` with the seven gone; an INJECTED identical paragraph across four seat cells post-cutoff turns it red and names the row; removing the injection restores green. *** AND A HARNESS BUG OF MINE IS RECORDED IN THE FILE, because it produced a wrong number first: `shared_notes` returns its key with NEWLINES REPLACED BY SPACES for display, so a verification that compares a recomputed key against the RETURNED one silently matches nothing for any note containing a newline -- and then reports the cluster as non-identical. ***  It made one cluster (`wdcnoirq`) look like a survivor when the fix had worked.  *Recompute the bucket; never compare against the display key.*  **Third time this session that a check of mine keyed on a correlate of the thing rather than the thing.** — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_boilerplate.py; 64-cluster two-key census over the ledger</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">wiringshghost</span><span class="ttl">check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s manifest named three .sh paths while its domain() is .py-only by construction -- three permanently unsatisfiable GHOST rows holding the pre-commit gate red</span><span class="tip">check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s manifest named three .sh paths while its domain() is .py-only by construction -- three permanently unsatisfiable GHOST rows holding the pre-commit gate red — note: *** A CHECK THAT CANNOT PASS -- the mirror of the check-that-cannot-fail, and it had been holding the pre-commit gate RED. ***  check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s MANIFEST named three `.sh&#x27; paths (opus_close7_20260915/scripts2/{laneP,laneQ,mkfreeze}.sh, CLASS_TOOL, #529&#x27;s lane runners and instrument freezer).  Its `domain()&#x27; is `.py&#x27;-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION: it runs `git ls-files -- &quot;*.py&quot;&#x27; and filters `f.endswith(&quot;.py&quot;)&#x27; in two further places.  A `.sh&#x27; path can therefore NEVER enter the domain, so a `.sh&#x27; manifest entry reports GHOST *whatever its git state*.  MEASURED, which is how it was found rather than reasoned: all three were `git add&#x27;ed, `git ls-files&#x27; listed all three, and the checker still called them ghosts -- while the FIVE `.py&#x27; files in the SAME DIRECTORY cleared.  That asymmetry is the tell.  FIXED by DELETING the three entries and preserving their descriptions as a comment in place.  The alternative -- widening domain() to `.sh&#x27; -- was rejected on a stated measurement risk: every unclassified shell driver under regress/ would go newly red, a flood this check was never scoped for.  TWO CONTROLS, both run: (1) the checker goes 3 GHOSTs -&gt; 0 and PROBEWIRING_PASS; (2) the three files remain TRACKED, so the record is preserved and only an undischargeable obligation was removed.  A backup of the pre-edit checker is at /tmp/cpw.before.py for this session.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** A CHECK THAT CANNOT PASS -- the mirror of the check-that-cannot-fail, and it had been holding the pre-commit gate RED. ***  check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s MANIFEST named three `.sh&#x27; paths (opus_close7_20260915/scripts2/{laneP,laneQ,mkfreeze}.sh, CLASS_TOOL, #529&#x27;s lane runners and instrument freezer).  Its `domain()&#x27; is `.py&#x27;-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION: it runs `git ls-files -- &quot;*.py&quot;&#x27; and filters `f.endswith(&quot;.py&quot;)&#x27; in two further places.  A `.sh&#x27; path can therefore NEVER enter the domain, so a `.sh&#x27; manifest entry reports GHOST *whatever its git state*.  MEASURED, which is how it was found rather than reasoned: all three were `git add&#x27;ed, `git ls-files&#x27; listed all three, and the checker still called them ghosts -- while the FIVE `.py&#x27; files in the SAME DIRECTORY cleared.  That asymmetry is the tell.  FIXED by DELETING the three entries and preserving their descriptions as a comment in place.  The alternative -- widening domain() to `.sh&#x27; -- was rejected on a stated measurement risk: every unclassified shell driver under regress/ would go newly red, a flood this check was never scoped for.  TWO CONTROLS, both run: (1) the checker goes 3 GHOSTs -&gt; 0 and PROBEWIRING_PASS; (2) the three files remain TRACKED, so the record is preserved and only an undischargeable obligation was removed.  A backup of the pre-edit checker is at /tmp/cpw.before.py for this session. — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py; PROBEWIRING_PASS after the edit</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">unreadseats23</span><span class="ttl">TWENTY-THREE real seat answers (~400 KB) across six panels of 2026-08-27 and 2026-09-07 are recorded nowhere and have never been read</span><span class="tip">TWENTY-THREE real seat answers (~400 KB) across six panels of 2026-08-27 and 2026-09-07 are recorded nowhere and have never been read — note: Split out from the gate&#x27;s section-G count once the two THINKING BURNS were separated (see `seatsreadburnsplit&#x27;).  These 23 are REAL ANSWERS with non-empty response fields.  *** THIS PROJECT HAS NOW FOUND REAL DEFECTS IN AN UNREAD REVIEW FOUR TIMES -- most recently on 2026-09-18, when grok&#x27;s FIXDIFF prescription of `isr 28 rd val&#x27; turned out to have been written down before #531 shipped and cost an extra kill table to rediscover. ***  The single largest is panel_20260907_184039/ollama_glm at 243,346 chars, which is notable twice over: panel_ollama.py DETECTED a thinking burn on the first shot and re-fired with think=False to recover a 121 KB real answer -- so the tooling did its job and the answer was still never opened.  TRIAGED, NOT READ: this row exists because the 25-count was measured and split, not because the content was reviewed.  Reading them is the work.  Panels: 20260827_050852 (4), 20260827_134019 (5), 20260907_051055 (4), 20260907_104814 (3), 20260907_184039 (4), 20260907_221353 (3).  *** FOUR UNREAD SEAT ANSWERS FROM _scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055 READ 2026-09-18 -- the FIRST of the 23 to be opened, chosen because this panel is cited by a LIVE HIGH row. ***  They contain real content, which is the fifth time this project has found something in an unread review.  (1) *** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY DERIVED THAT `+&#x27; -&gt; `|&#x27; IS AN UNCATCHABLE NULL MUTANT in the RGB pack: every term is a uint8_t shifted into DISJOINT bit ranges, so sum == OR always. ***  glm: &#x27;No test can or should catch it.&#x27;  kimi: &#x27;Uncatchable by construction.&#x27;  minimax: &#x27;NOT caught by behavioral tests.&#x27;  That is an EQUIVALENT mutant and therefore a RESULT, not a coverage gap -- the same disposition #531 reached for its latch-macro renumbering.  Three independent derivations is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence.  (2) glm AND kimi independently named a CLEAN-SWEEP mutant: the MIRROR EDIT IN THE PUT ARM (make PUT write [0,R,G,B]).  It round-trips perfectly against the corrected GET, so every round-trip row passes while the on-the-wire byte order is inverted.  A clause-(d) concern for whatever detector shipped.  (3) deepseek challenged a MEASURED VALUE and the resolution is PATH-DEPENDENT, which is worth more than either claim: it derived that an RGB PUT-&gt;GET round-trip on 0xaabbccdd yields **0xbbcc0000**, not the 0xbbccdd00 the records carry.  BOTH ARE CORRECT FOR DIFFERENT PATHS, verified here against dev_sgi_re.c: a COPY reads its source as four REAL bytes from guest memory (buf = {aa,bb,cc,dd}) so GET-reads-buf[1..3] gives 0xbbccdd00; a PUT-&gt;GET round-trip writes an explicit PAD at buf[3]=0 (the PUT arm sets it) so the same GET gives 0xbbcc0000.  **The defect, if any, is a record that quotes one path&#x27;s value while describing the other&#x27;s** -- NOT verified here which way #505&#x27;s brief put it, and deliberately NOT asserted.  Filed for checking.  (4) CORROBORATION OF TWO OPEN ROWS, from seats that had never been read: glm found &#x27;no arm initializes buf[4..7] -- a bufdepth-8 PUT transmits four uninitialised bytes&#x27; (= `sgirebufuninit&#x27;), and kimi found &#x27;all three 32-bit arms pack/unpack four bytes regardless of bufdepth, which can be 1, 2, 4 or 8&#x27; (= `sgirebufshiftub&#x27;).  kimi also independently re-found the YCRCB default writing four bytes of host random() (= `sgirandomcolour&#x27;, HIGH).  *** PROGRESS: 4 of 23 READ (panel_20260907_051055). NINETEEN REMAIN. ***  *** AN UNREAD REVIEW IS TIME-INDEXED, AND READING ONE LATE CAN MANUFACTURE PHANTOM WORK AS EASILY AS IT RECOVERS A DEFECT.  Measured 2026-09-18 on the second panel opened. ***  panel_20260907_184039: TWO seats independently (kimi and glm) flagged #507&#x27;s comment claim &#x27;here and three lines up&#x27; as FALSE, and glm named the actual lines it should cite.  glm went further -- &#x27;Q2: not shipped: #507 as written (comment)&#x27;.  A live L4 in a closed round, found by two seats, in an answer nobody had opened.  *** IT WAS ALREADY FIXED. ***  cpu_alpha_palcode.c now reads &#x27;four lines above&#x27; and carries its own correction note: &#x27;&quot;three lines up&quot; until 2026-09-07&#x27; -- the SAME DAY as that panel.  Verified here: `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr&#x27; is at :296 and the #507 comment block begins at :300, so FOUR is right and THREE was wrong.  The seats were correct when they wrote it and the defect was discharged, almost certainly by a sibling answer that WAS read.  *** THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE REMAINING 19, AND IT CHANGES HOW THEY MUST BE PROCESSED: these answers are up to THREE WEEKS OLD (2026-08-27 and 2026-09-07).  Every finding in them must be RE-CHECKED AGAINST THE CURRENT TREE before it is recorded as live.  The unread-review hazard is usually stated as &#x27;you miss real defects&#x27;; its mirror is &#x27;you re-raise fixed ones&#x27;, and a phantom row costs a round. ***  *Method for the rest, therefore: read for findings, then CHECK each against HEAD before filing -- exactly as the #507 claim was checked here rather than recorded on two seats&#x27; agreement.*  ALSO CAPTURED from that panel, still to check: kimi&#x27;s #506 `relative_addr&#x27; question, glm&#x27;s four per-round &#x27;single settling fact&#x27; items, and minimax&#x27;s observation on #508 that `return 2&#x27; reports every walked page writable.  *** PROGRESS: 8 of 23 READ.  FIFTEEN REMAIN. ***  *** THREE MORE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260907_104814; its glm is one of the two THINKING BURNS and has nothing to read).  THE TIME-INDEXING LESSON LANDED A SECOND TIME AND MORE SHARPLY. ***  deepseek&#x27;s Q6 claimed the gate&#x27;s `^$v  .*ok$&#x27; row pattern CANNOT MATCH, because the probe pads `%-16s&#x27; plus a space so only a 15-char name leaves exactly two.  **MEASURED FALSE HERE**: the `.*&#x27; after the two literal spaces absorbs the extra padding, so every name of &lt;= 15 chars matches -- `rgb chan&#x27; (9 trailing spaces) matches True; only a name of 16+ fails.  kimi, in the SAME unread panel, had already corrected deepseek: an unsatisfiable pattern makes `count&#x27; return 0 against a wanted 1 and FAILS LOUDLY rather than greening.  *** AND gate_arm.sh ALREADY CARRIES THAT CORRECTION IN ITS OWN COMMENT, measured at name lengths 15/16/17, INCLUDING the note that an earlier draft had copied the wrong &#x27;silently stops testing while staying green&#x27; sentence from a sibling block and that the pass-2 panel caught it. ***  So the gate was already right and already knew.  **Had I recorded deepseek&#x27;s claim as a finding I would have filed a false defect against a gate that documents the opposite, with a measurement.**  Checked instead of recorded, per the method this row adopted after the #507 case.  WHAT SURVIVES, none of it yet checked against HEAD and all of it flagged as such: (1) kimi -- *** THE GATE BLOCK GREPS STRINGS THE PROBE ITSELF PRINTED (row verdicts, SGIRGB_WITNESS=PASS), so it validates the probe&#x27;s SELF-REPORT rather than independent behaviour -- &#x27;the probe grades its own homework&#x27;. ***  Real, but ARCHITECTURAL: it is how every probe-backed gate in this tree works, and the mitigation is that the probe&#x27;s rows are themselves measured against mutants.  Worth a row of its own rather than a #531 rider.  (2) deepseek -- CONTRACT OVERCLAIM: the row set has `rgb chan&#x27; (GET@4), `rgb depth64&#x27; (GET@8) and `rgb samemode&#x27; (PUT@4), and NO row for PUT@8, while the contract claims &#x27;both directions at widths 4 and 8&#x27;.  Its M9_put8 mutant corrupts the RGB PUT arm only when bufdepth == 8 and is predicted to sweep clean.  CHECKABLE.  (3) kimi -- `rgb not corrupt&#x27; is an INEQUALITY against one specific value (0xbbccdd00), so it greens on ANY other wrong value, including a different corruption.  (4) kimi -- value-conditioned and location-conditioned mutants; and NO ABGR row exists, so a reader assuming sibling coverage would be wrong.  *** PROGRESS: 11 of 23 READ.  TWELVE REMAIN. ***  *** FOUR MORE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260827_050852).  THIS ONE IS A RATIFICATION, AND THAT IS RECORDED EXPLICITLY RATHER THAN LEFT BLANK -- the gate&#x27;s own rule: if a seat truly had nothing new to say, record THAT, because a blank cell and a seat failure must not look alike. ***  agy: VERDICT GO, &#x27;all premises, calculations, diff hunks and test oracles are verified against the repository&#x27;.  glm, minimax and deepseek concur.  NO live defect found, and none of the four raised a floor or coverage breach.  *** THE ONE SUBSTANTIVE CONVERGENCE, reached INDEPENDENTLY by agy and deepseek, and it is a warrant the round should keep: THE TWO DEFECTS WERE NOT SEPARABLE AND SHIPPING THEM TOGETHER WAS MANDATORY. ***  Bounding the initial push WITHOUT the re-arm refill would have made every transfer in (32768, 65536] worse than pre-fix: the initial push queues only 32768, the guest drains it, atapi_len goes 0, the re-arm then sets atapi_len = remaining while THE RING IS EMPTY -- so the guest is told bytes remain and none do.  That band WORKED pre-fix by accident (the whole transfer was pushed on the initial arm), so a half-fix would have INJECTED corruption into a working band.  glm&#x27;s one nit, a RECORDS point: the commit message states the re-arm fix is for the &gt;32768 remaining case and separately that bounding is for the &gt;262656 wrap, which reads as two independent fixes when the coupling is tighter than that.  Commit messages are immutable history, so this is noted rather than edited.  ALL FOUR independently confirmed the round&#x27;s SCOPE BLOCK is honest: a rung-1 direct-call harness is a DETECTOR and never a witness; only the FIRST re-arm is pinned; wdc_addtoinbuf() remains unbounded (filed); and *** wdcrearmzero STILL OWES A RUNG-3/4 WITNESS *** -- which is the one actionable item this panel leaves, and it is why the row is HELD rather than closed.  *** PROGRESS: 15 of 23 READ.  EIGHT REMAIN (panel_20260827_134019 x5 -&gt; wdcaltctrlbit [closed]; panel_20260907_221353 x3 -&gt; lcashiftub [closed/low]). ***  *** THREE MORE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260907_221353), AND THEY CONVERGED ON ONE SETTLING FACT THAT NOBODY EVER LOOKED UP -- ONE GREP, ELEVEN DAYS UNREAD. ***  glm, kimi and minimax independently named THE SAME single missing fact as the only thing standing between the round and a clean pass: THE DECLARED TYPE OF `tag&#x27; in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf).  kimi put it plainest: &#x27;What settles Q1 (the only open technical question): one line -- the declaration of `tag&#x27;.&#x27;  *** SETTLED 2026-09-18 BY READING IT: `int tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;&#x27; -- a plain int. ***  AND THE CONSEQUENCE, derived rather than asserted: in the fixed expression `tag &amp; (0x800U &lt;&lt; dev)&#x27; the UNSIGNED operand forces `tag&#x27; to be converted to unsigned int by the usual arithmetic conversions, so the AND is evaluated in unsigned and its TRUTH VALUE is identical to the pre-fix expression&#x27;s.  The fix removes the signed-overflow UB (`0x800 &lt;&lt; 20&#x27; on a plain int is 2^31, which does not fit) WITHOUT changing behaviour on any real target.  **THE ROUND&#x27;S UNIVERSAL NO-CHANGE CLAIM HOLDS**, and it is now settled by a reading rather than resting on an unverified premise -- which is what all three seats asked for.  OTHER POINTS THEY RAISED, none of them live defects: glm flagged P3 as &#x27;the round&#x27;s weakest settled premise -- asserted, uninlined, and precisely the shape seven seats ratified last time&#x27; (the premise-audit class, correctly identified); kimi noted a DUPLICATE CROSS-REFERENCE (the trailing /* #511 */ on the fixed line plus the citation inside the block -- keep one) and that the control&#x27;s 3/4 invariant score should be quoted in the `ubsanshiftlane&#x27; row as evidence; minimax noted `0x800U &lt;&lt; dev&#x27; is well-defined only if unsigned int is at least 32 bits, true on every host this project targets.  *** PROGRESS: 18 of 23 READ.  FIVE REMAIN (panel_20260827_134019, -&gt; wdcaltctrlbit [closed]). ***  *** THE LAST FIVE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260827_134019).  23 OF 23 NOW READ -- the section-G backlog is CLEARED. ***  This was a pass-1 DESIGN panel on a now-CLOSED row, so it was read for the question `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; exists to ask: DID THE PRESCRIPTIONS SURVIVE INTO THE SHIPPED WORK?  *** AND THE ANSWER IS YES -- A NEGATIVE RESULT THAT BOUNDS THE ROW I FILED THIS MORNING. ***  agy prescribed three items.  (1) the predicate fix -- SHIPPED, dev_wdc.c now triggers on WDCTL_RST (0x04) and not WDCTL_4BIT (0x08), with the rising-edge `prev_ctl&#x27; machinery and a recorded brace hazard beside it.  (2) the MACHINE_LANDISK alt-status mapping -- NOT in dev_wdc.c, and NOT a silent drop: it was SPLIT INTO ITS OWN ROW `landiskaux&#x27;, which is open, and OUTSTANDING_BUGS carries the coupling in full.  (3) acceptance rows -- folded, per the kimi/deepseek rulings in the same panel.  *** SO `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; HAS A POPULATION OF ONE (the #531 case), NOT A PATTERN.  A class filed from a single instance is a hypothesis, and this is the first test of it -- it did not generalise.  Recorded as such rather than left looking systemic. ***  AND THE COUPLING WAS HANDLED IN THE SAFE ORDER, which is worth keeping because the unsafe one was live: mapping the landisk register WHILE the predicate still triggered on 0x08 would have latched COMMAND_RESET on SIX routine Device Control writes per probe (a seat measured twelve writes, six of them 0x08, currently all landing on unmapped memory).  The round fixed the PREDICATE first, so `wdcaltctrlbit&#x27; is CLOSED as overtaken and `landiskaux&#x27; is now OPEN AND UNBLOCKED.  *** THAT MAKES `landiskaux&#x27; A ONE-LINE FIX THAT IS NOW SAFE TO TAKE -- a strong critical-and-easily-resolvable candidate for the next round. ***  The other seats&#x27; content was design judgement, no live defects: glm listed SEVEN uninlined premises and ranked them by load-bearingness (the premise-audit discipline working as intended); minimax named an option-set hole (C4/B5, &#x27;the brief&#x27;s C-options jump&#x27;); deepseek ruled that a channel reset must clear in-flight transfer, FIFO, phase and interrupt state but NOT per-device-life diagnostics like `warned_*&#x27; -- and dev_wdc.c carries 43 `warned_&#x27; references, so that distinction is load-bearing there.  *** CLOSED 2026-09-18: ALL 23 READ.  Two were already-fixed or outright false when checked against HEAD, which is why the method was read-THEN-check; one settled an open question three seats had named (the `tag&#x27; type); one was a ratification recorded explicitly rather than left blank. ***</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — 23 real answers, ~400 KB, none read.  Two burns separated out and recorded as SEAT DOWN rather than as unread reviews. — evidence: scratchpad/precommit_b.out section G; the six panel dirs</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** THE LAST FIVE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260827_134019).  23 OF 23 NOW READ -- the section-G backlog is CLEARED. ***  This was a pass-1 DESIGN panel on a now-CLOSED row, so it was read for the question `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; exists to ask: DID THE PRESCRIPTIONS SURVIVE INTO THE SHIPPED WORK?  *** AND THE ANSWER IS YES -- A NEGATIVE RESULT THAT BOUNDS THE ROW I FILED THIS MORNING. ***  agy prescribed three items.  (1) the predicate fix -- SHIPPED, dev_wdc.c now triggers on WDCTL_RST (0x04) and not WDCTL_4BIT (0x08), with the rising-edge `prev_ctl&#x27; machinery and a recorded brace hazard beside it.  (2) the MACHINE_LANDISK alt-status mapping -- NOT in dev_wdc.c, and NOT a silent drop: it was SPLIT INTO ITS OWN ROW `landiskaux&#x27;, which is open, and OUTSTANDING_BUGS carries the coupling in full.  (3) acceptance rows -- folded, per the kimi/deepseek rulings in the same panel.  *** SO `seatprescriptiondiff&#x27; HAS A POPULATION OF ONE (the #531 case), NOT A PATTERN.  A class filed from a single instance is a hypothesis, and this is the first test of it -- it did not generalise.  Recorded as such rather than left looking systemic. ***  AND THE COUPLING WAS HANDLED IN THE SAFE ORDER, which is worth keeping because the unsafe one was live: mapping the landisk register WHILE the predicate still triggered on 0x08 would have latched COMMAND_RESET on SIX routine Device Control writes per probe (a seat measured twelve writes, six of them 0x08, currently all landing on unmapped memory).  The round fixed the PREDICATE first, so `wdcaltctrlbit&#x27; is CLOSED as overtaken and `landiskaux&#x27; is now OPEN AND UNBLOCKED.  *** THAT MAKES `landiskaux&#x27; A ONE-LINE FIX THAT IS NOW SAFE TO TAKE -- a strong critical-and-easily-resolvable candidate for the next round. ***  The other seats&#x27; content was design judgement, no live defects: glm listed SEVEN uninlined premises and ranked them by load-bearingness (the premise-audit discipline working as intended); minimax named an option-set hole (C4/B5, &#x27;the brief&#x27;s C-options jump&#x27;); deepseek ruled that a channel reset must clear in-flight transfer, FIFO, phase and interrupt state but NOT per-device-life diagnostics like `warned_*&#x27; -- and dev_wdc.c carries 43 `warned_&#x27; references, so that distinction is load-bearing there. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260827_134019/*.txt; src/devices/dev_wdc.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:12574-12600</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** THREE MORE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 (panel_20260907_221353), AND THEY CONVERGED ON ONE SETTLING FACT THAT NOBODY EVER LOOKED UP -- ONE GREP, ELEVEN DAYS UNREAD. ***  glm, kimi and minimax independently named THE SAME single missing fact as the only thing standing between the round and a clean pass: THE DECLARED TYPE OF `tag&#x27; in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf).  kimi put it plainest: &#x27;What settles Q1 (the only open technical question): one line -- the declaration of `tag&#x27;.&#x27;  *** SETTLED 2026-09-18 BY READING IT: `int tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;&#x27; -- a plain int. ***  AND THE CONSEQUENCE, derived rather than asserted: in the fixed expression `tag &amp; (0x800U &lt;&lt; dev)&#x27; the UNSIGNED operand forces `tag&#x27; to be converted to unsigned int by the usual arithmetic conversions, so the AND is evaluated in unsigned and its TRUTH VALUE is identical to the pre-fix expression&#x27;s.  The fix removes the signed-overflow UB (`0x800 &lt;&lt; 20&#x27; on a plain int is 2^31, which does not fit) WITHOUT changing behaviour on any real target.  **THE ROUND&#x27;S UNIVERSAL NO-CHANGE CLAIM HOLDS**, and it is now settled by a reading rather than resting on an unverified premise -- which is what all three seats asked for.  OTHER POINTS THEY RAISED, none of them live defects: glm flagged P3 as &#x27;the round&#x27;s weakest settled premise -- asserted, uninlined, and precisely the shape seven seats ratified last time&#x27; (the premise-audit class, correctly identified); kimi noted a DUPLICATE CROSS-REFERENCE (the trailing /* #511 */ on the fixed line plus the citation inside the block -- keep one) and that the control&#x27;s 3/4 invariant score should be quoted in the `ubsanshiftlane&#x27; row as evidence; minimax noted `0x800U &lt;&lt; dev&#x27; is well-defined only if unsigned int is at least 32 bits, true on every host this project targets.  *** PROGRESS: 18 of 23 READ.  FIVE REMAIN (panel_20260827_134019, -&gt; wdcaltctrlbit [closed]). *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260907_221353/ollama_glm.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_221353/ollama_kimi.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_221353/ollama_minimax.txt; src/devices/dev_lca.c (int tag...; tag &amp; (0x800U &lt;&lt; dev))</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** AN UNREAD REVIEW IS TIME-INDEXED, AND READING ONE LATE CAN MANUFACTURE PHANTOM WORK AS EASILY AS IT RECOVERS A DEFECT.  Measured 2026-09-18 on the second panel opened. ***  panel_20260907_184039: TWO seats independently (kimi and glm) flagged #507&#x27;s comment claim &#x27;here and three lines up&#x27; as FALSE, and glm named the actual lines it should cite.  glm went further -- &#x27;Q2: not shipped: #507 as written (comment)&#x27;.  A live L4 in a closed round, found by two seats, in an answer nobody had opened.  *** IT WAS ALREADY FIXED. ***  cpu_alpha_palcode.c now reads &#x27;four lines above&#x27; and carries its own correction note: &#x27;&quot;three lines up&quot; until 2026-09-07&#x27; -- the SAME DAY as that panel.  Verified here: `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr&#x27; is at :296 and the #507 comment block begins at :300, so FOUR is right and THREE was wrong.  The seats were correct when they wrote it and the defect was discharged, almost certainly by a sibling answer that WAS read.  *** THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE REMAINING 19, AND IT CHANGES HOW THEY MUST BE PROCESSED: these answers are up to THREE WEEKS OLD (2026-08-27 and 2026-09-07).  Every finding in them must be RE-CHECKED AGAINST THE CURRENT TREE before it is recorded as live.  The unread-review hazard is usually stated as &#x27;you miss real defects&#x27;; its mirror is &#x27;you re-raise fixed ones&#x27;, and a phantom row costs a round. ***  *Method for the rest, therefore: read for findings, then CHECK each against HEAD before filing -- exactly as the #507 claim was checked here rather than recorded on two seats&#x27; agreement.*  ALSO CAPTURED from that panel, still to check: kimi&#x27;s #506 `relative_addr&#x27; question, glm&#x27;s four per-round &#x27;single settling fact&#x27; items, and minimax&#x27;s observation on #508 that `return 2&#x27; reports every walked page writable.  *** PROGRESS: 8 of 23 READ.  FIFTEEN REMAIN. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260907_184039/ollama_glm.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_184039/ollama_kimi.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_184039/ollama_minimax.txt; _scratchpad/panel_20260907_184039/ollama_deepseek.txt; src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c:296,:300-304</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="divider"><th colspan="10">dropped &mdash; premise refuted by measurement, no work owed</th></tr><tr class="fixhead st-dropped"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">a1a2</span><span class="ttl">a1a2  Gate 5 constants: the gate checks the PRINTED values, not the USED values</span><span class="tip">a1a2  Gate 5 constants: the gate checks the PRINTED values, not the USED values — commits: a20ebc5 48b36b0 521085b — HOLD: review 9/9; all findings fixed; regression Sunday — Statement of record: gate 5 grades the constants the driver PRINTS, not the ones it USES; and the zero branch is tested before the luna88k branch, so BUDGET=0 prints a green row while removing the only work ceiling. MANUAL - the mutants are predicted, not executed, so build them and watch the rows stay green BEFORE repairing. R3 RESEARCH CLOSURE: the hole is OBSERVED (both mutants green, literal lines banked). THE MAJORITY DESIGN IS REFUTED - shared locals cannot kill an argument-expression mutant, because a local proves what the caller INTENDED to pass, not what the callee RECEIVED. Chosen: record the callee&#x27;s formal parameters and print those; order the calibrated-rig case first and make it RED; factor the classifier into lib.sh so the branch is reachable offline; and ship a STRADDLE detector that reads the printed budget from the driver&#x27;s own output and proves N-1 does not stop while N+1 does. — witness: none -- claim REFUTED by reading; nothing to witness. — note: *** REFUTED 2026-08-27 and DROPPED. The claim is FALSE, and the code already contains the reasoning that refutes it. ***
