C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_v6.txt:66:- Ignore bit 4 only when `current_port == 0`. The repair never reads `current_port`. `(0)`, and the header already calls a previous-selector condition clause (e). A power-on store of 16 still restores the exit.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_v6.txt:78:Every probe refusal is odd or a power of two from bit 3 up, so none has low 3 bits equal to 6. Selector 6 is less than 8 and still takes the accept arm. A guest store of 14 is accepted, `current_port` becomes 14, and the next DATA access exits. A store whose low 32 bits are 6 (`0x100000006`) is accepted as port 6 and does not exit. Bits 0..2 never change `idata >= 8` (masking them together is the measured equivalent `EQ_maskhi`), so this is a predicate the compare does not distinguish: clause (e), a disclosure sentence in `(3)`, not one of the 58 rows. The verdict rests on the latch × bit cells, not on this one.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\opus5_f2_v6.md:58:     - The edit is DEPENDS-ON clause (2)'s operand swap, drawn from a field the body reads.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\opus5_f2_v6.md:62:   - **D2 = N21: REFINE.** It is real and outside the literal (0)-(8). It is not a floor breach: it is keyed on `idata == k` or on a value class, which the 2026-09-04 joint-cell ruling puts under clause (e). What is owed is an L4 wording fix:
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_f2_v6.txt:9:The carrier's 2026-09-04 rule, which the brief quotes in shortened form, has three bounds the floor reading drops. The floor is the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the device-state lvalues the repair path reads, and only where that pair is jointly reachable. The bits of the value being written are single-operand marginals, not a floor axis. A joint is owed only when the guarded body's own code distinguishes it; a value the body never tests as a case (`idata == k`, or one bit of a threshold) is clause (e). The generating-set sentence — at each floor cell, every idata bit the body tests, written both clear and set — was added for a body that already masks a bit (`idata & ICLR` at TxICR). It is what makes that bit × state joint L2. It does not turn every threshold-sensitive bit into a tested bit.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\pass_v6\grok_dedicated_f2_v6.txt:11:This arm tests `writeflag == MEM_READ`, `idata >= 8`, and `!warned_port`. Those partitions are {read, write}, {below 8, at or past 8}, and {latch 0, latch nonzero}. There is no `idata & (1ULL << k)` in the arm. The later relational-operator rule gives one compare three regions — below, at, and past the bound — and a window at a constant the body never tests is clause (e). All three regions are already held while the latch is clear: session A accepts 0..7 before any refusal; the first refusal is 8 in P, W, and B; past the bound, the first refusals are 9, `0x100`, `0x100000005`, and all-ones. "Operand swap onto a body-read field" is also the wrong warrant. Replacing the guard's operand with `warned_port` (`warned_port >= 8`) is already red, because the first refusal of 8 happens at latch 0 and would be stored. The mutant that masks bit 4 only while the latch is clear is a qualified mask, not that swap.
