C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:5:**F1 — N1/D1: UPHOLD the gap; OVERTURN the (0) classification; rule FLOOR — from the packet's own clause (e).** (READ/REASONED) The (0) side's examples ("refuse 9 only by sb after PORT=3", "`ld` sets bit 32 except at selector 5") each contain an axis the fix does *not* partition on (width, selector value). Clause (e) as inlined draws the line itself: the body "tests three things — the access direction, the written value… and the latch (!warned_port)"; and it exempts previous-selector/COMMAND/cell conditioning precisely because "the fix makes no partition of them". The fix *does* partition on `warned_port` (`if (!d->warned_port)` — READ from the verbatim C). N1's edit keys on one leg of a partition the fix itself creates, crossed with the compare's own operand. Both legs are body-tested axes; by the packet's internal logic this is floor, not (0). The one premise I cannot settle: the carrier's joint-cell clause text (the 2026-09-04 adjudication) is quoted only second-hand in §8/N1 — settling fact: that clause verbatim. The floor classification here does not need it, because clause (e)'s own partition/non-partition asymmetry suffices. The gap itself needs no artifact: the session list (READ, §5) shows every latch-clear write is 8, 9, 0x100, 0x100000005, or all-ones, so only bits 3, 8, 32 are decisive at latch-clear; 61 − 3 = 58 open (REASONED arithmetic).
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:9:**F3 — N21/D2: UPHOLD as a real, single-axis gap; rule FLOOR; the mooted single re-anchor only moves it — the stop condition is "no refused value held at exactly one anchor".** (REASONED from READ session data) The constant-5 write is conditioned on idata alone — a floor axis; it is *not* conditioned on current_port's value, COMMAND, or cells, so clause (e)'s exemption does not reach it, and (0) (two axes) does not reach it. It defeats "a refusal keeps the previous selector — previous 5 (P, W, Y, B)" — an effect the contract *claims* (READ §5) — via a relative oracle the rows cannot resolve. Auditing anchors per refused value from §5: already two-anchor: 8 (V@7), 16 (V@7), 0x100 (X7@7), 0x100000003 (V@7), 0xff (V@7), all-ones-sd (X4@4), 9 (many). Deficient (exactly one anchor, all @5): 105 single bits of B (all but 2^3, 2^4, 2^8) plus `0x200000000` (P) and `0xffffffff` (P, sw). That is 107 values. Repair: duplicate B's four groups at anchor 7 (+108 refusals, +4 reads) and add `0x200000000`, `0xffffffff` refused at 7 (P's PORT=7 tail or V). Why this closes rather than moves: after the repair a pure-constant write fails one anchor; surviving edits must read current_port — i.e., condition on a state clause (e) explicitly declares beyond-floor. WARNING to the repair author: (a) re-anchoring *only* B's sd group (grok's "e.g.") moves the gap to the new anchor for the other three groups; (b) distinct anchors *per width* (sb@5, sh@6, …) do **not** close it either — a constant-write keyed on width remains single-axis and invisible. Only same-value-two-anchors closes it. Terminates; mirror audit is symmetric (post-repair no value is @7-only).
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:15:**F6 — Q3: what all of us missed.** (1) The `sw`-truncation defect inside the accepted N1 *remedy* (F2) — every seat argued the gap's classification; nobody simulated the repair's rows against the fix for k ≥ 32. (2) The true N21 repair scope: the value-by-value anchor audit (107 deficient values) and the two failed shortcuts (single re-anchor; per-width anchors) in F3. (3) The R-summary anomaly coupling N17 and N20 (F5). (4) N14's escape hatch is closed by this packet itself — (0) is inlined verbatim in §5, so the conditional (0)-vote must resolve here, and it resolves the other way under clause (e)'s partition test.
C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260925_201529\ollama_kimi.txt:17:**F7 — Premises I cannot settle from the packet (named, with the single settling fact).** (a) The carrier joint-cell rule verbatim (quoted second-hand in §8) — settling fact: the 2026-09-04 adjudication text; note my N1 ruling stands on clause (e) as inlined regardless. (b) R's program order (N17, N20) — settling fact: R's session block in `regress/fbctrl_port_probe.py`. (c) The 117-row composition vs gate 16's pin — settling fact: the probe's row list; not load-bearing for this verdict since both repairs add rows and change the pin mechanically. (d) Commit hash a9a2be3d (N6/N18) — uncontroverted here.
