C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\panels\panel_20260924_115517\codex.txt:3874:GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\fbctrlportkill_20260924\fable5_p1.md-8-**Ruling 1 (F1 — keep the DATA-arm `exit(1)`, or quiet-refuse?): KEEP it.** I read both precedents. `#521` (CHANGELOG) leaves an unreachable `default:` "deliberately in place as a tripwire for a later edit" — the direct precedent. Codex's dissent cites `sh4_val_first()` (`dev_sh4.c:1824-1841`), which I read: it *is* an unreachable bounds check that returns 0 silently. But its own comment says it silences **because "an unbounded key must not become an unbounded print"** — i.e. flood control for a helper called in a guest loop. The DATA guard is unreachable and non-looping, so that warrant does not transfer. Codex's stated reason — "a future upstream-check regression would restore process termination" — is *inverted*: that is the tripwire working, converting a re-opened PORT bug into a loud death rather than a silent OOB into `port[bad]`. Opus **measured** this: with `>` accepting 8, the kept guard fires loudly. Keep it, and disclose under clause (e) that it is unobservable on a correct build. (I keep codex's dissent as the second-strongest option; its idiom is real, its warrant backwards.)
